const markdown0 = "# How Narrarium works\n\n> This guide explains the site architecture and where your data lives.\n\n## In a nutshell\n\nNarrarium is a **fully front-end application**. There is no server of ours that stores your books or your keys. Everything runs in the browser and relies on services you already use.\n\nThe \"back-end\" is made of two pieces that you provide:\n\n- **GitHub** = where the book lives. Each book is a GitHub repository made of Markdown files (chapters, paragraphs, characters, locations, secrets, scripts, drafts, and so on). Every change is a real commit on the repository.\n- **Drive (Google Drive or OneDrive)** = where your settings and personal app data live: configuration, keys, Copilot chats, copy history, costs. Which one is used depends on the account you sign in with (Google → Google Drive, Microsoft → OneDrive).\n\nYou **bring your own LLM (AI model)**: in settings you add your integration (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) with your own key. Narrarium does not ship a model — it uses yours.\n\n## The diagram\n\n```\n            ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n            │       Browser (the site)     │\n            │   React, fully front-end     │\n            └───────┬───────────────┬──────┘\n                    │               │\n          book      │               │   settings, chats,\n       (Markdown)   │               │   costs, clipboard\n                    ▼               ▼\n              ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────────────┐\n              │  GitHub  │   │  Google Drive /  │\n              │  (repo)  │   │     OneDrive     │\n              └──────────┘   └──────────────────┘\n\n                    ▲\n                    │  direct AI calls (with your key)\n                    ▼\n              ┌────────────────────────┐\n              │  OpenAI / Azure OpenAI │\n              └────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n## What this means for you\n\n- **Privacy**: your text stays between your browser, your GitHub, your Drive and your AI provider. It does not go through a server of ours.\n- **Control**: the book is a normal GitHub repository. You can clone it, back it up, open it with other tools.\n- **AI cost**: requests go straight to your provider with your key, so the cost is yours. Narrarium can **estimate** it if you enter prices (see the cost guide).\n\n## Why a GitHub token (PAT) is needed\n\nTo read and write the book files, the browser needs permission to GitHub: a **Personal Access Token (PAT)**. You create it on GitHub and paste it into Narrarium (you can have a default token and/or a dedicated token per book). Without a PAT, the site cannot save your chapters.\n";
const markdown1 = "# Come funziona Narrarium\n\n> Questa guida spiega l'architettura del sito e dove vivono i tuoi dati.\n\n## In breve\n\nNarrarium è una **applicazione interamente front-end**. Non c'è un nostro server che conserva i tuoi libri o le tue chiavi. Tutto gira nel browser e si appoggia a servizi che usi già.\n\nIl \"back-end\" è composto da due pezzi che metti tu:\n\n- **GitHub** = dove vive il libro. Ogni libro è un repository GitHub fatto di file Markdown (capitoli, paragrafi, personaggi, luoghi, segreti, script, bozze, ecc.). Tutte le modifiche sono commit veri sul repository.\n- **Drive (Google Drive o OneDrive)** = dove vivono le tue impostazioni e i dati personali dell'app: configurazioni, chiavi, chat del Copilot, storico copie, costi. Quale dei due viene usato dipende dall'account con cui accedi (Google → Google Drive, Microsoft → OneDrive).\n\nL'**LLM (modello AI) lo aggiungi tu**: nelle impostazioni inserisci la tua integrazione (OpenAI o Azure OpenAI) con la tua chiave. Narrarium non fornisce un modello: usa il tuo.\n\n## Lo schema\n\n```\n            ┌──────────────────────────────┐\n            │        Browser (sito)        │\n            │   React, tutto front-end     │\n            └───────┬───────────────┬──────┘\n                    │               │\n          libro     │               │   impostazioni, chat,\n        (Markdown)  │               │   costi, clipboard\n                    ▼               ▼\n              ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────────────┐\n              │  GitHub  │   │  Google Drive /  │\n              │  (repo)  │   │     OneDrive     │\n              └──────────┘   └──────────────────┘\n\n                    ▲\n                    │  richieste AI dirette (con la tua chiave)\n                    ▼\n              ┌────────────────────────┐\n              │  OpenAI / Azure OpenAI │\n              └────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n## Cosa significa per te\n\n- **Privacy**: i tuoi testi restano tra il tuo browser, il tuo GitHub, il tuo Drive e il tuo provider AI. Non passano da un nostro server.\n- **Controllo**: il libro è un repository GitHub normale. Puoi clonarlo, fare backup, aprirlo con altri strumenti.\n- **Costi AI**: le richieste vanno direttamente al tuo provider con la tua chiave, quindi i costi sono i tuoi. Narrarium può **stimarli** se inserisci i prezzi (vedi la guida sui costi).\n\n## Perché serve un token GitHub (PAT)\n\nPer leggere e scrivere i file del libro, il browser ha bisogno di un permesso verso GitHub: un **Personal Access Token (PAT)**. Lo crei tu su GitHub e lo incolli in Narrarium (puoi avere un token di default e/o un token dedicato per ogni libro). Senza PAT, il sito non può salvare i capitoli.\n";
const markdown2 = "# Getting started\n\n> Start locally, then connect only the services you need.\n\n## 1. Open Narrarium\n\nNarrarium opens directly into a durable local workspace. No account or network connection is required.\n\nSettings, the book registry, reader state, bookmarks, custom actions, clipboard, costs, chats, and chat archive segments are written locally first. Closing and reopening the browser does not require a provider login.\n\n## 2. Create a book\n\nThe default storage mode is **This device only**. A local book uses the same file working copy, commits, recovery snapshots, and dirty tracking as a GitHub-backed book, but has no remote target.\n\nYou can later open Book Settings and attach the same working copy to a new private GitHub repository. A PAT is supported without OAuth.\n\n## 3. Configure AI\n\nNarrarium does not include a model. Open **Settings → AI Integrations** and add OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, GitHub Models, or another supported provider. Configuration and credentials are saved in the local account dataset. Then open **Settings → AI Router** to choose which model handles each task, with fallbacks.\n\n## 4. Optional account replicas\n\nOpen **Account & Sync** to independently enable Google Drive, OneDrive, or GitHub. These are replicas of the same local account dataset, not login requirements.\n\n- Connector credentials and enabled/disabled state remain on this device.\n- Logical account data uses a UTC manifest, content hash, and vector clock.\n- A remote error leaves the local copy safe and marks only that replica pending or in error.\n- Divergent replicas require an explicit authoritative-copy decision.\n- Google Drive and OneDrive can stay connected with **long-lived credentials** that renew themselves, so you do not have to sign in again every hour. The cards on **Account & Sync** show which kind each connection has and offer **Switch to long-lived credentials**. This needs the Narrarium token service (a Cloudflare Worker); without it, the usual one-hour (Google) or 24-hour (Microsoft) sign-in applies and nothing else changes. Google keeps them until you revoke access (the app must be published, not in testing: otherwise 7 days); Microsoft keeps them for 90 days, renewed at every use. **Disconnect** forgets them on this device and revokes Google's; Microsoft's can only be revoked from your Microsoft account.\n\nGitHub PAT is the currently functional static-browser connection. The OAuth PKCE flow is implemented, but GitHub's token endpoint does not expose a browser-readable CORS response; see `docs/github-oauth-static-client.md`.\n\n## 5. Start writing\n\n- Open a chapter and add a paragraph.\n- Use a script to plan a scene, generate a draft, then refine the final paragraph.\n- Select text and use the contextual Improve or Synonym action.\n- Use **Sync now** only when you want enabled remote replicas updated immediately.\n";
const markdown3 = "# Primi passi\n\n> Parti in locale e collega soltanto i servizi che ti servono.\n\n## 1. Apri Narrarium\n\nNarrarium si apre direttamente in un workspace locale durevole. Non sono necessari account o connessione di rete.\n\nImpostazioni, registro libri, stato reader, bookmark, azioni personalizzate, clipboard, costi, chat e segmenti dell'archivio chat vengono scritti prima in locale. Chiudere e riaprire il browser non richiede il login a un provider.\n\n## 2. Crea un libro\n\nLa modalità predefinita è **Solo questo dispositivo**. Un libro locale usa la stessa working copy di file, commit, snapshot di recovery e dirty tracking di un libro GitHub, ma non ha un target remoto.\n\nIn seguito puoi aprire le Impostazioni libro e collegare la stessa working copy a una nuova repository GitHub privata. Il PAT è supportato senza OAuth.\n\n## 3. Configura l'AI\n\nNarrarium non include un modello. Apri **Impostazioni → Integrazioni AI** e aggiungi OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, GitHub Models o un altro provider supportato. Configurazione e credenziali vengono salvate nel dataset account locale. Poi apri **Impostazioni → AI Router** per scegliere quale modello gestisce ogni task, con i fallback.\n\n## 4. Repliche account opzionali\n\nApri **Account e sincronizzazione** per abilitare indipendentemente Google Drive, OneDrive o GitHub. Sono repliche dello stesso dataset account locale, non requisiti di accesso.\n\n- Le credenziali dei connettori e il loro stato attivo/disattivo restano su questo dispositivo.\n- I dati logici dell'account usano manifest UTC, hash dei contenuti e vector clock.\n- Un errore remoto lascia al sicuro la copia locale e marca soltanto quella replica come in attesa o in errore.\n- Le repliche divergenti richiedono una scelta esplicita della copia autorevole.\n- Google Drive e OneDrive possono restare collegati con **credenziali durature** che si rinnovano da sole, così non devi accedere di nuovo ogni ora. Le schede di **Account e sincronizzazione** mostrano che tipo di credenziali ha ogni connessione e propongono **Passa a credenziali durature**. Serve il servizio di accesso di Narrarium (un Worker Cloudflare); senza, vale l'accesso di sempre, di un'ora (Google) o di 24 ore (Microsoft), e non cambia altro. Google le mantiene finché non revochi l'accesso (l'app deve essere pubblicata, non in test: altrimenti 7 giorni); Microsoft le mantiene per 90 giorni, rinnovati a ogni uso. **Disconnetti** le dimentica su questo dispositivo e revoca quelle di Google; quelle di Microsoft si possono revocare solo dal tuo account Microsoft.\n\nIl PAT GitHub è il collegamento attualmente funzionante nel browser statico. Il flusso OAuth PKCE è implementato, ma il token endpoint GitHub non espone una risposta CORS leggibile dal browser; vedi `docs/github-oauth-static-client.md`.\n\n## 5. Inizia a scrivere\n\n- Apri un capitolo e aggiungi un paragrafo.\n- Usa uno script per progettare la scena, genera la bozza e poi rifinisci il paragrafo finale.\n- Seleziona testo e usa l'azione contestuale Migliora o Sinonimo.\n- Usa **Sincronizza ora** quando vuoi aggiornare immediatamente le repliche remote abilitate.\n";
const markdown4 = "# Writing flow\n\n> From script to final paragraph, with ghostwriters and editing tools.\n\n## The three stages: Script → Draft → Final\n\nEach paragraph can go through three stages:\n\n1. **Script** — the scene's blueprint. A visual editor with nestable blocks: sections, dialogues (with lines, actions, emotions), secrets, and references to characters, locations, items, factions, events. Curly braces `{ }` are containers, square brackets `[ ]` are elements. You don't write the syntax by hand: you build it with buttons.\n2. **Draft** — the first prose, generated from the script with AI or written by hand.\n3. **Final** — the definitive paragraph, polished from the draft.\n\nThey are all connected: from the script you generate/regenerate the draft; from the draft you generate the final; and you can also build the script starting from the draft or the existing paragraph. The chapter's \"Scripts\" and \"Drafts\" pages show every paragraph with its state.\n\n## Ghostwriters\n\nA **ghostwriter** is a writer with their own style (tone, voice, rhythm, vocabulary, influences, what to avoid, etc.). You create them in the book's Ghostwriter section and they are saved in the repository.\n\n- Every book has a selected **default ghostwriter**, created automatically when missing.\n- The ghostwriter profile is the single prose contract and includes writing style and punctuation rules.\n- You can pick a ghostwriter when you generate script/draft/final; it ends up in the file's metadata.\n- The style can also have a **per-chapter** exception.\n\n## Improve and Synonym\n\nWhile writing prose (paragraph, draft, canon sheet):\n\n- **Improve**: select a part (or nothing for the whole text) → a preview opens with an old/new diff. You can **Regenerate** or **Apply**. It always gets context: book summary, chapter summary (if any), the text, and the style/ghostwriter.\n- **Synonym**: select 1-3 words → it suggests **3 alternatives**. Pick one or ask for **3 more** (excluding the ones already seen). It preserves the spaces around the word.\n\n## The text menu (right-click / selection)\n\nThe menu is available **everywhere on the site**:\n\n- **Desktop**: right-click. On an editable field you get Select all, Copy, Cut, Paste, Special paste (plus Improve/Synonym if it's a prose editor). On non-editable selected text you get Copy and Special paste.\n- **Mobile**: select text normally; a small wand button appears near the selection; tap it to open the menu. (No long-press, so the native selection stays intact.)\n\n**Special paste**: keeps a history of the last 20 copies/cuts made in the site (also saved to Drive), so you can re-paste something you copied earlier.\n\n## Copilot and voice\n\n- The **Copilot** is the assistant: it knows the page context (book/chapter/paragraph) and can create, edit, summarize, search, handle branches, etc. It replies in the app's language.\n- The **ghost** opens **live voice** mode: you talk and it replies by voice. You can also say \"read me chapter 1\" and it reads the text.\n- Text-to-speech uses the voice set in Settings; when reading the book it reads only the body (not the metadata), unless you ask for the metadata.\n";
const markdown5 = "# Flusso di scrittura\n\n> Dallo script al paragrafo finale, con ghostwriter e strumenti di editing.\n\n## I tre livelli: Script → Bozza → Finale\n\nOgni paragrafo può attraversare tre stadi:\n\n1. **Script** — il copione della scena. Un editor visuale a blocchi annidabili: sezioni, dialoghi (con battute, azioni, emozioni), segreti, e riferimenti a personaggi, luoghi, oggetti, fazioni, eventi. Le graffe `{ }` sono contenitori, le quadre `[ ]` sono elementi. Non devi scrivere la sintassi a mano: la costruisci con i pulsanti.\n2. **Bozza** — la prima prosa, generabile dallo script con l'AI o scritta a mano.\n3. **Finale** — il paragrafo definitivo, rifinito dalla bozza.\n\nSono tutti connessi: dallo script generi/rigeneri la bozza; dalla bozza generi il finale; e puoi creare lo script anche partendo dalla bozza o dal paragrafo già scritto. Le pagine \"Script\" e \"Bozze\" del capitolo mostrano tutti i paragrafi con il loro stato.\n\n## Ghostwriter\n\nUn **ghostwriter** è uno scrittore con uno stile proprio (tono, voce, ritmo, vocabolario, influenze, cosa evitare, ecc.). Li crei nella sezione Ghostwriter del libro e li salvi nel repository.\n\n- Ogni libro ha un **ghostwriter predefinito** selezionato, creato automaticamente quando manca.\n- Il profilo del ghostwriter è l'unico contratto della prosa e include stile di scrittura e regole di punteggiatura.\n- Puoi scegliere un ghostwriter quando generi script/bozza/finale; finisce nei metadati del file.\n- Lo stile può avere anche un'eccezione **per capitolo**.\n\n## Migliora e Sinonimo\n\nMentre scrivi prosa (paragrafo, bozza, scheda canon):\n\n- **Migliora**: seleziona una parte (o niente per tutto il testo) → si apre un'anteprima con il confronto vecchio/nuovo. Puoi **Rigenerare** o **Applicare**. Riceve sempre il contesto: sunto del libro, sunto del capitolo (se presente), il testo, e lo stile/ghostwriter.\n- **Sinonimo**: seleziona 1-3 parole → ti propone **3 alternative**. Scegli quella giusta o chiedi **Altri 3** (esclude quelle già viste). Mantiene gli spazi attorno alla parola.\n\n## Il menu del testo (tasto destro / selezione)\n\nIl menu è disponibile **ovunque nel sito**:\n\n- **Desktop**: clic destro. Su un campo modificabile hai Seleziona tutto, Copia, Taglia, Incolla, Incolla speciale (più Migliora/Sinonimo se è un editor di prosa). Su testo selezionato non modificabile hai Copia e Incolla speciale.\n- **Mobile**: selezioni il testo normalmente; appare un piccolo pulsante a bacchetta vicino alla selezione; lo tocchi e si apre il menu. (Non c'è long-press, così la selezione di sistema resta intatta.)\n\n**Incolla speciale**: tiene lo storico delle ultime 20 copie/tagli fatti nel sito (salvato anche su Drive), così puoi reincollare qualcosa copiato in precedenza.\n\n## Copilot e voce\n\n- Il **Copilot** è l'assistente: conosce il contesto della pagina (libro/capitolo/paragrafo) e può creare, modificare, riassumere, cercare, gestire branch, ecc. Risponde nella lingua dell'app.\n- Il **fantasmino** apre la modalità **voce live**: parli e lui risponde a voce. Puoi anche dirgli \"leggimi il capitolo 1\" e ti legge il testo.\n- La sintesi vocale usa la voce impostata nei Settings; per leggere il libro legge solo il corpo del testo (non i metadati), salvo che tu non chieda i metadati.\n";
const markdown6 = "# Local data, GitHub, Drive, and costs\n\n> Where data lives in the offline-first architecture.\n\n## Local source of truth\n\nNarrarium writes every normal user action to durable browser storage first. The account dataset includes settings, the book registry, reader state, bookmarks, custom actions, AI routing and credentials, clipboard, costs, chats, and lossless chat segments.\n\nRemote failures never roll back local changes. The global status distinguishes **Saved locally**, **Sync pending**, **needs login**, and confirmed remote replicas.\n\n## Books\n\nA book can be **local-only** or connected to GitHub. Local-only is the default and uses the same file working copy, local commits, dirty tracking, locks, diagnostics, and recovery snapshots without inventing a remote owner or repository.\n\nA local book can later be attached to a new private GitHub repository. The existing local repository identity, files, pending history, and recovery records are retained. GitHub repository operations receive a resolved token and do not distinguish OAuth from PAT internally.\n\n## Account replicas\n\nGoogle Drive, OneDrive, and the private GitHub repository `narrarium.settings` are independent replicas of one logical account dataset. Any combination can be enabled on one browser.\n\nThe enabled providers, connector OAuth tokens, provider identities, retry state, folder IDs, and last connector errors are device-local and are never copied to another client.\n\nEach remote copy has a manifest with:\n\n- schema version;\n- snapshot UUID;\n- UTC ISO-8601 modification time;\n- local device ID;\n- vector clock;\n- deterministic content hash.\n\nVector clocks classify replicas as same, ahead, behind, or diverged. Diverged copies are never overwritten automatically. Choosing an authoritative copy creates a recovery snapshot and a new reconciliation version that dominates every observed vector clock.\n\n## GitHub account data\n\nAccount sync through GitHub uses exactly `narrarium.settings`. Missing repositories are created private. A public repository is rejected before account data is uploaded. Writes use one aggregate Git tree commit such as `Sync Narrarium account data`, not one commit per keystroke.\n\nPAT is supported directly. GitHub OAuth with PKCE is prepared but disabled because a real Chromium verification confirmed that GitHub's token endpoint does not expose a browser-readable CORS response. No opaque `no-cors` workaround or backend was introduced. See `github-oauth-static-client.md`.\n\n## Drive\n\nGoogle Drive and OneDrive remain supported as optional account replicas and export destinations. Existing legacy settings, costs, clipboard, and chat files can be imported into the common local dataset. New account snapshots use conditional provider revisions where available so concurrent remote changes are not silently overwritten.\n\nDisconnecting a provider leaves local and remote data intact. Remote deletion is a separate, explicitly confirmed action.\n\n### Long-lived credentials\n\nBy default a Google or OneDrive connection holds an access token that lasts about an hour (Google) or up to 24 hours (Microsoft), and then the replica asks you to reconnect. With the Narrarium token service (the Cloudflare Worker that also proxies GitHub Models), Account & Sync can connect with **long-lived credentials** instead. They renew themselves quietly, with no window, so syncing keeps going; the Google Drive and OneDrive cards say which kind a connection has and offer **Switch to long-lived credentials** when the service is available. Without the service nothing changes.\n\n- **Google:** no expiry until you revoke the access (the OAuth consent screen must be **In production**; in *Testing* they expire after 7 days). Google issues them only when you consent, so if a reconnect brings none, the card says so and tells you how to get them.\n- **Microsoft:** 90 days, renewed at every use.\n- They are kept on this device only, and only if **Remember connection on this device** is on: sealed with a key that cannot be exported, never in the synchronized account data, in exports or in logs. This guards the stored copy; it does not guard against a script running in the page.\n- **Disconnect** forgets them on this device and revokes Google's. Microsoft offers no revocation to the service: revoke from the app-permissions page of your Microsoft account.\n- A refused renewal moves the replica to **Login needed** and the header indicator asks you to reconnect. A broken connection or a busy service is only an error and is retried later.\n\n## Costs and exports\n\nCost counters are stored locally immediately and participate in account versioning. DOCX, PDF, EPUB, submission packages, and exported chat files remain generated artifacts that can be downloaded or uploaded to a selected Drive folder.\n\n## Browser repository limits\n\n- text or Markdown file: **2 MiB**;\n- binary asset: **25 MiB**;\n- one repository mutation: **50 MiB**;\n- one clone, pull, or repair transfer: **250 MiB**, further constrained by available browser quota.\n";
const markdown7 = "# Dati locali, GitHub, Drive e costi\n\n> Dove vivono i dati nell'architettura offline-first.\n\n## Fonte locale autorevole\n\nNarrarium scrive prima ogni normale azione utente nello storage durevole del browser. Il dataset account comprende impostazioni, registro libri, stato reader, bookmark, azioni personalizzate, routing e credenziali AI, clipboard, costi, chat e segmenti lossless delle chat.\n\nGli errori remoti non annullano mai le modifiche locali. Lo stato globale distingue **Salvato localmente**, **Sync in attesa**, **richiede login** e repliche remote confermate.\n\n## Libri\n\nUn libro può essere **solo locale** o collegato a GitHub. La modalità solo locale è predefinita e usa la stessa working copy di file, commit locali, dirty tracking, lock, diagnostica e snapshot di recovery senza inventare owner o repository remoti.\n\nUn libro locale può essere collegato in seguito a una nuova repository GitHub privata. Identità repository locale, file, cronologia in attesa e recovery vengono conservati. Le operazioni repository GitHub ricevono un token risolto e non distinguono internamente OAuth da PAT.\n\n## Repliche account\n\nGoogle Drive, OneDrive e la repository GitHub privata `narrarium.settings` sono repliche indipendenti dello stesso dataset account logico. Sullo stesso browser può essere abilitata qualsiasi combinazione.\n\nProvider attivi, token OAuth dei connettori, identità provider, stato retry, ID cartelle ed errori locali dei connettori appartengono esclusivamente al dispositivo e non vengono copiati su altri client.\n\nOgni copia remota possiede un manifest con:\n\n- versione schema;\n- UUID snapshot;\n- ora di modifica UTC ISO-8601;\n- device ID locale;\n- vector clock;\n- hash deterministico dei contenuti.\n\nI vector clock classificano le repliche come uguali, avanti, indietro o divergenti. Le copie divergenti non vengono mai sovrascritte automaticamente. La scelta della copia autorevole crea un recovery e una nuova versione di riconciliazione che domina tutti i vector clock osservati.\n\n## Dati account GitHub\n\nIl sync account GitHub usa esattamente `narrarium.settings`. Se manca, viene creata privata. Una repository pubblica viene rifiutata prima del caricamento. Le scritture usano un unico commit Git aggregato come `Sync Narrarium account data`, non un commit per carattere.\n\nIl PAT è supportato direttamente. OAuth GitHub con PKCE è predisposto ma disabilitato perché una verifica reale in Chromium ha confermato che il token endpoint GitHub non espone una risposta CORS leggibile dal browser. Non sono stati introdotti workaround `no-cors` opachi né backend. Vedi `github-oauth-static-client.md`.\n\n## Drive\n\nGoogle Drive e OneDrive restano disponibili come repliche account e destinazioni di esportazione opzionali. I vecchi file di impostazioni, costi, clipboard e chat possono essere importati nel dataset locale comune. I nuovi snapshot account usano revisioni condizionali del provider quando disponibili per non sovrascrivere modifiche concorrenti.\n\nLa disconnessione lascia intatti dati locali e remoti. La cancellazione remota è un'azione separata e confermata esplicitamente.\n\n### Credenziali durature\n\nDi norma una connessione Google o OneDrive ha un access token che dura circa un'ora (Google) o fino a 24 ore (Microsoft), poi la replica ti chiede di riconnetterti. Con il servizio di accesso di Narrarium (il Worker Cloudflare che fa anche da proxy per GitHub Models), Account e sincronizzazione può invece collegarsi con **credenziali durature**. Si rinnovano da sole, in silenzio e senza finestre, quindi la sincronizzazione continua; le schede Google Drive e OneDrive dicono che tipo di credenziali ha la connessione e propongono **Passa a credenziali durature** quando il servizio è disponibile. Senza il servizio non cambia nulla.\n\n- **Google:** nessuna scadenza finché non revochi l'accesso (la schermata di consenso OAuth deve essere **In produzione**; in *Testing* scadono dopo 7 giorni). Google le rilascia solo quando dai il consenso: se una riconnessione non le porta, la scheda lo dice e spiega come ottenerle.\n- **Microsoft:** 90 giorni, rinnovati a ogni uso.\n- Restano solo su questo dispositivo, e solo se è attivo **Ricorda la connessione su questo dispositivo**: sigillate con una chiave che non si può esportare, mai nei dati account sincronizzati, nelle esportazioni o nei log. Proteggono la copia conservata; non proteggono da uno script che gira nella pagina.\n- **Disconnetti** le dimentica su questo dispositivo e revoca quelle di Google. Microsoft non offre al servizio una revoca: revoca dalla pagina delle autorizzazioni delle app del tuo account Microsoft.\n- Un rinnovo rifiutato porta la replica su **Accesso necessario** e l'indicatore nell'header ti invita a riconnetterti. Una connessione interrotta o un servizio occupato sono solo un errore e si riprova più tardi.\n\n## Costi ed esportazioni\n\nI contatori dei costi vengono salvati subito in locale e partecipano alla versione account. DOCX, PDF, EPUB, pacchetti di submission e chat esportate restano artefatti generati, scaricabili o caricabili in una cartella Drive scelta.\n\n## Limiti repository nel browser\n\n- file di testo o Markdown: **2 MiB**;\n- asset binario: **25 MiB**;\n- singola mutazione repository: **50 MiB**;\n- singolo trasferimento clone, pull o repair: **250 MiB**, ulteriormente limitato dalla quota browser disponibile.\n";
const markdown8 = "# Narrarium Framework\n\nNarrarium Framework is a local-first writing stack built around three pieces:\n\n- a strict but extensible repository convention where the repository *is* the book\n- a local MCP server that teaches OpenCode or Claude how to create, search, validate, and enrich that repository\n- an Astro-based reader that turns the repository into a browsable site and EPUB pipeline\n\n## Packages\n\n- `packages/core`: schemas, templates, repo scaffolding, search, validation, and EPUB export helpers\n- `packages/sdk-typescript`: dedicated TypeScript SDK package for remote GitHub and Azure DevOps book access\n- `packages/mcp-server`: local stdio MCP server for OpenCode, Claude Desktop, and compatible clients\n- `packages/create-narrarium-book`: starter CLI to scaffold a new book repository from the terminal\n- `packages/astro-reader`: Astro reader with spoiler-safe public mode, full canon opt-in, EPUB export, and doctor tooling\n- `packages/engine`: shared autopilot, director, simulated readers, and personal-server sync, used identically by the web app and the personal server\n- `packages/server`: personal server started with `npx narrarium-server`, which runs the autopilot and the director with the browser closed and syncs with the web app\n\n## Local MCP workflow\n\nThe intended workflow is local-first:\n\n1. OpenCode runs with the local Narrarium MCP server enabled.\n2. You ask for book tasks in natural language.\n3. OpenCode calls Narrarium MCP tools to scaffold the repository, create entities, create chapters, search canon, validate metadata, and optionally fetch Wikipedia research.\n4. The same repository can then be rendered as a website or exported to EPUB.\n\nThis keeps the actual writing artifacts in your filesystem instead of hiding them inside a hosted app.\n\n## Quick start from npm\n\nIf you just want to use Narrarium, start from the published packages:\n\nThe reader toolchain requires Node.js 22.12 or newer.\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en\ncd my-book\nnpm run dev\nnpm run doctor\n```\n\nThis scaffolds a book repo, creates `reader/` by default, installs the reader dependencies, prepares OpenCode config, and gives you a live reading site plus EPUB export while you write.\nThe generated root scripts include `npm run dev`, `npm run build`, `npm run export:epub`, and `npm run doctor`.\n\nIf you want sample content from the start:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --sample\n```\n\nIf you want a book repo without the reader scaffold:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --no-reader\n```\n\nThe starter also has an interactive mode:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book\n```\n\nTo refresh the managed Narrarium scaffolding inside an existing repo:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book --upgrade .\n```\n\nThe upgrade also migrates legacy story prose links like `[Mariamne](../../characters/mariamne-ii/)` into plain-text canon mentions so the reader can link visible names itself.\n\nAdd `--with-reader` if you also want to refresh the generated reader scaffold and root convenience files.\n\n## Run the MCP server from npm\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-mcp-server\n```\n\nIf the package is already installed in the project or globally, the binary is:\n\n```bash\nnarrarium-mcp\n```\n\n## Scaffold a standalone reader from npm\n\nCreate a standalone Astro reader app inside or beside a book repo:\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-astro-reader reader --book-root .. --package-name my-book-reader\ncd reader\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\n\nThe reader includes dedicated indexes for chapters, characters, locations, factions, items, secrets, and timeline events. `npm run dev` watches the linked book repo, refreshes the EPUB, and triggers a full browser reload when canon changes.\n\nBy default the generated reader runs in a spoiler-safe public mode:\n\n- secret pages stay out of the public atlas and nav\n- direct canon pages fall back to teaser or locked views when `known_from` or `reveal_in` say the lore is not safe yet\n- search, popups, and canon backlinks respect the same thresholds\n- chapter and scene prose should keep canon names as plain text; the reader upgrades visible mentions into spoiler-safe popups and links automatically, including legacy internal canon links during runtime\n\nFor author-only or spoiler-friendly deployments, set one of these before building or running the reader:\n\n```bash\nNARRARIUM_READER_CANON_MODE=full\n# or\nNARRARIUM_READER_ALLOW_FULL_CANON=true\n```\n\nIf you want EPUBCheck validation during export or build, also set one of these:\n\n```bash\nEPUBCHECK_CMD=epubcheck\n# or\nEPUBCHECK_JAR=/absolute/path/to/epubcheck.jar\n```\n\n## Develop this monorepo\n\nThese commands are for working on the Narrarium framework repository itself:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm run build\n```\n\nRun the automated test suite with:\n\n```bash\nnpm run test\n```\n\nFor a publish-ready workspace check:\n\n```bash\nnpm run release:check\n```\n\nBuild the GitHub Pages documentation site locally with:\n\n```bash\nnpm run docs:build\n```\n\nThe production Narrarium site lives in `src/narrarium-site` and is now a Vite/React static app. It includes the public landing page, docs, MCP reference, legal pages, and the authenticated `/app` workspace. The docs are generated from repository Markdown at build time by `src/narrarium-site/scripts/generate-docs.mjs`.\n\nTo deploy it on GitHub Pages:\n\n- enable **Settings -> Pages -> Source: GitHub Actions** in the repository\n- keep `src/narrarium-site/public/CNAME` set to the custom domain, currently `narrarium.net`\n- the Google and Microsoft public OAuth client IDs are built into the app; no GitHub Actions secret is required for them\n- optionally override those IDs at build time with `VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` or `VITE_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID` if you create a different OAuth app registration\n- configure the Google OAuth app with `https://narrarium.net` as an authorized JavaScript origin\n- configure the Entra ID app registration with `https://narrarium.net/` as a SPA redirect URI\n- keep `SITE_BASE=/` in `.github/workflows/book-management-system.yml` for the custom domain deployment\n\nThe workflow `Deploy Narrarium Site` builds `src/narrarium-site/dist` and uploads it with the official GitHub Pages artifact action. The build also writes `404.html` from `index.html`, so React Router paths such as `/docs/...` and `/app/books` survive direct refreshes on GitHub Pages.\n\nRepo-only helper commands:\n\n```bash\nnpm run create:book -- my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --sample\nnpm run dev:mcp\nnpm run reader:init -- reader --book-root .. --package-name my-book-reader\nnpm run sync:script-ledger -- /path/to/book\n```\n\n`sync:script-ledger` deterministically regenerates `state/script-ledger.md` from `scripts/**/*.md`; omit the path to use the current working directory.\n\nIf you want the public HTTP version locally, for Vercel-style setup and research flows:\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev:http -w narrarium-mcp-server\n```\n\nThis serves:\n\n- `http://localhost:3000/mcp`\n- `http://localhost:3000/health`\n\n## OpenCode config\n\nAn example project config lives in `opencode.jsonc` and points OpenCode to the local MCP server build output.\nIt also tunes the default `build` and `plan` agents for book work with higher reasoning effort, detailed summaries, and more verbose responses while keeping temperature moderate for canon consistency.\nGenerated book repos also point `instructions` to `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, so OpenCode and Copilot share the same repository-specific writing workflow.\nGenerated book repos also include `context.md` for stable historical, geographic, social, and world-context notes that should stay visible while writing chapters and scenes.\nGenerated book repos also include `ideas.md`, `story-design.md`, `notes.md`, and `promoted.md` so unstable ideas, reviewed notes, structural design, and promoted archive items stay separate. Chapter-local variants live under `drafts/<chapter>/` and are created when draft work starts.\nGenerated book repos select `ghostwriters/default.md` from `book.md`. A ghostwriter profile is the single prose contract, including its frontmatter writing and punctuation fields plus its markdown body; chapters, paragraphs, drafts, and scripts can select another profile with `ghostwriter` frontmatter.\nThe Astro reader now exposes an author-facing `Workshop` area in full canon mode so you can inspect drafts, ideas, notes, story design, and promoted items alongside the published canon pages.\nBook repos also include `conversations/` as a portable place to keep exported writing chats.\nThe generated `.opencode/plugins/conversation-export.js` plugin updates `conversations/RESUME.md`, `conversations/CONTINUATION.md`, and per-session exports automatically when OpenCode sessions go idle.\nThe generated `/resume-book` command and MCP tool `resume_book_context` help you restart from repo state on a fresh machine or session, including `context.md`, `plot.md`, `resumes/`, and `state/` snapshots when present. `/resume-book` also supports scoped targets such as a chapter or paragraph, for example `/resume-book chapter:002-ledger-suspicion 002-tense-exchange`.\nFor dialogue-heavy revision, the MCP layer also supports `review_dialogue_action_beats` for beat-by-beat proposals and `apply_dialogue_action_beats` for applying only the confirmed beat selections to a final paragraph.\n\n## Agent rules\n\nProject-level OpenCode and agent rules live in `AGENTS.md`.\n\n## Main MCP tools\n\nThese are the main building blocks exposed by the local MCP server:\n\n- `init_book_repo`: scaffold a book repository in a target folder\n- `setup_framework`: return the exact `npx` commands to bootstrap a new Narrarium project\n- `repository_spec`: return the repo model and canon rules\n- `character_wizard`: return the checklist of fields needed for a full character\n- `create_character`: create a rich character file with voice, role, backstory, and function in book\n- `location_wizard`, `faction_wizard`, `item_wizard`, `secret_wizard`: return the checklist for each canon type\n- `timeline_event_wizard`, `chapter_wizard`, `paragraph_wizard`: return the checklist for those creation flows\n- `chapter_writing_context`, `paragraph_writing_context`, `resume_book_context`: assemble the context to resume or write book prose safely\n- `create_location`, `create_faction`, `create_item`, `create_secret`, `create_timeline_event`: create rich canonical files for those types\n- `start_wizard`, `wizard_answer`, `wizard_status`, `wizard_finalize`, `wizard_cancel`: run a true multi-step guided creation session\n- `create_entity`: create faster stubs for other canon files\n- `update_chapter`, `update_paragraph`: update existing chapter and scene files without structural migration\n- `update_entity`: patch frontmatter and body on existing canon files\n- `create_asset_prompt`, `register_asset`, `generate_asset_image`: manage canonical art prompts and image files\n- `rename_entity`, `rename_chapter`, `rename_paragraph`: rename canon safely and move matching asset folders too\n- `search_book`: search the repository before inventing canon\n- `query_canon`: answer natural-language canon questions using state, resumes, chapters, and fallback search, including locations, knowledge, inventory, relationships, conditions, open loops, secret holders, first appearances, and chapter-range evolution queries\n- `revise_paragraph`: propose a targeted editorial pass for a final scene without writing files, and suggest `state_changes` review when continuity-sensitive beats are involved\n- `revise_chapter`: propose a chapter-level editorial pass with diagnosis and scene-by-scene suggestions, again without writing files automatically\n- `list_related_canon`: find files that reference an id or concept\n- `sync_resume`: refresh chapter or total summaries from current files\n- `sync_all_resumes`: refresh all chapter resumes plus the total summary in one pass\n- `evaluate_chapter`: refresh a full chapter evaluation using all paragraph text, objective scores, editorial reading against the selected ghostwriter profiles, canon coherence checks, a weighted verdict, a dedicated explanation of why that verdict landed there, and revision next steps\n- `evaluate_paragraph`: refresh one paragraph evaluation while still reading the whole chapter as context, with objective score, editorial score, canon coherence, a weighted verdict, and a dedicated explanation of why that verdict landed there\n- `evaluate_book`: refresh the full-book evaluation and optionally all chapter and paragraph evaluations with objective scores, editorial checks, canon coherence checks, a weighted verdict, and a dedicated explanation of why that verdict landed there\n- `wikipedia_search` and `wikipedia_page`: research factual or historical material\n- `export_epub`: turn the repository into an EPUB\n\nFinal chapter and paragraph mutations through the MCP layer auto-refresh `plot.md`, the per-chapter resumes, and `resumes/total.md`. Structured story state stays manual on purpose: rewrites mark `state/status.md` as dirty, then you decide when to run `sync_story_state`. Evaluations stay manual so critique remains explicit.\n\nFor factual or historical work, Narrarium now checks existing `research/wikipedia/` snapshots first and reuses them before fetching Wikipedia again when it can. If you need a refresh, use `forceWikipediaRefresh` or limit reuse with `maxWikipediaSnapshotAgeDays`.\n\n## Story state workflow\n\nNarrarium now separates two continuity layers:\n\n- `resumes/` stays human-readable and narrative-first\n- `state/` stores structured continuity snapshots for agents, checks, and continuity review\n\nThe intended workflow is:\n\n1. Write or revise chapter and paragraph prose.\n2. Let Narrarium auto-refresh `plot.md` and the resume files.\n3. Record chapter-specific structured deltas in `resumes/chapters/<slug>.md` under `state_changes` frontmatter.\n4. Run `sync_story_state` manually when you want refreshed continuity snapshots.\n\nThat produces:\n\n- `state/status.md`: whether story state is stale, when it was last mutated, and which files changed\n- `state/current.md`: the latest consolidated continuity snapshot\n- `state/chapters/*.md`: per-chapter structured snapshots after applying each chapter delta in order\n\nRecommended `state_changes` keys in chapter resumes:\n\n- `locations`\n- `knowledge_gain` and `knowledge_loss`\n- `inventory_add` and `inventory_remove`\n- `relationship_updates`\n- `conditions`\n- `wounds`\n- `open_loops_add` and `open_loops_resolved`\n\n`doctorBook()` and `npm run doctor` now warn if `state/` is missing or stale, and writing-context tools read `state/current.md` plus `state/status.md` when available.\n\nFor continuity questions and range-based canon queries, see `docs/query-canon.md`.\n\nFor proposal-only editorial revision passes on final scene files, see `docs/revise-paragraph.md`.\n\nFor chapter-level proposal-only editorial passes, see `docs/revise-chapter.md`.\n\nFor explicit per-chapter style overrides with book-level fallback, see `docs/style-profiles.md`.\n\n## Practical image examples\n\nWith the local MCP server and `OPENAI_API_KEY` configured, a typical image workflow looks like this.\n\n| Use case | Subject | Asset path | Typical tool flow |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Book cover | `book` | `assets/book/cover.*` | `register_asset` or `generate_asset_image` |\n| Character portrait | `character:lyra-vale` | `assets/characters/lyra-vale/primary.*` | `create_asset_prompt` -> `generate_asset_image` |\n| Chapter art | `chapter:001-the-arrival` | `assets/chapters/001-the-arrival/primary.*` | `create_asset_prompt` -> `generate_asset_image` |\n| Scene art | `paragraph:001-the-arrival:001-at-the-gate` | `assets/chapters/001-the-arrival/paragraphs/001-at-the-gate/primary.*` | `generate_asset_image` directly or after `create_asset_prompt` |\n\nCreate a reusable character portrait prompt:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"create_asset_prompt\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"character:lyra-vale\",\n    \"body\": \"# Intent\\n\\nPrimary portrait for Lyra.\\n\\n# Prompt\\n\\nPortrait of Lyra Vale, guarded expression, harbor fog, muted cinematic palette, portrait composition, 2:3 ratio.\\n\\n# Notes\\n\\nKeep facial features consistent across future chapter and scene art.\",\n    \"orientation\": \"portrait\",\n    \"aspectRatio\": \"2:3\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGenerate the actual image into `assets/characters/lyra-vale/primary.png`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"generate_asset_image\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"character:lyra-vale\",\n    \"provider\": \"openai\",\n    \"model\": \"gpt-image-1\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGenerate a scene image directly for a paragraph:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"generate_asset_image\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"paragraph:001-the-arrival:001-at-the-gate\",\n    \"prompt\": \"Lyra arriving at Gray Harbor's gate in cold fog, suspicious guards, cinematic portrait framing, book-cover quality illustration, 2:3 ratio.\",\n    \"provider\": \"openai\",\n    \"model\": \"gpt-image-1\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nImport an image you created elsewhere into the canonical assets tree:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"register_asset\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"book\",\n    \"assetKind\": \"cover\",\n    \"sourceFilePath\": \"C:/renders/my-book-cover.png\",\n    \"body\": \"# Intent\\n\\nMain book cover.\\n\\n# Prompt\\n\\nFinal cover prompt used for the external render.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGenerate chapter art into `assets/chapters/001-the-arrival/primary.png`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"generate_asset_image\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n    \"prompt\": \"Lyra approaching Gray Harbor through cold fog, chapter-opening illustration, portrait orientation, dramatic negative space, consistent with the book's visual language.\",\n    \"provider\": \"openai\",\n    \"model\": \"gpt-image-1\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe recommended place for reusable style rules and prompt templates is `guidelines/images.md`.\n\nWhen you store asset metadata, prefer adding `alt_text` and `caption` in the asset markdown frontmatter so the web reader and EPUB can render accessible descriptions and captions consistently.\n\nThe Astro reader now auto-renders these canonical assets when present for:\n\n- `book` cover on the home page\n- entity detail pages such as characters, locations, factions, items, secrets, and timeline events\n- chapter pages and paragraph or scene sections\n\nIn public reader mode, those canon surfaces still respect `known_from` and `reveal_in` before exposing full details.\n\nIf you later rename canon with `rename_entity`, `rename_chapter`, or `rename_paragraph`, Narrarium also moves the matching asset folders.\n\n## Repository spec\n\nThe current repository convention is documented in `docs/repository-spec.md`.\n\n## Autopilot and director\n\nThe web app and the personal server share one autopilot (`narrarium-engine`): concept, chapter plan, scenes, blind simulated readers, specialists and a **director** that decides as the author's stand-in. The director can also be asked at any time what it thinks of the book, a chapter, or a scene. See `docs/autopilot-spec.md`, `packages/engine/README.md`, and `packages/server/README.md`.\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-server\n```\n\nThe server keeps its API and its admin panel on `127.0.0.1` (ports 24242 and 24243). Narrarium pairs with it from **Settings → Personal server** with a code approved in the admin panel, checks its signed identity at every connection, and can update it to the latest verified npm release. For remote access the admin panel manages a dedicated Cloudflare connector; autostart is available on Linux (systemd) and macOS (launchd).\n\n## Publishing\n\nThe final public package set is:\n\n- `narrarium`\n- `narrarium-sdk`\n- `narrarium-mcp-server`\n- `create-narrarium-book`\n- `narrarium-astro-reader`\n- `narrarium-engine`\n- `narrarium-server`\n\nThe public package line started at `0.1.0`; current workspace versions may be newer.\n\nPublishing notes and release order live in `docs/publishing.md`.\n\n## GitHub Actions\n\nTwo workflows are included:\n\n- `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: runs build, validation, and tests on pushes and pull requests\n- `.github/workflows/publish-npm.yml`: auto-publishes bumped package versions on `main`, with manual dispatch and GitHub Release as fallbacks\n- `.github/workflows/server.yml` and `.github/workflows/publish-server-npm.yml`: personal server tests on Linux, Windows and macOS, and its tag-triggered npm release\n- `.github/workflows/deploy-vercel-mcp.yml`: deploys the public HTTP MCP endpoint to Vercel\n- `.github/workflows/deploy-docs-pages.yml`: builds and publishes the documentation site to GitHub Pages\n\nCurrent production domains:\n\n- docs site: `https://narrarium.net`\n- public MCP endpoint: `https://narrarium.space/mcp`\n- public MCP health: `https://narrarium.space/health`\n\nnpm publishing uses npm Trusted Publishing (OIDC from GitHub Actions, no stored npm token, provenance on every version):\n\n1. Publish the first version of a new package once by hand; npm can register a trusted publisher only on an existing package\n2. On npmjs.com register `publish-npm.yml` (environment `production`) as the package's trusted publisher; `narrarium-server` uses `publish-server-npm.yml` (environment `npm-publish`) and `narrarium-server-v<version>` tags\n3. Bump the package versions and merge that change to `main`\n\nOn `main`, the publish workflow compares local package versions against npm and only publishes versions that are not already online. Packages not on npm yet are skipped with a warning.\nWhen it finds unpublished versions, it runs `npm run release:check` first, then publishes in dependency order. Details live in `docs/publishing.md`.\n\nFor the Vercel deploy workflow, add this repository secret:\n\n- `VERCEL_TOKEN`\n\nThe workflow is already pinned to:\n\n- `VERCEL_ORG_ID=team_Jut3umUxSp1D1Z1sqmv4iUjc`\n- `VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=prj_b9bxn0P4ilMmG40DiZoZ057qA1Zj`\n\nFor local or CI image generation with `generate_asset_image`, also set:\n\n- `OPENAI_API_KEY`\n\n## Vercel MCP\n\nThis repository now includes a Vercel-ready public MCP endpoint:\n\n- `api/mcp.ts`\n- `api/health.ts`\n- `vercel.json`\n\nAfter deploying the repo to Vercel, the public endpoints are:\n\n- `/mcp`\n- `/health`\n\nImportant: the Vercel deployment is intended for setup guidance, repository spec guidance, and Wikipedia research. Actual local filesystem writing still belongs to the local stdio MCP server.\n";
const markdown9 = "# Narrarium Agent Rules\n\nUse Narrarium as a book-writing framework where the repository is the source of truth.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Search canon before inventing new facts.\n2. Prefer Narrarium MCP tools over ad-hoc filesystem edits when creating or updating book structure.\n3. Use rich creation tools for major canon files:\n   - `create_character`\n   - `create_location`\n   - `create_faction`\n   - `create_item`\n   - `create_secret`\n   - `create_timeline_event`\n4. If the brief is incomplete, start a guided flow with:\n   - `start_wizard`\n   - `wizard_answer`\n   - `wizard_finalize`\n5. After significant structural changes, refresh maintenance files:\n   - `sync_plot`\n   - `sync_resume` or `sync_all_resumes`\n   - `evaluate_chapter` or `evaluate_book`\n\nNarrarium MCP already auto-refreshes `plot.md` and the resume files after final chapter or paragraph mutations. Evaluations remain manual unless explicitly requested.\n\n## Versioning and patch notes\n\n- Every application change that is committed and pushed must bump `src/narrarium-site/package.json` and the root lockfile version according to the repository release workflow.\n- Before the version bump is committed, add a matching entry to `src/narrarium-site/src/content/patch-notes.json`.\n- Every patch-note entry must contain a title, summary, and non-empty change list in both English (`en`) and Italian (`it`).\n- Patch notes must describe user-visible behavior and navigation, not only internal implementation details.\n- Never commit or push a Narrarium site version without a matching bilingual patch note. `npm run bms:build` enforces this rule.\n- After each versioned commit, push `main` and monitor the `Deploy Narrarium Site` GitHub Actions workflow until completion, unless the user explicitly requests otherwise.\n- `narrarium-server` is released only by pushing a `narrarium-server-v<version>` tag after bumping `packages/server/package.json` and `packages/server/src/version.ts` together; the `Publish narrarium-server` workflow publishes it through npm Trusted Publishing with provenance. Apart from the one-time bootstrap publish of `0.1.0` described in `packages/server/README.md`, never publish it by hand or add it back to the generic publish workflow: the server's updater installs only releases attested by that workflow.\n\n## Canon discipline\n\n- Do not duplicate canon if the fact already exists in another markdown file.\n- Keep structured facts in frontmatter and prose in markdown body.\n- In chapter and scene prose, write character, item, location, faction, secret, and timeline-event names as plain text. Do not insert markdown links to canon files or reader routes; the reader will link visible canon mentions.\n- Do not reveal a secret before its `known_from` or `reveal_in` threshold.\n- Treat `guidelines/`, `drafts/`, `plot.md`, `conversations/`, `resumes/`, `evaluations/`, and `secrets/` as first-class context, not optional notes.\n- Treat `director.md` (the director profile: the author's stand-in) and `evaluations/director/` as first-class context when judging or revising prose.\n- `autopilot/state.json`, `autopilot/activity.json`, and `autopilot/plan.md` belong to the autopilot. Read them for context, but do not edit them while an autopilot run is active; change the autopilot through its interventions instead.\n\n## Historical or factual content\n\n- If a request is historical, factual, or based on a real place, use Wikipedia research first.\n- Save research snapshots into `research/wikipedia/` when they inform canon.\n\n## Chapter and scene updates\n\n- Use `create_chapter` and `create_paragraph` for new structure.\n- After creating or updating any file in `scripts/**/*.md`, run `sync_script_ledger` or `npm run sync:script-ledger -- <book-root>` and inspect reported errors or warnings.\n- Use `create_chapter_draft` and `create_paragraph_draft` for rough scaffolding before final prose.\n- Use `chapter_writing_context` and `paragraph_writing_context` before drafting polished prose from rough material.\n- Use `resume_book_context` when restarting work from exported conversation history or a fresh session.\n- Use `update_chapter` and `update_paragraph` for existing files.\n- Use `update_chapter_draft` and `update_paragraph_draft` when refining rough material.\n- Use `create_chapter_from_draft` and `create_paragraph_from_draft` when turning rough drafts into final story files.\n- Avoid renumbering or renaming chapter and paragraph files unless the user explicitly asks for structural migration.\n- Before writing new chapter or paragraph prose, read the selected `ghostwriters/<slug>.md` profile, the relevant prior story context, and any matching draft files. Resolve the selection from paragraph, chapter, then `book.md`.\n- Keep `plot.md` updated whenever chapter progression, timeline anchors, or reveal timing changes.\n\n## Reader and publishing\n\n- Use `npm run create:book` to scaffold a new book repository.\n- Use `npm run reader:init` or `narrarium-reader-init` to scaffold a standalone reader app.\n- Keep package naming aligned with the public package set:\n  - `narrarium`\n  - `narrarium-sdk`\n  - `narrarium-mcp-server`\n  - `create-narrarium-book`\n  - `narrarium-astro-reader`\n  - `narrarium-engine`\n  - `narrarium-server`\n- Autopilot, director, simulated readers, and personal-server sync live once in `packages/engine` (`narrarium-engine`). The web app and `narrarium-server` only provide adapters (book store, AI port); never duplicate that logic in either host.\n";
const markdown10 = "# Local workspace, connector credentials, and session persistence\n\nNarrarium is a static, offline-first client. A local workspace exists independently from remote identities and connector credentials.\n\n## Local lifecycle\n\nOpening Narrarium never requires login and never validates a provider token. The same workspace remains usable after browser restarts, network loss, token expiry, or disconnecting every provider.\n\nProvider validation is lazy and occurs only for an explicit or scheduled remote operation such as sync, push, pull, remote creation, export, or remote deletion. Invalid credentials move only that connector to `needs-auth`; local saves continue.\n\n## Connections\n\nGoogle, Microsoft, and GitHub connection records are device-local. Each may be enabled independently as an account replica. Enabling or disabling a connector does not change the logical account vector clock.\n\nWithout **Remember this connection**, bearer material stays in memory or provider session storage. With remember enabled, the minimum connector material needed for a later remote attempt is retained locally. The application still does not call the provider during ordinary local startup.\n\nLong-lived credentials (see below) are the exception for Google Drive and OneDrive: with the token broker, the connection renews its access token by itself, and only a refused refresh token turns into `needs-auth`.\n\nPAT credentials have no synthetic login expiry. They are attempted only when needed and become `needs-auth` after GitHub rejects them.\n\n## Long-lived credentials for Google Drive and OneDrive\n\nA connection made the usual way only holds an access token. It lasts about an hour for Google and up to 24 hours for Microsoft (the limit of the single-page-app platform that MSAL uses). After that the replica reports **Login needed** and asks to reconnect.\n\nWhen the token broker is configured, **Account & Sync** connects with **long-lived credentials** instead, and renews them by itself, without a window and without asking. The Google Drive and OneDrive cards say which kind of credentials the connection has, and offer **Switch to long-lived credentials** for a connection that only has session ones.\n\n- **The broker** is the Cloudflare Worker that also proxies GitHub Models (`infra/models-github-proxy`). It keeps the Google and Microsoft client secrets, swaps authorization codes and renews tokens, and stores nothing. The site looks for it at the origin of `VITE_GITHUB_MODELS_BASE`, or at `VITE_AUTH_BROKER_BASE` when that is set, and asks `GET /oauth/status` once per session. If there is no broker, or it is not configured for the provider, nothing changes and nothing is offered: Google keeps the implicit flow, Microsoft keeps MSAL. The owner's one-time steps (Google and Azure consoles, repository secrets) are in the Worker's README.\n- **Google** uses the authorization code flow in a popup. Google issues a refresh token only when the user consents, so connecting again without a new consent keeps the one already stored for the same account; if Google issues none, the connection stays a session one and says so. A refresh token does not expire until the user revokes access, provided the OAuth consent screen is **In production** (in *Testing* it expires after 7 days); Google also expires one that was not used for 6 months.\n- **Microsoft** uses the authorization code with PKCE in a popup that returns to `auth/microsoft-callback.html`. The refresh token lasts 90 days and is renewed at every use: Microsoft rotates it, and the site always keeps the newest one. The account identity (`homeAccountId`, `localAccountId`) is built from the `client_info` of the token response exactly as MSAL builds it, so a connection that is switched from MSAL keeps being the same account.\n- **One renewal at a time.** The Web Lock `narrarium-token-refresh:<provider>` serializes renewals across tabs (without Web Locks, within the tab), and the connection is read again once the lock is held, because another tab may have renewed meanwhile.\n- **What a failure means.** A refresh token that the provider refuses (`invalid_grant`) moves the replica to `needs-auth`, so the header indicator asks to reconnect. A broker that is unreachable, slow, rate-limiting or failing is a passing problem: the replica reports an error (or offline), nothing is deleted, and the next attempt tries again. A broker that is no longer configured also asks to reconnect, but keeps the token in case it comes back.\n- **Ahead of time.** After start-up and whenever the tab becomes visible again, a connection whose access token has expired or is about to is renewed in the background and, if its replica had failed or was waiting for the login, the sync is retried. Never with a window; one attempt at a time per service, with a growing pause after each failure (30 seconds up to 15 minutes, or what the broker asked).\n\n### Where the refresh token lives\n\nThe refresh token is the long-lived secret. It exists in memory and, only when **Remember connection on this device** is on, sealed in a database of its own (`narrarium-credential-vault`, apart from the account data): AES-GCM under a key that WebCrypto made once for this device and cannot export, bound to the provider and the account, with a fresh IV each time. Without WebCrypto it is kept in memory only, for the life of the tab. It is never written to the device configuration record, never part of the synchronized account data, an export, a log, an error report or the LLM debug history.\n\nThis protects the stored records, for instance a copy of the database or a casual look in the developer tools. It does **not** protect against code that runs in the page: a script injected into the page can ask WebCrypto to decrypt exactly as the application does, which is why PATs and tokens must still be treated as the secrets they are.\n\n### Disconnecting and deleting\n\n**Disconnect**, **Disconnect all accounts** and **Delete all local data** forget the refresh tokens and the key that sealed them. A Google refresh token is also revoked, through the broker (best effort: a revocation that fails never blocks the disconnection). Microsoft has no revocation the broker can use, so its token is only forgotten on this device; to revoke the access completely, use the app-permissions page of the Microsoft account. Google treats the authorization the user gave the application as one grant, so revoking it can also stop the long-lived credentials of other devices, which then ask to reconnect.\n\n## Logout and deletion\n\nDisconnecting or logging out removes connector credentials and leaves the local workspace and remote data intact. Deleting remote Narrarium data is a separate provider-specific operation. Deleting the local workspace is a separate destructive action guarded by a Data Safety Report and exact `DELETE` confirmation.\n\n## Security boundaries\n\nJavaScript-readable browser storage is exposed to a successful same-origin script injection. Private Drive storage and a private GitHub repository are not dedicated secret vaults. PATs and configured AI API credentials remain syncable for product continuity, but OAuth access tokens, refresh tokens, provider session internals, connector enabled state, and the GitHub OAuth client secret are never included in the account dataset.\n\nThe GitHub OAuth client secret requested for the static build is triple-Base64 obfuscated only. This is explicitly not encryption. Browser token exchange is currently disabled after real Chromium testing confirmed GitHub's missing CORS grant; see `github-oauth-static-client.md`.\n";
const markdown11 = "# Autopilot and Director Spec\n\nNarrarium can write a book on autopilot while the author keeps the final word. The same engine ([`narrarium-engine`](../packages/engine/README.md)) runs in the web app and in the personal server ([`narrarium-server`](../packages/server/README.md)), so the book files, the decisions and the live events are identical wherever it runs.\n\n## Roles\n\n- **Architect and ghostwriter** — design the concept and the plan, then write scene by scene following the selected ghostwriter profile.\n- **Simulated readers** — read each finished chapter *blind*: no canon, no secrets, no plan. They write the same reader evaluations as the Reader evaluations page, then a panel summary.\n- **Specialists** — check continuity, canon, secrets and reveal timing, timeline, character voice, style and pacing. Every finding must quote the text.\n- **Director** — the author's stand-in. It approves or corrects the plan, decides on every finding (accept with a precise fix, or reject with a reason), scores the chapter and carries standing guidance forward. The software, not the model, turns accepted findings into repairs. When its autonomy allows it, the director asks the author instead of deciding a critical matter.\n\n## Pipeline\n\n1. Concept: story design, world context, characters, locations, factions, secrets with their reveal chapter.\n2. Plan: chapters; simulated readers react to the pitch and the plan; the director approves or asks for changes. By default the autopilot pauses here so the author can read the plan.\n3. For every chapter: scene plan → prose → readers → specialists → director → repairs → verification round (up to the director's limit) → consolidation (chapter summary, recap, story so far, `plot.md`).\n4. Final: the director's judgement on the whole book.\n\nA book that already has chapters is continued after the last one; chapters written by the author are never rewritten unless the author asks.\n\n## Author interventions\n\n| Scope | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Next step only | Delivered once to the next writing step, then marked as applied. |\n| From now on | A permanent rule for every following step, the director included. |\n| Revise what is already written | The director picks the scenes that must change; they are rewritten and reviewed again. |\n\nAn intervention can interrupt the running step, which is then repeated with it. The author's instructions always win over the director's taste.\n\n## Asking the director\n\nOn any book, chapter or scene page, **Ask the director** returns a structured opinion: verdict, score, strengths, concerns with quoted evidence, decisions, next steps and, when asked, a direct answer. Opinions can be kept in the book.\n\n## Files\n\n| Path | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `director.md` | Director profile: `type: director`, `name`, `persona`, `autonomy` (`full`, `ask-on-critical`, `ask-always`), `strictness`, `approval_score`, `max_review_rounds`, `priorities`, `taste`, `red_lines`, `reader_weights`, and the vision in the body. |\n| `autopilot/state.json` | Brief, options, plan, current review cycle, author rules and interventions, director guidance, statistics. |\n| `autopilot/activity.json` | The last 200 steps: what, why, next, result, model, tokens and cost. |\n| `autopilot/plan.md` | Human-readable mirror of the plan. |\n| `evaluations/director/` | Director opinions (`type: director-opinion`) for the book, chapters and paragraphs. |\n| `evaluations/readers/` | Simulated reader evaluations and panel summaries. |\n\nChapters, paragraphs, canon entries, resumes and `plot.md` use the normal Narrarium formats and pass `validate_book`/`doctor_book`. Run `sync_plot`, `sync_all_resumes` and `sync_story_state` to regenerate the deterministic maintenance files after an autopilot session.\n\n## Where it runs\n\n- **In the browser**: each step is a local commit in the book's working copy; GitHub books are published as the author chose (every chapter unless chosen otherwise). Only one tab runs a book at a time; other tabs follow it live and can pause it, take a pause back, change the options that may change during a run, or send interventions.\n- **On the personal server** (`npx narrarium-server`): the autopilot keeps working with the browser closed. GitHub books travel through GitHub (the server pushes after every step unless the author chose otherwise); local books are exchanged with a three-way file sync. The browser follows the server live.\n\n### Quick commands\n\nThe web app puts the commands the author reaches for most in one bar (the Autopilot page, the \"This book\" tab of the live panel and the \"Live\" tab for the book in focus). They all go through the engine, once, so the browser and the server behave the same:\n\n- **Pause** `now`, `after-step` or `after-chapter`, and **cancel** a pause that waits (`session.pause`, `session.cancelPause`; `summary.pausePending` says what waits).\n- **Run one step or one chapter**: a start with `limits: { steps: 1 }` or `{ chapters: 1 }`, for that run only (`summary.limits`).\n- **Publish** never, every chapter or every step: `options.publishEvery`, which `session.updateOptions` changes during a run (`summary.publish` says what is in force). A browser publishes from both hooks, `afterCommit` (every step) and `afterChapter`.\n- **Where it runs**: a handover plan per book, an intention of the tab that made it. It asks the run to pause (after the step, or now), waits until it has ended, moves the book (`moveToServer` or `moveToBrowser`: the same consents and conflict handling as \"Where it runs\"), and starts the autopilot on the other side only if it was running; with a number of steps it runs that many there (`limits.steps`) and comes back. A move that cannot be made leaves the book stopped where it was, with the reason; a failure after the move (the start on the other side, the way back) does not move the book back, and the message says where it is. A handover never sends a secret: a server that lacks the AI configuration or the book's GitHub token sends the author to \"Where it runs\".\n- **Older servers** that do not announce `autopilot-controls` ignore `limits` and take `after-chapter` and `cancel` for `after-step`, so the web app does not send those commands to them and says which version of the server has them.\n\n### The personal server\n\n- **Pairing and identity**: the browser pairs with a nine-digit code generated in the server's admin panel (`http://127.0.0.1:24243`) and approved there by the owner. Before sending its token, the browser asks the server to sign a fresh nonce with its Ed25519 key and compares the key's fingerprint with the one remembered at pairing.\n- **Permissions**: each client token carries `read`, `write`, `run` and, only when the owner allows it, `update`.\n- **Updates**: a client with `update` (or the admin panel) can ask the supervisor to install the latest npm release. The supervisor installs it only when it carries the npm registry signature and the provenance attestation of this repository's publishing workflow, and only when no autopilot is running. A failed installation keeps the previous version.\n- **Network**: the server listens on `127.0.0.1` only and calls only the AI endpoints approved by its owner. Remote access goes through HTTPS, for example the Cloudflare connector managed from the admin panel.\n\nSee the [`narrarium-server` README](../packages/server/README.md) for commands, data folders and the release procedure.\n\n## AI router tasks\n\nThe autopilot uses the router tasks **Autopilot** (writing), **Director**, **Reader evaluation**, **Reader evaluation summary**, **Review** (specialists) and **Simple tasks**. Tasks without a route fall back to models tagged `default`. For long scenes, set the context window and the max output tokens of the models.\n";
const markdown12 = "# Dependency security policy\n\nNarrarium treats the root lockfile and every workspace manifest as one supply-chain surface. CI runs `npm audit --audit-level=info` after a clean install, and `npm run dependency:check` also enforces direct-dependency and chunk-policy invariants.\n\nThe workspace pins npm `12.0.2`. npm 11 can install Sharp's non-host WASM fallbacks as orphaned packages on Linux; npm 12 produces the intended lockfile tree, and the gate runs `npm ls --depth=0` to reject actual extraneous packages.\n\n## 0.76.93 disposition\n\nThe audit was reduced from 13 vulnerable package entries (9 high, 4 moderate) to zero production and development advisories.\n\n### Removed direct dependencies\n\n- `@azure/openai`: no runtime imports. Azure OpenAI support continues through the supported `openai` package and its `AzureOpenAI` client.\n- `mammoth`: no runtime imports. Bounded DOCX extraction uses the existing ZIP/XML implementation.\n- `@radix-ui/react-collapsible` and `@radix-ui/react-tooltip`: no source imports or wrappers.\n- `fast-glob`: moved out of the browser site's runtime dependencies because that workspace uses it only for docs generation and tests. It remains a required runtime dependency of the Node.js `narrarium` core for repository scanning.\n\nThe lockfile no longer retains the Azure REST stack or Mammoth-only XML, promise, parser and utility packages.\n\n### Fixed compatible updates\n\n- PostCSS `8.5.26`, nanoid `3.3.18` and `5.1.16`, and brace-expansion `2.1.4` resolve the build-time and transitive advisories.\n- Astro `7.2.6` resolves the Astro, Sharp, SVGO and js-yaml advisories in the static reader workspace.\n- Model Context Protocol SDK `1.30.0` resolves the Hono adapter/runtime, URI and IP parsing advisory chains used by the MCP workspace.\n- React Router `7.18.2` resolves the client-navigation open-redirect advisory and the SSR hydration advisory. Narrarium remains a client-only BrowserRouter application, but the major migration removes both advisory records rather than relying only on reachability.\n\nAstro 7 requires Node.js `>=22.12.0`; both the published reader and its default scaffold CLI declare that engine floor.\n\n### Chunk policy\n\nManual chunk rules exist only for packages that produce actual, stable boundaries: React/framework, Octokit, JSZip and OpenAI. Obsolete PDF.js, Mammoth and Azure OpenAI rules are forbidden by `scripts/check-dependency-policy.mjs`.\n\n## Update policy\n\n- Do not run broad `npm audit fix` without reviewing the manifest and lockfile diff.\n- Prefer the smallest compatible security update first.\n- Major migrations require typecheck, production build, route-family E2E, GitHub Pages basename E2E and PWA/offline checks.\n- A dependency may remain temporarily only with a documented reachability analysis, owner and follow-up issue. The current audit has no such exception.\n";
const markdown13 = "# Narrarium deployment routing\n\nNarrarium is hosted on GitHub Pages and uses React Router's browser-history URLs.\n\n## Supported contract\n\n- Finite entry points such as `/`, `/app/`, `/app/books/`, `/app/patch-notes/`, `/login/`, `/docs/`, and every published `/docs/<slug>/` page have physical HTML files and return HTTP 200.\n- Repository-derived deep links such as `/app/books/<book-id>/chapters/<chapter-id>` are unbounded and cannot have physical files generated ahead of time.\n- GitHub Pages returns the shared `404.html` application shell for those dynamic paths. The initial origin status is therefore 404, but the shell keeps the original URL and React Router opens the requested book location.\n- Once the PWA controls the page, its service worker serves the cached application shell for navigation requests, including dynamic deep links.\n\nThis is an intentional hosting tradeoff. A browser refresh retains the current dynamic location. HTTP monitors, link-preview crawlers, and clients that reject a 404 before running JavaScript will not treat dynamic routes as successful responses.\n\n## Rejected alternatives\n\n- Hash routing would change canonical and shared URLs to `/#/app/...`, complicate PWA navigation and OAuth fragments, and leave existing clean deep links returning 404.\n- A true HTTP 200 for every clean dynamic route requires a host or edge layer with an unmatched-route rewrite to `/index.html`. The project has explicitly chosen to remain entirely on GitHub Pages.\n- A JavaScript redirect in `404.html` cannot change the status of the initial response and adds an unnecessary navigation round trip.\n\nIf the hosting constraint changes, retain the clean BrowserRouter URLs and add a server-side SPA fallback rather than migrating to hash routing.\n";
const markdown14 = "# GitHub OAuth in the static Narrarium client\n\nNarrarium includes the requested GitHub OAuth Web Flow preparation with PKCE:\n\n- one-time random `state`;\n- one-time `code_verifier`;\n- SHA-256 `code_challenge` with `S256`;\n- ten-minute attempt expiry;\n- callback state validation and immediate attempt invalidation;\n- an isolated triple-Base64 client-secret decoder.\n\nThe triple Base64 value is obfuscation only. It is not encryption and does not protect a client secret shipped in a public JavaScript bundle. The decoded value is never persisted or logged.\n\n## Browser verification\n\nOn 2026-08-31 the token exchange was tested from a real headless Chromium page whose origin was `https://narrarium.net`.\n\nThe browser request to:\n\n```text\nPOST https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token\n```\n\nfailed with:\n\n```text\nTypeError: Failed to fetch\n```\n\nA direct HTTP inspection of the same POST response showed no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header. GitHub's OAuth documentation also states that CORS preflight requests are not supported for the flow.\n\nConsequently, a static browser cannot read the token response. `mode: \"no-cors\"` is intentionally not used because it would return an opaque, unreadable response. No proxy, backend, worker, or serverless function has been introduced.\n\nThe PKCE implementation remains isolated for a future GitHub CORS change, but the production UI disables OAuth and presents PAT as the working browser-only option. Account and book repository adapters consume a generic token, so enabling OAuth later will not require another storage or repository refactor.\n";
const markdown15 = "# Publishing\n\nNarrarium is prepared to publish as seven public npm packages; six follow the release order below and `narrarium-server` has its own release workflow.\n\n## Final package names\n\n- `narrarium`\n- `narrarium-sdk`\n- `narrarium-mcp-server`\n- `create-narrarium-book`\n- `narrarium-astro-reader`\n- `narrarium-engine`\n- `narrarium-server`\n\n## Versioning note\n\n- The public package line started at `0.1.0`.\n\nThe public packages use independent versions. Each package version is published and checked separately; dependency ranges must still resolve to the workspace versions in publication order.\n\n## Release order\n\n1. `narrarium`\n2. `narrarium-engine`\n3. `narrarium-sdk`\n4. `narrarium-astro-reader`\n5. `narrarium-mcp-server`\n6. `create-narrarium-book`\n\nThe starter and the reader depend on the published package names, so publish the lower-level packages first. `narrarium-engine` depends on `narrarium`.\n\n`narrarium-server` is not part of this order: it bundles the engine into a self-contained package (no runtime dependencies) and is released separately, see below.\n\n## Dry run checks\n\nFrom the workspace root:\n\n```bash\nnpm run release:check\n```\n\nThis runs:\n\n- workspace build\n- TypeScript validation\n- automated package tests\n- `npm pack --dry-run` for every public package\n\n## GitHub Actions setup\n\nThe repository includes:\n\n- `.github/workflows/ci.yml`\n- `.github/workflows/publish-npm.yml`\n- `.github/workflows/server.yml`: personal server tests on Linux, Windows and macOS\n- `.github/workflows/publish-server-npm.yml`: `narrarium-server` releases\n\nPackages are published with **npm Trusted Publishing**: GitHub Actions proves its identity to npm through OIDC, so no npm token is stored, and every version carries a provenance attestation.\n\n1. A package must already exist on npm before a trusted publisher can be registered. Publish the first version of a new package once by hand from a clean, built checkout (`npm ci`, `npm run build`, `npm login` with two-factor authentication, then `npm publish -w <package> --access public --provenance=false`).\n2. On npmjs.com open the package **Settings → Trusted publishing** and add GitHub Actions: owner `KeyserDSoze`, repository `GhostWriterFramework`, workflow `publish-npm.yml`, environment `production`.\n3. Merge version bumps to `main`. Optionally use either fallback trigger when needed:\n   - create a GitHub Release tagged `vnarrarium@0.1.58`, `vcreate-narrarium-book@0.1.59`, and so on\n   - run the `Publish npm packages` workflow manually\n\nNo npm token is stored in the repository: the workflow has only the `id-token: write` permission, and every package is registered with its trusted publisher.\n\nOn pushes to `main`, the publish workflow checks npm first and only publishes workspace packages whose local version is not already present in the registry. It never creates a package: a package that is not on npm yet is skipped with a warning asking for its first manual publish.\nWhen unpublished versions are detected, it runs the full release checks and then publishes packages in dependency order.\nOn GitHub Release events it verifies an unambiguous package tag such as `vnarrarium@0.1.57` or `vcreate-narrarium-book@0.1.58`, checks the tagged package's workspace dependency ranges, and checks that dependencies occur earlier in the publication order. The old ambiguous `v0.1.58` form is rejected.\n\nIf GitHub Actions fails with `E401`, `E403` or `EOTP`, the package has no trusted publisher for this workflow and environment yet (check the workflow file name and the environment): register it as described above.\n\nIf GitHub Actions fails with `E404` while publishing, npm is usually telling you that the package does not exist yet or is not available to the publishing identity: publish its first version by hand, then register the trusted publisher.\n\n## The personal server (`narrarium-server`)\n\nThe personal server updates itself from npm and installs only releases that carry the provenance attestation of `publish-server-npm.yml`, so it is published only by that workflow, through npm Trusted Publishing (OIDC, no `NPM_TOKEN`):\n\n1. Bump `version` in `packages/server/package.json` and `SERVER_VERSION` in `packages/server/src/version.ts`, commit and push.\n2. Push the tag `narrarium-server-v<version>`, for example `git tag narrarium-server-v0.1.2 && git push origin narrarium-server-v0.1.2`.\n\nThe very first version (`0.1.0`) must be published by hand, because npm binds a Trusted Publisher only to an existing package; then configure the Trusted Publisher (repository `KeyserDSoze/GhostWriterFramework`, workflow `publish-server-npm.yml`, environment `npm-publish`). The exact steps are in the [`narrarium-server` README](../packages/server/README.md#maintainers-releasing).\n\n## Publish example\n\nPublish in release order from the workspace root:\n\n```bash\nnpm publish -w narrarium --access public\nnpm publish -w narrarium-sdk --access public\nnpm publish -w narrarium-astro-reader --access public\nnpm publish -w narrarium-mcp-server --access public\nnpm publish -w create-narrarium-book --access public\n```\n\nYou can also use the root helper script locally:\n\n```bash\nnpm run publish:all\n```\n\n## Notes\n\n- The default production flow is: bump versions, merge to `main`, and let GitHub Actions publish the packages that are not already on npm.\n- `narrarium-reader-init` supports published installs and can also be driven locally from the workspace.\n- `create-narrarium-book --with-reader` keeps using a local file dependency when run from this monorepo so the generated reader works immediately during development.\n";
const markdown16 = "# Query Canon Guide\n\n`query_canon` is the fastest way to ask the repository a continuity question in natural language.\n\nIt sits between raw search and full manual file reading:\n\n- `search_book` finds text matches\n- `query_canon` tries to answer the question\n- source files are still returned so the answer stays auditable\n\n## What it can answer\n\nCurrent-state questions:\n\n- where a character or entity is\n- what a character knows\n- what a character has in inventory\n- what a relationship currently is\n- what conditions or wounds are active\n- which open loops are still active\n- who holds a secret\n- when something first appears\n\nRange and evolution questions:\n\n- how a relationship changes between two chapters\n- how a condition or wound state changes between two chapters\n- which open loops open or resolve across a chapter range\n\n## Where the answer comes from\n\n`query_canon` combines several layers, in this order:\n\n1. structured story state in `state/chapters/` and `state/current.md`\n2. chapter resume deltas in `resumes/chapters/*.md` under `state_changes`\n3. canon metadata in entity files\n4. chapter and paragraph text\n5. fallback repository search\n\nThis means the tool is strongest when:\n\n- chapter resumes are up to date\n- `state_changes` is filled in carefully\n- `sync_story_state` has been run after stable rewrites\n\nIf story state is stale, the answer still works, but the tool adds a note so the user knows continuity snapshots may lag behind recent edits.\n\n## Best use cases\n\nUse `query_canon` when you want a short answer plus sources.\n\nGood examples:\n\n- `Where is Lyra right now?`\n- `What does Lyra know after chapter 4?`\n- `What is Lyra's relationship with Taren?`\n- `What condition is Lyra in?`\n- `What open loops are still active?`\n- `Who knows this secret?`\n- `When does the brass key first appear?`\n- `How does Lyra's relationship with Taren change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `How does Lyra's condition change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `What open loops change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n\nLess ideal examples:\n\n- broad thematic analysis with no concrete target\n- style questions better answered from prose directly\n- requests that depend on facts never written into canon, resumes, or state\n\n## Instant queries\n\nThese ask for the latest known state, or for state through a specific chapter.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nWhere is Lyra right now?\nWhat does Lyra know after chapter 4?\nWhat does Lyra have?\nWhat is Lyra's relationship with Taren?\nWhat condition is Lyra in?\nWhat open loops are still active?\n```\n\nIf the question mentions a chapter like `after chapter 4`, the tool scopes the answer to that point in the book.\n\n## Arc queries\n\nThese compare two chapter snapshots and summarize the change.\n\nExamples:\n\n```text\nHow does Lyra's relationship with Taren change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?\nHow does Lyra's condition change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?\nWhat open loops change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?\n```\n\nThe answer is built from the ordered story-state snapshots and chapter resume deltas, so it can say things like:\n\n- a relationship moves from `wary-trust` to `guarded-loyalty`\n- a condition changes from `exhausted` to `focused`\n- an open loop is opened in one chapter and resolved in another\n\n## Scope controls\n\nNatural-language parsing is supported, but exact controls are available too.\n\n### MCP tool\n\n`query_canon` accepts:\n\n- `question`\n- `throughChapter`\n- `fromChapter`\n- `toChapter`\n- `limit`\n\nUse `throughChapter` for point-in-time answers.\n\nUse `fromChapter` plus `toChapter` for range comparisons.\n\n### Core API\n\n```ts\nimport { queryCanon } from \"narrarium\";\n\nconst result = await queryCanon(\"my-book\", \"What does Lyra know?\", {\n  throughChapter: \"chapter:004-market-ashes\",\n});\n\nconst arc = await queryCanon(\"my-book\", \"How does Lyra's condition change?\", {\n  fromChapter: \"chapter:003-rain-gate\",\n  toChapter: \"chapter:008-black-ledger\",\n});\n```\n\n## Output fields\n\n`query_canon` returns structured output so agents can decide what to do next.\n\n- `answer`: short human-readable answer\n- `confidence`: `high`, `medium`, or `low`\n- `intent`: what kind of query was recognized\n- `matchedTarget`: the entity or chapter id that matched best\n- `throughChapter`: the point-in-time chapter scope, when used\n- `fromChapter` and `toChapter`: the resolved comparison range, when used\n- `sources`: the files used to support the answer\n- `notes`: ambiguity warnings, stale-state warnings, and fallback notes\n\n## Recommended workflow\n\nFor best results:\n\n1. write or revise final chapter or paragraph prose\n2. let the normal maintenance sync refresh `plot.md` and `resumes/`\n3. update `state_changes` in the relevant chapter resume\n4. run `sync_story_state`\n5. use `query_canon` to check continuity and evolution\n\nThis makes `query_canon` useful not only for drafting, but also for revision and continuity QA.\n\n## Limitations\n\n`query_canon` is strong, but it is not magic.\n\n- it can only answer from what is actually written in canon, resumes, state, or prose\n- if `state_changes` is incomplete, state-based answers may be partial\n- if names are ambiguous, the tool may choose one target and add an ambiguity note\n- open loops are global state, so target-specific filtering is heuristic\n- some broad literary questions are still better served by directly reading the chapter text\n\n## When to use something else\n\n- use `search_book` when you want raw text matches\n- use `list_related_canon` when you want files that mention an id or concept\n- use `chapter_writing_context` or `resume_book_context` when you need broader drafting context instead of a single answer\n\n## Related docs\n\n- `docs/repository-spec.md`\n- `packages/mcp-server/README.md`\n- `packages/core/README.md`\n";
const markdown17 = "# Reader Password Protection\n\nNarrarium Reader supports AES-256-GCM build-time content encryption.\nWhen enabled, all prose is encrypted before the static site is generated.\nVisitors must enter the correct password in the browser before any text is revealed.\nThe password never leaves the browser — all decryption runs client-side via the Web Crypto API.\n\nProtected content includes chapter and scene bodies, canon dossier bodies and popup notes, workshop documents and draft summaries, timeline prose, evaluation bodies, and glossary/search data. Titles, navigation labels, structural metadata, threshold hints, and asset bytes remain available to the static page so routes can be discovered and rendered. Public mode still applies spoiler thresholds independently of password encryption.\n\n## How it works\n\n1. At build time, `NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD` is read from the environment.\n2. A 16-byte random salt is generated once for the entire build (module-level singleton).\n3. Every prose block is encrypted with AES-256-GCM using a PBKDF2-derived key (100 000 iterations, SHA-256, 32-byte key).\n4. The ciphertext and IV are embedded in the HTML as `data-enc-iv` / `data-enc-ct` attributes.\n5. The PBKDF2 salt and an AES-GCM canary ciphertext are embedded in `<body>` for the client; the password itself is never embedded.\n6. The client derives the AES key via PBKDF2 and verifies it by decrypting the canary before decrypting all content in place.\n7. The derived AES key is stored in `localStorage` so the reader auto-decrypts on subsequent page loads.\n\n## Local development\n\nAdd the variable to the reader's `.env` file (not committed to git):\n\n```bash\nNARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD=your-secret-password\n```\n\nRun `npm run dev` as usual. The build log will confirm:\n\n```\n[narrarium-reader] Content encryption enabled (AES-256-GCM).\n```\n\nIf the variable is absent, the log instead says:\n\n```\n[narrarium-reader] NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD not set — building without encryption.\n```\n\n## GitHub Pages deployment\n\n### 1. Store the password as a repository secret\n\nIn the book repository on GitHub:\n**Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → New repository secret**\n\n- Name: `NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD`\n- Value: your password\n\n### 2. Pass the secret to the build step\n\nOpen `.github/workflows/deploy-pages.yml` (created by `reader:init`).\nFind the `Build site` step and add the secret to the `env:` block:\n\n```yaml\n- name: Build site\n  env:\n    SITE_BASE: /${{ github.event.repository.name }}/\n    NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD }}\n  run: npm run build\n```\n\nWithout the explicit `env:` mapping, GitHub Actions will **not** pass the secret to `process.env`, so the build will silently produce an unencrypted site.\n\n### 3. Verify\n\nAfter the next workflow run, check the build log for:\n\n```\n[narrarium-reader] Content encryption enabled (AES-256-GCM).\n```\n\nIf you see the `not set` message instead, the secret is not reaching the build step.\n\n## Scaffolded workflows\n\nReaders scaffolded from `narrarium-astro-reader` v0.1.31 and later include the `NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD` line as a comment in the generated workflow, ready to uncomment:\n\n```yaml\n- name: Build site\n  env:\n    SITE_BASE: /${{ github.event.repository.name }}/\n    # NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.NARRARIUM_READER_PASSWORD }}\n  run: npm run build\n```\n\nFor readers scaffolded with an earlier version, add the line manually.\n\n## Security notes\n\n- The salt is public and embedded in the HTML — its purpose is to prevent rainbow tables, not to hide the encryption parameters.\n- The canary is public ciphertext. It verifies the derived key through AES-GCM authentication and does not provide a fast password-validation oracle.\n- Security comes entirely from the password entropy. Use a strong, randomly generated password.\n- Content on pages that have never been visited will not be cached by the browser, but the derived AES key is stored in `localStorage`. Clearing site data removes it.\n- There is no server-side component. This feature is designed for static hosting (GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc.).\n";
const markdown18 = "# Repository Spec\n\nNarrarium treats a repository as the source of truth for a book project.\n\n## Root structure\n\n```text\nbook.md\ncontext.md\nideas.md\nstory-design.md\nnotes.md\npromoted.md\nplot.md\nguidelines/\n  images.md\nghostwriters/\n  default.md\ncharacters/\nitems/\nlocations/\nfactions/\ntimelines/\n  main.md\n  events/\nsecrets/\nchapters/\n  001-chapter-title/\n    chapter.md\n    001-paragraph-title.md\n    002-paragraph-title.md\naudit/\n  book.md\n  chapters/\n    001-chapter-title/\n      chapter.md\n      paragraphs/\n        001-paragraph-title.md\n        002-paragraph-title.md\ndrafts/\n  001-chapter-title/\n    chapter.md\n    ideas.md\n    notes.md\n    promoted.md\n    001-paragraph-title.md\n    002-paragraph-title.md\nconversations/\n  README.md\n  RESUME.md\n  CONTINUATION.md\n  sessions/\n.opencode/\n  commands/\n    resume-book.md\n  plugins/\n    conversation-export.js\nresumes/\n  total.md\n  chapters/\nstate/\n  current.md\n  status.md\n  chapters/\nevaluations/\n  total.md\n  chapters/\n  paragraphs/\noperations/\n  rewrite-from-reader-feedback/\n    chapters/<chapter-slug>/<operation-id>/\n      manifest.md\n      snapshots/<paragraph-slug>-before.md\n      snapshots/<paragraph-slug>-generated.md\n    paragraphs/<chapter-slug>/<paragraph-slug>/<operation-id>/\n      manifest.md\n      snapshots/<paragraph-slug>-before.md\n      snapshots/<paragraph-slug>-generated.md\nresearch/\n  wikipedia/\n    en/\n    it/\nassets/\n  book/\n  characters/\n  items/\n  locations/\n  factions/\n  timelines/events/\n  secrets/\n  chapters/\n.opencode/skills/narrarium-book/SKILL.md\n.claude/skills/narrarium-book/SKILL.md\n```\n\n## Naming rules\n\n- directories and filenames use lowercase slugs\n- chapter directories begin with a three-digit ordinal, for example `001-the-arrival`\n- paragraph files begin with a three-digit ordinal inside each chapter directory\n- entities use stable ids in frontmatter such as `character:lyra-vale`\n\n## Audit companions\n\nThe `audit/` tree stores the current author-facing Audit companion for a canonical source file. Audit documents are support content, not chapter or paragraph prose.\n\nMappings are deterministic:\n\n- `book.md` maps to `audit/book.md`\n- `chapters/<chapter>/chapter.md` maps to `audit/chapters/<chapter>/chapter.md`\n- `chapters/<chapter>/<paragraph>.md` maps to `audit/chapters/<chapter>/paragraphs/<paragraph>.md`\n\nKeep at most one current Audit companion at each mapped path. Source renames move the corresponding Audit file or chapter subtree and rewrite `chapter:` and `paragraph:` references. Do not retain timestamped copies or old-path duplicates in `audit/`; Git history is the history of prior Audit results.\n\nCore does not currently expose semantic `deleteChapter`, `deleteParagraph`, `deleteEntity`, or `deleteBook` operations. `NarrariumBookWorkspace.deleteDocument(path)` is a generic single-document change and does not cascade. A caller deleting source files directly must also delete their mapped Audit companions; `doctorBook()` reports malformed and orphan Audit files but never removes them automatically.\n\n## Reader-feedback rewrite operations\n\nGenerated prose drafts use the canonical path `drafts/<chapter-slug>/<paragraph-slug>.md`. Implementations may read the legacy `chapters/<chapter-slug>/drafts/<paragraph-slug>.md` path as a fallback, but all new writes use the canonical path.\n\nEvery applied rewrite has a regular Git-tracked operation directory under `operations/rewrite-from-reader-feedback/`. The manifest records the target ids, base remote head, progress, full LLM run metadata, aggregate token/cost data, source hashes, and every modified draft. Before any draft changes, the operation commits and pushes its manifest plus a `before` snapshot for each target file. An absent original draft is represented in the manifest with `existedBefore: false` and an empty marker snapshot.\n\nEach successfully generated paragraph is committed and pushed with its `generated` snapshot and updated manifest. Chapter rewrites make one routed generation call per paragraph in chapter order; incomplete operations remain `failed`, `cancelled`, or `conflict` and retain the list of successfully modified files.\n\nRollback is targeted, never a branch reset. A draft is restored only when its current SHA-256 matches the operation's applied hash, unless the caller explicitly selects force-restore for that file. Conflicts may instead keep the current file or cancel rollback. Restored files and the final manifest status are written in one commit and push. Chapter and paragraph renames/reorders move these operation trees and rewrite stored target references; `doctorBook()` reports malformed paths, missing manifests, and operations whose source target no longer exists.\n\n## Markdown frontmatter\n\nAll content files should start with YAML frontmatter.\n\n### Common keys\n\n- `type`: semantic type such as `character`, `location`, `chapter`, `paragraph`\n- `id`: stable internal id\n- `name` or `title`: human label\n- `status`: optional lifecycle state\n- `canon`: usually `draft`, `canon`, or `deprecated`\n- `tags`: optional tag list\n- `refs`: optional ids related to this file\n- `secret_refs`: optional linked secret ids for hidden canon tied to the entity\n- `private_notes`: optional author-facing hidden canon note\n- `known_from`: optional threshold for when the reader can safely know the hidden canon\n- `reveal_in`: optional chapter or milestone where hidden canon should fully surface\n- `sources`: optional research sources\n- `historical`: marks content that should be checked against external sources\n\n### Ghostwriter profiles\n\n- `book.md` selects the default profile with `ghostwriter: default`\n- `ghostwriters/<slug>.md` contains one complete prose contract: style and punctuation frontmatter plus detailed body instructions\n- chapter, paragraph, draft, and script frontmatter can select another profile with `ghostwriter`\n- resolution uses the closest explicit selection, then paragraph, chapter, and book\n\nThe generated reader uses `known_from` and `reveal_in` for spoiler-safe search, canon popups, public atlas pages, and backlink filtering.\n\n### Resume and state-specific keys\n\n- `state_changes`: optional structured continuity delta stored in chapter resume frontmatter\n- `dirty`: used in `state/status.md` to mark whether continuity snapshots are stale\n- `last_story_mutation_at`: when final story prose last changed\n- `last_story_state_sync_at`: when `sync_story_state` last rebuilt structured state\n- `changed_paths`: final story files that changed since the last story-state sync\n- `reason`: brief machine-readable reason such as `paragraph-updated` or `chapter-renamed`\n\n### Character example\n\n```md\n---\ntype: character\nid: character:lyra-vale\nname: Lyra Vale\naliases:\n  - The Glass Fox\nrole_tier: main\nstory_role: protagonist\nspeaking_style: Controlled, observant, exact. She uses short sentences when pressured.\nbackground_summary: Raised in trade politics and covert exchanges around Gray Harbor.\nfunction_in_book: Primary viewpoint anchor for the opening movement and reader entry into the world.\noccupation: Broker of information\norigin: Gray Harbor\nfirst_impression: Competent, composed, difficult to read\ntraits:\n  - observant\n  - guarded\n  - adaptable\ndesires:\n  - protect her leverage\n  - uncover the truth behind the missing archive\nfears:\n  - becoming predictable\n  - failing the few people she trusts\ninternal_conflict: She wants intimacy but trusts control more than honesty.\nexternal_conflict: Several factions want the same information she is trying to bury and decode.\narc: Moves from strategic distance toward costly emotional commitment.\nrelationships:\n  - Has unfinished history with the Night Syndicate.\nfactions:\n  - faction:night-syndicate\nhome_location: location:gray-harbor\nintroduced_in: chapter:001-the-arrival\nstatus: alive\ncanon: draft\nhistorical: false\ntags:\n  - spy\n  - diplomat\ntimeline_ages:\n  chapter:001-the-arrival: 29\nsecret_refs:\n  - secret:lyra-is-the-heir\nprivate_notes: Lyra already suspects the missing archive was hidden by her own bloodline.\nknown_from: chapter:006-blood-in-ledgers\nreveal_in: chapter:008-crown-of-ashes\n---\n```\n\n### Secret example\n\n```md\n---\ntype: secret\nid: secret:lyra-is-the-heir\ntitle: Lyra is the lost heir\nholders:\n  - character:lyra-vale\nreveal_in: chapter:008-crown-of-ashes\nknown_from: chapter:008-crown-of-ashes\nstatus: hidden\n---\n```\n\n### Chapter resume with state delta example\n\n```md\n---\ntype: resume\nid: resume:chapter:001-the-arrival\ntitle: Resume 001-the-arrival\nchapter: chapter:001-the-arrival\nstate_changes:\n  locations:\n    \"character:lyra-vale\": \"location:gray-harbor\"\n  knowledge_gain:\n    \"character:lyra-vale\":\n      - guards-are-on-a-new-rotation\n  inventory_add:\n    \"character:lyra-vale\":\n      - item:brass-key\n  relationship_updates:\n    \"character:lyra-vale\":\n      \"character:taren-dane\": wary-trust\n  open_loops_add:\n    - find-the-ledger\n---\n\n# Chapter Summary\n\nLyra returns to Gray Harbor and realizes the city is already watching for her.\n```\n\n### Ghostwriter selection example\n\n```md\n---\ntype: chapter\nid: chapter:002-glass-confession\nnumber: 2\ntitle: Glass Confession\nghostwriter: intimate-confession\n---\n```\n\n## Story state snapshots\n\nNarrarium now distinguishes between narrative summaries and structured continuity state.\n\n### Why both exist\n\n- `resumes/` is for humans and LLM prose context: what happened, what matters, what tone and movement the chapter has\n- `state/` is for structured continuity: where characters are, what they know, what they carry, who trusts whom, and what loops remain open\n\nThis avoids forcing every continuity fact into a single prose summary while still keeping the repository readable.\n\n### Files in `state/`\n\n- `state/status.md`: persistent dirty flag and sync metadata\n- `state/current.md`: consolidated latest snapshot after all known chapter deltas\n- `state/chapters/<slug>.md`: snapshot after each chapter in reading order\n\n### Manual sync model\n\nStory-state sync is intentionally manual.\n\nAfter final entity, chapter, or paragraph mutations Narrarium does this automatically:\n\n- refreshes `plot.md`\n- refreshes `resumes/chapters/*.md`\n- refreshes `resumes/total.md`\n- marks `state/status.md` as dirty\n\nIt does **not** automatically rebuild `state/current.md`. The author decides when continuity is stable enough.\n\nDraft files, scripts, notes, and evaluations are working support material and do not mark story state dirty. They are not part of the canonical continuity snapshot until promoted into final canon or final prose.\n\nWhen ready, run:\n\n```text\nsync_story_state\n```\n\nThat command reads `state_changes` from chapter resumes in order and rebuilds all structured story-state snapshots.\n\n### Recommended `state_changes` shape\n\nUse these keys when they help continuity:\n\n- `locations`\n- `knowledge_gain`\n- `knowledge_loss`\n- `inventory_add`\n- `inventory_remove`\n- `relationship_updates`\n- `conditions`\n- `wounds`\n- `open_loops_add`\n- `open_loops_resolved`\n\nTreat `state_changes` as the chapter delta, not as a full-world snapshot.\n\n### Context and diagnostics\n\n- `chapter_writing_context`, `paragraph_writing_context`, and `resume_book_context` read `state/current.md` when present\n- if `state/status.md` is dirty, those contexts also surface the stale-state warning\n- `doctorBook()` warns about missing or stale `state/` files so continuity drift is visible in CI and reader scaffolds\n\n## Operational rules for the agent\n\n- search the repository before inventing canon\n- use `start_wizard` for guided creation when the brief is incomplete and multiple fields still need collecting\n- use `character_wizard` before `create_character` when character information is incomplete\n- use `create_chapter_draft` and `create_paragraph_draft` when roughing a scene before writing final prose\n- use `save_book_item` and `save_chapter_item` for structured active idea and note entries\n- use `promote_book_item` and `promote_chapter_item` to move reviewed items out of active queues while preserving them in promoted archives\n- use `update_book_notes` for freeform edits to `notes.md` or `story-design.md`, and `update_chapter_notes` for freeform chapter-local note edits when you are editing the documents themselves instead of managing structured entries\n- use `chapter_writing_context` and `paragraph_writing_context` before drafting polished prose from rough material\n- use `resume_book_context` or `/resume-book` when restarting from exported conversation history\n- use `create_chapter_from_draft` and `create_paragraph_from_draft` when promoting rough material into final story files\n- use `sync_plot` to refresh the root plot map after story progression, reveal timing, or timeline changes\n- use `location_wizard`, `faction_wizard`, `item_wizard`, and `secret_wizard` before creating rich canon files when briefs are incomplete\n- use `query_canon` when the agent needs an answer like where someone is, what they know, who holds a secret, when something first appears, or how a relationship/condition/open loop changes across a chapter range\n- see `docs/query-canon.md` for a dedicated guide with examples, scope controls, and limits\n- use `revise_paragraph` for proposal-only editorial passes on final scene files when you want a rewrite suggestion without mutating the repo yet\n- see `docs/revise-paragraph.md` for revision modes, continuity review behavior, and the manual apply flow\n- use `revise_chapter` for proposal-only chapter diagnosis and scene-by-scene revision plans before deciding what to apply manually\n- see `docs/revise-chapter.md` for the chapter-level workflow and output model\n- use `resumes/` to keep running summaries stable\n- use `state/` for structured continuity snapshots and refresh it manually with `sync_story_state` after stable rewrites\n- use `evaluations/` for structural critique, continuity checks, quality notes, editorial reading against selected ghostwriter profiles, canon coherence checks against active characters, locations, factions, items, and timelines, plus an explicit explanation of why the weighted verdict landed where it did\n- chapter and paragraph evaluations should be saved files, not just transient chat output\n- use `conversations/` for portable exported chat history, resume files, and continuation prompts; treat it as support material, not canon\n- use `npm run doctor` or `doctorBook()` to catch broken references, stale maintenance files, missing asset descriptions, and spoiler-threshold problems\n- if content is historical or factual, fetch research before writing canon\n- before fetching Wikipedia again, reuse a matching snapshot from `research/wikipedia/` when one already exists; use explicit refresh controls when the snapshot is stale or should be bypassed\n- prefer updating existing canon files over duplicating similar facts elsewhere\n- before writing final chapter or paragraph prose, read the selected `ghostwriters/<slug>.md` profile, relevant prior story files, and any matching files in `drafts/`\n- write character, item, location, faction, secret, and timeline-event names as plain text in chapter and paragraph prose; do not insert markdown links to canon files or reader routes because the reader resolves visible mentions automatically\n- treat `ideas.md`, `notes.md`, `story-design.md`, `promoted.md`, and their chapter-draft variants as working support material, not canon; move stable facts into canon files when they become true in the book\n- keep `plot.md` aligned with chapter summaries, reveals, and dated timeline anchors\n- final chapter and paragraph mutations through Narrarium MCP auto-refresh `plot.md` plus the chapter and total resumes, but story-state sync stays manual; evaluations remain explicit/manual\n\n## Reader behavior\n\nGenerated readers default to a spoiler-safe public mode.\n\n- secret pages stay hidden from the public atlas and nav\n- direct canon pages may render as teaser or locked views instead of full dossiers\n- search, canon popups, and story backlinks only surface lore that is safe for the current chapter threshold\n- plain-text canon mentions in chapter and paragraph prose are upgraded by the reader into spoiler-safe popups and links when they are visible at the current threshold\n\nFor author-only or spoiler-friendly deployments, enable full canon mode with `NARRARIUM_READER_CANON_MODE=full` or `NARRARIUM_READER_ALLOW_FULL_CANON=true` before running the reader build.\n\n## Asset conventions\n\n- keep binary images under `assets/`, not beside canon markdown files\n- mirror canon structure inside `assets/`, for example `assets/characters/lyra-vale/primary.png`\n- keep asset metadata and prompt history in sibling markdown files such as `assets/characters/lyra-vale/primary.md`\n- asset markdown may include `alt_text` and `caption` so reader and EPUB output can reuse consistent descriptions\n- default image orientation is portrait and default aspect ratio is `2:3`\n- chapter scene assets live under `assets/chapters/<chapter-slug>/paragraphs/<paragraph-slug>/`\n- store the book-level visual language in `guidelines/images.md`\n";
const markdown19 = "# Review Dialogue Action Beats Guide\n\n`review_dialogue_action_beats` is a proposal-only editorial tool for final paragraph files in `chapters/`.\n\nIt reviews dialogue-adjacent action beats one by one instead of rewriting the entire scene in one pass.\n\n## What it is for\n\nUse it when you want to know whether an action beat:\n\n- clarifies the speaker well\n- adds tension, blocking, psychology, or subtext\n- fits the character's canon profile\n- should be tightened, replaced, removed, or turned into a simple `said` fallback\n\nIt can also suggest missing beats and low-confidence recurring tics worth observing for a character.\n\n## Workflow\n\nTypical flow:\n\n1. read `paragraph_writing_context`\n2. run `review_dialogue_action_beats`\n3. inspect each beat proposal and decide which choices you want\n4. apply only the confirmed selections with `apply_dialogue_action_beats`\n5. if the beat changes affect continuity-sensitive material, review `state_changes`\n\nThe paragraph context should always include the selected ghostwriter profile. Its frontmatter and body should define dialogue action beats, said-tag fallbacks, use of space, and psychologically meaningful gestures when those constraints matter.\n\n## What it returns\n\n- `reviewId`\n- `paragraphHash`\n- `previewBody`\n- beat-level proposals with explicit `choiceId` values\n- optional tic suggestions\n- source files used for the pass\n\n## What it does not do\n\n- it does not write files\n- it does not rewrite the whole paragraph blindly\n- it does not auto-update character canon with tic suggestions\n\n## Apply step\n\n`apply_dialogue_action_beats` applies only the selected beat changes after confirmation.\n\nIt validates:\n\n- `reviewId`\n- `expectedParagraphHash`\n\nIf the paragraph changed after review, apply should be treated as stale and the review should be rerun.\n";
const markdown20 = "# Revise Chapter Guide\n\n`revise_chapter` is the proposal-only editorial tool for a final chapter in `chapters/`.\n\nIt does **not** write files.\n\nUse it when you want a chapter diagnosis, a revision plan, and scene-by-scene rewrite suggestions before deciding what to apply manually.\n\n## What it is for\n\n`revise_chapter` is the chapter-scale counterpart to `revise_paragraph`.\n\nUse it when you want help with:\n\n- chapter rhythm\n- scene order pressure and momentum\n- consistency of delivery across the chapter\n- repeated exposition or slack prose\n- identifying which scenes deserve revision first\n\nIt works best when the chapter already exists in `chapters/` and you want a controlled editorial pass rather than an automatic rewrite.\n\n## What it returns\n\nThe tool returns:\n\n- chapter-level diagnosis notes\n- a revision plan ordered by scene priority\n- scene proposals for the paragraphs that changed or need continuity review\n- an overall continuity impact signal\n- optional merged `suggestedStateChanges`\n- source files used for the pass\n\nIt is intentionally proposal-only.\n\n## Revision modes\n\nCurrent supported modes:\n\n- `clarity`\n- `pacing`\n- `dialogue`\n- `voice`\n- `tension`\n- `show-dont-tell`\n- `redundancy`\n\nThe same mode is applied across the chapter, but the result is still scene-aware.\n\nThat means:\n\n- some scenes may change a lot\n- some scenes may barely change\n- some scenes may mostly generate continuity warnings rather than heavy prose edits\n\n## Intensity\n\n`revise_chapter` accepts:\n\n- `light`\n- `medium`\n- `strong`\n\nUse `medium` as the normal editorial pass.\n\nUse `strong` when you want a more aggressive cleanup plan, still without writing files.\n\n## How it works\n\nAt a high level:\n\n1. read the final chapter and its scene files\n2. review chapter-level context already present in the repository\n3. run the requested editorial mode across the chapter's scenes\n4. build chapter diagnosis notes\n5. produce a revision plan\n6. suggest `state_changes` review if continuity-sensitive beats appear\n\nThis means the tool is not just one giant rewrite blob. It is meant to help you decide where to act first.\n\n## Typical use cases\n\nGood examples:\n\n- `revise_chapter` with `mode: pacing` when a chapter feels slow or over-explained\n- `revise_chapter` with `mode: tension` when the chapter needs sharper pressure and cleaner scene escalation\n- `revise_chapter` with `mode: redundancy` when multiple scenes repeat the same emotional or informational point\n- `revise_chapter` with `mode: voice` when the chapter reads flatter than the surrounding material\n\n## Continuity behavior\n\nJust like `revise_paragraph`, this tool does not mutate canon or state files.\n\nBut if one or more scenes contain continuity-sensitive beats such as:\n\n- location changes\n- knowledge or reveal beats\n- inventory movement\n- relationship shifts\n- conditions or wounds\n- open loops opening or resolving\n\nthe result can include:\n\n- `overallContinuityImpact`\n- per-scene `continuityImpact`\n- merged `suggestedStateChanges`\n\nThat means:\n\n- if you later apply one or more proposed scene rewrites with `update_paragraph`\n- and those same beats still matter in the revised text\n- you should review the relevant chapter resume `state_changes`\n\nThen run `sync_story_state` manually when the rewrite is stable.\n\n## Recommended workflow\n\nTypical flow:\n\n1. read `chapter_writing_context`\n2. run `revise_chapter`\n3. review the diagnosis and revision plan\n4. choose which scene proposals are worth keeping\n5. apply selected changes manually with `update_paragraph`\n6. review any suggested `state_changes`\n7. run `sync_story_state` when ready\n\nThis keeps revision explicit and lets you avoid accidental chapter-wide mutations.\n\n## MCP example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"revise_chapter\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"chapter\": \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n    \"mode\": \"pacing\",\n    \"intensity\": \"medium\",\n    \"preserveFacts\": true\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Core API example\n\n```ts\nimport { reviseChapter } from \"narrarium\";\n\nconst result = await reviseChapter(\"my-book\", {\n  chapter: \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n  mode: \"tension\",\n  intensity: \"medium\",\n});\n\nconsole.log(result.chapterDiagnosis);\nconsole.log(result.revisionPlan);\nconsole.log(result.proposedParagraphs);\nconsole.log(result.suggestedStateChanges);\n```\n\n## Difference from `revise_paragraph`\n\n- `revise_paragraph` is a scalpel for one scene\n- `revise_chapter` is an editorial pass over the whole chapter\n\nUse `revise_paragraph` when you already know which scene needs work.\n\nUse `revise_chapter` when you want Narrarium to tell you which scenes should move first and why.\n\n## Limits\n\n`revise_chapter` is intentionally conservative.\n\n- it does not write files\n- it does not reorder chapter files automatically\n- it is best for revision planning and targeted scene proposals, not full autonomous chapter replacement\n- continuity suggestions are heuristics and still need author review\n\n## Related docs\n\n- `docs/revise-paragraph.md`\n- `docs/query-canon.md`\n- `docs/repository-spec.md`\n- `packages/core/README.md`\n- `packages/mcp-server/README.md`\n";
const markdown21 = "# Revise Paragraph Guide\n\n`revise_paragraph` is a proposal-only editorial tool for final scene files in `chapters/`.\n\nIt does **not** write files.\n\nUse it when you want Narrarium to suggest a better version of a scene before deciding whether to apply it with `update_paragraph`.\n\n## What it is for\n\n`revise_paragraph` sits between context gathering and actual mutation:\n\n- `paragraph_writing_context` gives you what to read first\n- `revise_paragraph` proposes a focused rewrite\n- `update_paragraph` applies the rewrite only if you decide to keep it\n\nThis keeps revision explicit and audit-friendly.\n\n## What it returns\n\nThe tool returns:\n\n- a proposed paragraph body\n- editorial notes about what changed and why\n- a `continuityImpact` signal\n- optional `suggestedStateChanges`\n- source files used for the pass\n\n## Revision modes\n\nCurrent supported modes:\n\n- `clarity`\n- `pacing`\n- `dialogue`\n- `voice`\n- `tension`\n- `show-dont-tell`\n- `redundancy`\n\nThese are not generic \"rewrite better\" modes. Each one tries to push the paragraph in a specific direction.\n\n### `clarity`\n\nUse this when the scene is understandable but muddy.\n\nIt focuses on:\n\n- trimming filler phrases\n- shortening long chained sentences\n- making the beat easier to parse on first read\n\n### `pacing`\n\nUse this when the scene drags or over-explains.\n\nIt focuses on:\n\n- faster sentence rhythm\n- removing slow lead-ins\n- trimming repeated beats that stall movement\n\n### `dialogue`\n\nUse this when the speech is hard to follow or the beat spacing is muddy.\n\nIt focuses on:\n\n- clearer dialogue beat separation\n- tighter narration around spoken lines\n- less clutter around the exchange\n\n### `voice`\n\nUse this when the paragraph feels distant from the viewpoint or sounds too generic.\n\nIt focuses on:\n\n- reducing filter verbs\n- keeping the beat closer to the active viewpoint\n- preserving the existing facts while making the voice feel less detached\n\n### `tension`\n\nUse this when the scene should feel sharper, tighter, or more pressured.\n\nIt focuses on:\n\n- removing cushioning words\n- stronger pressure cues\n- shorter sentence rhythm where useful\n\n### `show-dont-tell`\n\nUse this when the paragraph explains the emotional or narrative beat too directly.\n\nIt focuses on:\n\n- reducing summary-distance phrasing\n- trimming filter constructions\n- pushing the prose closer to in-scene language\n\n### `redundancy`\n\nUse this when the paragraph repeats the same emotional or narrative point.\n\nIt focuses on:\n\n- removing duplicate emphasis\n- collapsing repeated sentence work\n- tightening the paragraph around its strongest beat\n\n## Intensity\n\n`revise_paragraph` accepts:\n\n- `light`\n- `medium`\n- `strong`\n\nUse `light` when the paragraph is already close.\n\nUse `medium` as the default editorial pass.\n\nUse `strong` when the wording needs more serious compression or reshaping, while still staying proposal-only.\n\n## Continuity behavior\n\nThis tool does not write canon or state files, but it does try to warn you when the paragraph touches continuity-sensitive material.\n\nIf the scene includes things like:\n\n- movement or location changes\n- knowledge or revelation beats\n- item possession changes\n- relationship shifts\n- conditions or wounds\n- open loops or promises\n\nthe result may include:\n\n- `continuityImpact: possible` or `continuityImpact: clear`\n- `suggestedStateChanges`\n\nThat does **not** mean files were changed. It means:\n\n- if you accept and apply the proposal\n- and the revised scene still carries those beats\n- you should review the chapter resume `state_changes`\n\nAfter that, if needed, run `sync_story_state` manually.\n\n## Recommended workflow\n\nTypical flow:\n\n1. read `paragraph_writing_context` so the pass includes the selected ghostwriter profile and the point-in-time story context\n2. run `revise_paragraph`\n3. inspect the proposed body and editorial notes\n4. if you want it, apply the revision manually with `update_paragraph`\n5. if continuity-sensitive beats are involved, review `state_changes`\n6. run `sync_story_state` when the rewrite is stable\n\n## MCP example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"revise_paragraph\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"chapter\": \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n    \"paragraph\": \"001-at-the-gate\",\n    \"mode\": \"tension\",\n    \"intensity\": \"medium\",\n    \"preserveFacts\": true\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Core API example\n\n```ts\nimport { reviseParagraph } from \"narrarium\";\n\nconst result = await reviseParagraph(\"my-book\", {\n  chapter: \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n  paragraph: \"001-at-the-gate\",\n  mode: \"clarity\",\n  intensity: \"medium\",\n});\n\nconsole.log(result.proposedBody);\nconsole.log(result.editorialNotes);\nconsole.log(result.suggestedStateChanges);\n```\n\n## Limits\n\n`revise_paragraph` is intentionally conservative.\n\n- it proposes, but does not apply\n- it is best for targeted passes, not full scene reinvention\n- continuity suggestions are heuristics, not automatic canon truth\n- if the paragraph changes the story materially after you apply it, you still need to review resumes and state manually\n\n## Related docs\n\n- `docs/revise-chapter.md`\n- `docs/query-canon.md`\n- `docs/repository-spec.md`\n- `packages/core/README.md`\n- `packages/mcp-server/README.md`\n";
const markdown22 = "# Ghostwriter Profiles\n\nNarrarium uses ghostwriters as its single prose-style model.\n\n## Default profile\n\nEvery book selects a default ghostwriter in `book.md`:\n\n```yaml\nghostwriter: default\n```\n\nThe corresponding profile lives at `ghostwriters/default.md`. Narrarium creates and selects this profile automatically when it is missing.\n\nA ghostwriter contains the complete prose contract, including:\n\n- writing and punctuation style\n- voice and tone\n- person and tense expectations\n- sentence rhythm and dialogue treatment\n- vocabulary and influences\n- strengths and patterns to avoid\n\n## Overrides\n\nSet `ghostwriter` in chapter or paragraph frontmatter to select another profile for that scope. Resolution order is explicit selection, paragraph, chapter, then book default.\n\n## How writing context works\n\n`chapter_writing_context` and `paragraph_writing_context` resolve the selected profile from `ghostwriters/` and combine it with the relevant point-in-time canon, summaries and draft context.\n\n## Review flow\n\nFor paragraph review:\n\n1. read `paragraph_writing_context`\n2. run `revise_paragraph`\n3. inspect the proposal\n4. ask the user for confirmation\n5. apply with `update_paragraph` only after confirmation\n\nThe same selected ghostwriter guides drafting, revision and review. Standalone writing-style and punctuation-style files are not part of the active model.\n";
const markdown23 = "# narrarium-astro-reader\n\nAstro reader and reader scaffolding CLI for Narrarium book repositories.\n\nRequires Node.js 22.12 or newer.\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install narrarium-astro-reader\n```\n\n## Scaffold a reader app\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-reader-init reader --book-root ..\n```\n\nOr directly from the published package:\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-astro-reader reader --book-root .. --package-name my-book-reader\n```\n\n## What the reader includes\n\n- book landing page plus chapter-by-chapter reading\n- previous, next, and jump chapter navigation\n- reading preferences, bookmark, and continue-reading state stored locally\n- live search across canon, chapters, and scenes\n- character, location, faction, item, secret, and timeline indexes\n- canon mention popups, backlinks, and asset rendering for book, entity, chapter, and scene art\n- a Full read chapter mode that opens an immersive browser-fullscreen reading view with continuous prose, auto-hiding minimal previous/jump/next navigation, a small ghost hint for rediscovering controls, and canon popups still available\n- dismissible Read Aloud controls that can be reopened from a subtle masthead speaker button and stay out of the way during Full read mode\n- automatic EPUB export to `public/downloads/book.epub`\n- live watcher for book markdown, canon, and assets during `npm run dev`\n- `npm run doctor` for broken references, spoiler thresholds, asset metadata, and stale `plot.md`, `resumes/`, or `state/`\n- optional EPUBCheck validation during export or build\n- starter GitHub Pages deployment workflow when scaffolded into a standalone app\n\n## Reader modes\n\nThe generated reader defaults to a spoiler-safe public mode.\n\nIn public mode:\n\n- secrets stay hidden from the public atlas and nav\n- direct canon pages fall back to teaser or locked views when `known_from` or `reveal_in` say a dossier is not safe yet\n- search, canon popups, and backlinks follow the same thresholds\n- chapter and scene prose should keep canon names as plain text; the reader upgrades visible mentions into canon popups and also rewrites legacy internal canon links at runtime\n\nIf you want an author-only or spoiler-friendly deployment, enable full canon mode:\n\n```bash\nNARRARIUM_READER_CANON_MODE=full\n# or\nNARRARIUM_READER_ALLOW_FULL_CANON=true\n```\n\n## Local development\n\nThe scaffold creates `.env` with the computed book root already filled in. Update it if the reader should point somewhere else:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\n\nTypical `.env` values:\n\n```bash\nNARRARIUM_BOOK_ROOT=..\n# NARRARIUM_READER_CANON_MODE=full\n# EPUBCHECK_CMD=epubcheck\n# EPUBCHECK_JAR=/absolute/path/to/epubcheck.jar\n```\n\n`npm run dev` watches the linked book repo, regenerates the EPUB on changes, and triggers a full browser reload.\n`npm run build` refreshes the EPUB automatically before Astro builds the site.\n\n## Validation and export\n\n```bash\nnpm run doctor\nnpm run export:epub\nnpm run build\n```\n\nIf `EPUBCHECK_CMD` or `EPUBCHECK_JAR` is set, EPUB export and build also run EPUBCheck.\n\n`npm run doctor` also reports if the linked book repository has stale or missing structured story-state snapshots, so author-facing continuity drift shows up before deployment.\n\n## GitHub Pages\n\nYou can pass `--pages-domain example.com` when scaffolding to emit `public/CNAME` and a Pages workflow already pointed at that domain.\n";
const markdown24 = "# narrarium\n\nCore Narrarium helpers for local-first book repositories.\n\n## Includes\n\n- repository scaffolding and managed book structure\n- markdown frontmatter schemas and rich canon templates\n- canon search, related-link discovery, and validation\n- draft promotion, plot sync, and resume or evaluation helpers\n- manual story-state sync helpers built from chapter resume deltas\n- `doctorBook()` checks for broken references, spoiler thresholds, asset metadata, and stale maintenance files\n- EPUB export helpers with opening matter, scene navigation, and optional canon index generation\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install narrarium\n```\n\n## What it does\n\n- scaffold the canonical book repository structure\n- validate markdown frontmatter and file placement\n- create rich canon files for characters, locations, factions, items, secrets, and timeline events\n- create and update chapters, scenes, chapter drafts, and paragraph drafts\n- normalize chapter and paragraph prose so canon names stay plain text instead of hand-authored markdown links to canon files\n- answer natural-language canon questions with `queryCanon()`\n- propose targeted scene revisions with `reviseParagraph()` without writing files yet\n- propose chapter-level editorial plans with `reviseChapter()` without mutating files\n- sync `plot.md`, chapter resumes, and total resume files\n- sync `state/current.md`, `state/status.md`, and `state/chapters/` from `state_changes`\n- export the book to EPUB\n- diagnose repository drift with `doctorBook()`\n\n## Quick example\n\n```js\nimport {\n  initializeBookRepo,\n  createCharacterProfile,\n  createChapter,\n  createParagraph,\n  doctorBook,\n} from \"narrarium\";\n\nawait initializeBookRepo(\"my-book\", {\n  title: \"My Book\",\n  author: \"Author Name\",\n  language: \"en\",\n});\n\nawait createCharacterProfile(\"my-book\", {\n  name: \"Lyra Vale\",\n  roleTier: \"main\",\n  speakingStyle: \"Measured and observant.\",\n  backgroundSummary: \"Raised in covert trade circles.\",\n  functionInBook: \"Primary viewpoint anchor.\",\n});\n\nawait createChapter(\"my-book\", {\n  number: 1,\n  title: \"The Arrival\",\n});\n\nawait createParagraph(\"my-book\", {\n  chapter: \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n  number: 1,\n  title: \"At The Gate\",\n});\n\nconst report = await doctorBook(\"my-book\");\nconsole.log(report.ok, report.issues);\n```\n\n## Hidden canon and assets\n\n- use `known_from` and `reveal_in` to mark when canon is safe for public reader views\n- use `secret_refs` and `private_notes` for author-facing hidden canon\n- store asset metadata in markdown beside images and prefer `alt_text` plus `caption` so web and EPUB output stay accessible\n\n## Remote book manager foundations\n\n`narrarium` now also exports the first SDK foundations for remote book access:\n\n- `BookManager` as the high-level entry point\n- `LocalStorageBookProfileStore` and `InMemoryBookProfileStore` for connection profiles\n- `NarrariumBookWorkspace` for in-memory edits before commit and push\n- `NarrariumRemoteProvider` so GitHub and Azure DevOps adapters can plug into the same flow\n- `GitHubRemoteProvider` with real GitHub snapshot loading plus direct commit/push scaffolding\n- `AzureDevOpsRemoteProvider` with matching Azure DevOps snapshot loading and direct push support\n- high-level workspace helpers like `upsertCharacter`, `updateChapter`, and `updateParagraph`\n\nQuick example:\n\n```js\nimport {\n  AzureDevOpsRemoteProvider,\n  BookManager,\n  GitHubRemoteProvider,\n  LocalStorageBookProfileStore,\n} from \"narrarium\";\n\nconst manager = new BookManager({\n  profileStore: new LocalStorageBookProfileStore(),\n  providers: [new GitHubRemoteProvider()],\n});\n\nconst profile = await manager.createGitHubProfile({\n  name: \"Primary Book\",\n  owner: \"your-org\",\n  repository: \"your-book\",\n  branch: \"main\",\n  token: \"github-token\",\n});\n\nconst snapshot = await manager.loadBook(profile);\nconst workspace = manager.beginWorkspace(snapshot);\n\nworkspace.updateChapter(\"chapter:001-the-arrival\", {\n  frontmatter: { title: \"The Arrival Revised\" },\n});\nworkspace.updateParagraph(\"paragraph:001-the-arrival:001-at-the-gate\", {\n  body: \"# Scene\\n\\nThe harbor measures every returning face.\",\n});\n```\n\n`GitHubRemoteProvider` can now resolve the branch head, read the Narrarium markdown tree, build a typed snapshot, and push workspace changes back with GitHub commits. `AzureDevOpsRemoteProvider` now supports the same load and direct push flow through the Azure DevOps Git REST API.\n\nFor Azure DevOps, register `new AzureDevOpsRemoteProvider()` and create a profile with `organization`, `project`, `repository`, `branch`, and PAT token.\n\n## Story state and continuity\n\nNarrarium keeps structured continuity separate from the narrative summaries:\n\n- `resumes/chapters/*.md` stays readable and can include a `state_changes` frontmatter block\n- `state/current.md` stores the latest consolidated continuity snapshot\n- `state/chapters/*.md` stores the snapshot after each chapter\n- `state/status.md` tracks whether story state is stale and why\n\nStory mutations mark the state as stale, but they do not auto-sync the snapshots. That is deliberate: you decide when a rewrite is stable enough to refresh continuity state.\n\nChapter and paragraph prose should keep canon names as plain text. If older story files still contain internal canon links, `upgradeBookRepo()` migrates them during a repo upgrade and normal create/update flows strip them on save.\n\nTypical flow:\n\n```js\nimport {\n  syncAllResumes,\n  syncStoryState,\n  updateParagraph,\n} from \"narrarium\";\n\nawait updateParagraph(\"my-book\", {\n  chapter: \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n  paragraph: \"001-at-the-gate\",\n  body: \"# Scene\\n\\nThe harbor watches before it welcomes.\",\n});\n\nawait syncAllResumes(\"my-book\");\nawait syncStoryState(\"my-book\");\n```\n\nYou can also query the repository semantically:\n\n```js\nimport { queryCanon } from \"narrarium\";\n\nconst result = await queryCanon(\"my-book\", \"What does Lyra know after chapter 4?\");\nconsole.log(result.answer);\nconsole.log(result.sources);\n```\n\nTypical `queryCanon()` prompts:\n\n- `Where is Lyra right now?`\n- `What does Lyra know after chapter 4?`\n- `What is Lyra's relationship with Taren?`\n- `What condition is Lyra in?`\n- `What open loops are still active?`\n- `How does Lyra's relationship with Taren change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `How does Lyra's condition change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `What open loops change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `Who knows this secret?`\n- `When does the brass key first appear?`\n\nFor exact control, `queryCanon()` also accepts `fromChapter`, `toChapter`, and `throughChapter` options.\n\nSee `docs/query-canon.md` for a fuller guide with use cases, scope rules, output fields, and limitations.\n\nYou can also run proposal-only editorial passes on a final paragraph:\n\n```js\nimport { reviseParagraph } from \"narrarium\";\n\nconst revision = await reviseParagraph(\"my-book\", {\n  chapter: \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n  paragraph: \"001-at-the-gate\",\n  mode: \"tension\",\n  intensity: \"medium\",\n});\n\nconsole.log(revision.proposedBody);\nconsole.log(revision.editorialNotes);\nconsole.log(revision.suggestedStateChanges);\n```\n\nSee `docs/revise-paragraph.md` for the modes, continuity behavior, and manual apply workflow.\n\nFor broader chapter-level passes:\n\n```js\nimport { reviseChapter } from \"narrarium\";\n\nconst revision = await reviseChapter(\"my-book\", {\n  chapter: \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n  mode: \"pacing\",\n  intensity: \"medium\",\n});\n\nconsole.log(revision.chapterDiagnosis);\nconsole.log(revision.revisionPlan);\nconsole.log(revision.proposedParagraphs);\n```\n\nSee `docs/revise-chapter.md` for the chapter workflow and output model.\n\nNarrarium also supports explicit chapter-level style overrides with a global book fallback. See `docs/style-profiles.md`.\n\nRecommended `state_changes` keys inside chapter resumes:\n\n- `locations`\n- `knowledge_gain` and `knowledge_loss`\n- `inventory_add` and `inventory_remove`\n- `relationship_updates`\n- `conditions`\n- `wounds`\n- `open_loops_add` and `open_loops_resolved`\n\nSee the root `README.md` and `docs/repository-spec.md` for the full repo model.\n";
const markdown25 = "# create-narrarium-book\n\nStarter CLI for scaffolding and upgrading Narrarium book repositories.\n\nRequires Node.js 22.12 or newer for the bundled Astro reader toolchain.\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install -g create-narrarium-book\n```\n\n## Use\n\n```bash\ncreate-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en\n```\n\nYou can also use the published package directly with `npx`:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en\n```\n\nBy default the starter:\n\n- scaffolds a reader app in `reader/`\n- installs the reader dependencies automatically\n- writes root convenience scripts for `npm run dev`, `npm run build`, `npm run export:epub`, and `npm run doctor`\n- prepares OpenCode config, portable conversation exports, `/resume-book`, and GitHub Pages wiring\n\nThe generated reader starts in spoiler-safe public mode by default. For an author-only or spoiler-friendly atlas, set `NARRARIUM_READER_CANON_MODE=full` in the reader environment before `dev` or `build`.\n\nIf you already know the GitHub Pages custom domain, pass `--pages-domain your-domain.com` to preconfigure the generated reader and Pages workflow.\n\n## Upgrade an existing book repo\n\nRefresh the managed Narrarium scaffolding in an existing repo:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book --upgrade .\n```\n\nIf the repo has a generated reader and you also want to refresh it:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book --upgrade . --with-reader\n```\n\nThe upgrade command creates missing folders, refreshes managed OpenCode and skill files, updates root convenience scripts, and migrates legacy story prose links like `[Mariamne](../../characters/mariamne-ii/)` into plain-text canon mentions so the reader can resolve visible names automatically.\n\nUpgrade also creates `ghostwriters/default.md` and selects it when the current selection is missing or invalid. It never imports old writing-style content into the profile, overwrites an existing profile, or deletes old style files.\n\n## What it creates\n\n- canonical book folders such as `characters/`, `locations/`, `factions/`, `chapters/`, `drafts/`, and `secrets/`\n- `context.md`, `ideas.md`, `story-design.md`, `notes.md`, `promoted.md`, `plot.md`, `ghostwriters/default.md`, `conversations/`, `resumes/`, `evaluations/`, and `research/wikipedia/`\n- `book.md` selects the default ghostwriter; chapter, paragraph, draft, or script frontmatter can select another profile\n- `opencode.jsonc` plus bundled Narrarium skills for OpenCode and Claude, including `instructions` that point to `.github/copilot-instructions.md` so the same book-writing rules are reused by both tools\n- `conversations/README.md` plus automatic OpenCode exports, `RESUME.md`, `CONTINUATION.md`, and a `/resume-book` command for restarting from repo state globally or from a target chapter/paragraph\n- optional sample content\n- standalone Astro reader scaffold with spoiler-safe public mode, live reload while writing, EPUB export, doctor checks, and GitHub Pages workflow\n- root-level convenience scripts that proxy to the generated reader app\n\n## Useful flags\n\n```bash\ncreate-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --sample\ncreate-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --with-reader\ncreate-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --no-reader\ncreate-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --no-install\ncreate-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --pages-domain mybook.com\ncreate-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --with-reader --reader-dir reader\ncreate-narrarium-book --upgrade .\ncreate-narrarium-book --upgrade . --with-reader\n```\n\nRun without arguments for the interactive prompt flow.\n";
const markdown26 = "# narrarium-engine\n\nShared Narrarium engine: the **autopilot**, the **director**, the **simulated readers** and the **personal-server sync**. The same code runs in the Narrarium web app (browser) and in [`narrarium-server`](../server/README.md) (Node.js), so a book behaves identically wherever its autopilot runs.\n\nThe package is isomorphic: it only uses `fetch`, Web Crypto, `AbortController` and `structuredClone`, with no Node or DOM APIs.\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install narrarium-engine\n```\n\n## What it contains\n\n| Area | Main exports |\n|---|---|\n| Book access | `BookStore` (list/read/commit with compare-and-swap, `readMany`), `BookConflictError`, `loadBookSnapshot` |\n| Autopilot | `AutopilotSession`, `AutopilotRun`, `advanceState`, `nextStep`, `applyIntervention`, `summarizeAutopilotState`, `effectivePublish`, `hostDefaultPublish`, `normalizeRunLimits`, `runnerActivity`, `runnerHeartbeatAgeMs`, `AutopilotRuleError` |\n| Director | `askDirector`, `listDirectorOpinions`, `parseDirector`, `serializeDirector`, `directorPrompt` |\n| Readers | `readerPersonaSystemPrompt`, `mergeReaderPersonas`, `READER_EVALUATION_TOOL`, `chapterReaderText` |\n| Ghostwriters | `parseGhostwriter`, `ghostwriterPrompt` |\n| Markdown | `parseMarkdown` (with `error`), `splitFrontmatter`, `stripFrontmatter`, `renderMarkdown`, `FrontmatterError` |\n| Text | `clipMiddle`, `truncate`, `tail`, `countWords`, `languageCode`, `tokensPerWord`, `scaleOutputTokens`, `sceneCharacterLimit` |\n| AI | `AiPort` (the only door to language models), `createFetchAi` (OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, GitHub Models), `buildChatRequest`, `resolveAiCandidates`, `pickAiRuntimeConfig`, `effectiveAiBase`, `redactSecrets` |\n| Personal server | `NarrariumServerClient`, `pairWithServer`, `pairWithServerDetailed`, `verifyServerIdentity`, `serverCompatibility`, `remoteBookStore`, protocol types (scopes, pairing, updates), `ServerSentEventParser` |\n| Sync | `planFileSync`, `syncBookStores` (three-way sync of two book stores, with the files it leaves out in `skipped`) |\n| Testing | `narrarium-engine/testing`: `MemoryBookStore`, `seedBookFiles`, `createFakeAutopilotAi` |\n\n## Running the autopilot\n\nA host provides two adapters: a `BookStore` over the book files and an `AiPort` over its AI configuration.\n\n```ts\nimport { AutopilotSession, createFetchAi, pickAiRuntimeConfig } from \"narrarium-engine\";\n\nconst session = new AutopilotSession({\n  bookId: \"my-book\",\n  store,                                  // your BookStore\n  ai: createFetchAi(pickAiRuntimeConfig(settings)),\n  host: \"server\",\n  hostLabel: \"my laptop\",\n});\n\nsession.subscribe((event) => console.log(event.type, event));\nawait session.start({\n  brief: { idea: \"A harbor town built on glass hides a lie.\", targetChapters: 12 },\n  options: { pauseAfterPlan: true },\n});\nawait session.waitForIdle();\n```\n\nEvery step is one atomic commit of the book files plus `autopilot/state.json` and `autopilot/activity.json`. A step whose files changed in the meantime (for example because the author edited them) is never overwritten: it is repeated on the latest text.\n\n### Session options\n\n| Option | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `bookId`, `store`, `ai`, `host`, `hostLabel` | Required: the book, its `BookStore`, the `AiPort`, `\"browser\"` or `\"server\"`, and the name the author sees. |\n| `hostId` | Stable identity of the host (device or server id). Without it the label identifies the host. |\n| `ownRunnerAlive` | Whether a run recorded by this same host is alive outside this session (another tab). Without it such a run is taken for interrupted. |\n| `hooks` | `beforeStep(signal)`, `afterCommit({ task, message, paths })` and `afterChapter({ chapterNumber, title })`, for a host that syncs. Which of the last two is called, and when, is decided by the engine from where the book is published: see *Quick controls → Publishing*. |\n| `defaultPublish` | Where the host publishes when the author has not chosen (`options.publishEvery`): `\"step\"`, `\"chapter\"` or `\"off\"`. Without it a server host publishes after every step, as it always did, and a browser host as `options.syncAfterChapter` says. |\n| `now`, `maxEvents` | The clock, and how many live events are kept for late subscribers (400). |\n| `retryDelayMs` | The base delay of the retries of a failed step (4000 ms): see *Retries*. `0` makes them instant. |\n| `random` | Source of the retry jitter, in [0, 1) (default `Math.random`). |\n| `heartbeatMs` | While a run is active the session emits a `state` event this often (default 30 000 ms), with the run's heartbeat in memory; nothing is saved for it. `0` switches it off. |\n| `persistHeartbeatMs` | The longest silence of `autopilot/state.json` during a run (default 300 000 ms): after it the run saves a fresh `runner.heartbeatAt`, so that another host does not take a long step for a dead run. `0` switches it off. |\n\nA run whose heartbeat is older than `RUNNER_STALE_MINUTES` (20) is taken for dead. `runnerActivity(state)` says `\"none\"`, `\"active\"` or `\"stale\"`, and `runnerHeartbeatAgeMs(state)` how old the heartbeat is, in milliseconds, for a state and for the summary of a live `state` event (`null` when nobody runs).\n\n### Pipeline\n\n1. **Concept** — story design, world context, characters, locations, factions and secrets (skipped when the book already has a design or prose).\n2. **Summaries of the author's chapters** — only for a book with chapters written by the author: see *Chapters the author wrote*.\n3. **Plan** — chapter plan; simulated readers react to it and the director approves it or asks for changes. The run can pause here for the author.\n4. **Per chapter** — scene plan, prose scene by scene (streamed), then the review cycle:\n   - blind simulated readers (no canon, no secrets) and a panel summary;\n   - specialists (continuity, canon, secrets, timeline, voice, style, pacing) with evidence anchored in the text;\n   - the **director** accepts or rejects every finding; the software, not the model, derives the repairs;\n   - accepted fixes are applied; a new round verifies them, up to the director's round limit;\n   - consolidation: chapter summary, recap, story so far, `plot.md`.\n5. **Final** — the director's judgement on the whole book.\n\n### Author interventions\n\n`session.intervene({ scope, text, interrupt? })`:\n\n- `next_step` — delivered once to the next writing step;\n- `from_now` — a permanent rule for every following step, the director included;\n- `rewrite` — the director picks the written scenes that must change; they are rewritten and reviewed again.\n\nWith `interrupt: true` the running step is abandoned and repeated with the new instruction. An intervention is saved at once, also while a step runs.\n\nPermanent rules (`from_now`) are edited with `session.updateRule(index, text, expectedText?)` and `session.removeRule(index, expectedText?)`; `expectedText` is the rule as the author saw it, and a rule that changed in the meantime is never overwritten. A rule that cannot be saved throws `AutopilotRuleError`, whose `code` is `rule_empty`, `rule_too_long` (a rule is at most `MAX_RULE_CHARACTERS`, 800, because it goes into every request; it is refused, never cut), `rule_not_found`, `rule_changed` or `rule_duplicate`. The author's answer to a question of the director (`QuestionAnswer`) is a note used once; with `asRule: true` it becomes a permanent rule instead (never the default).\n\n### Quick controls\n\nWhat the web app's quick-command bar uses: pause at the end of the chapter, the limits of one run, where the book is published, and the settings that can change while a run goes on. They live here, once, so the browser and the personal server behave the same.\n\n**Pausing.** `session.pause(mode)` takes `\"now\"` (the request in flight is abandoned), `\"after-step\"` (the default: the step in flight finishes and is saved, then the run pauses) or `\"after-chapter\"` (the step in flight finishes and the run goes on to the end of the chapter in progress, its closing and the publishing of the chapter included, then pauses with `Chapter N completed: paused, as you asked.`, in the language of the book). It answers whether there was a run, or a start in flight, to ask.\n\n- A chapter is *in progress* from the moment its scenes are planned until it is closed. With none in progress (the concept, the plan, the summaries of the author's chapters, the first step of a chapter that has not started, the moment between two chapters) it is `\"after-step\"`, and so is a wait before the retry of such a step; the wait before the retry of a step of the chapter goes on, because the chapter still has to be finished. A request made at the boundary, right after a chapter was closed, pauses before the next one starts. After the last step of the book the run is complete, and says so.\n- The stricter request counts, whatever the order: `\"now\"`, then `\"after-step\"`, then `\"after-chapter\"`. A request made while `start()` is in flight is kept for the run.\n- `session.cancelPause()` takes back a pause that is waiting (`\"after-step\"` or `\"after-chapter\"`) without stopping anything, and answers whether there was one. A `\"now\"` that happened cannot be taken back. `AutopilotRun` has the same: `stopAfterChapter()`, `cancelPause()` and `pausePending`.\n- When the run ends for it, `run-ended` carries `code: \"chapterPause\"`. If the option `pauseAfterEachChapter` would pause at the same moment, the request that was just made is the one that is said.\n\n**Limits of one run.** `session.start({ limits: { steps, chapters } })` pauses the run after that many steps saved, or chapters completed, by this run. Nothing of it is saved: the next run has no limit unless it asks. A value that is not a whole number above zero is no limit (`normalizeRunLimits`). A step that fails or is abandoned does not count; with `steps: 1` a run makes one step (also one of the plan, or the final judgement) and stops, so three of them make three steps and three commits. `chapters` is the same limit as the saved option `chaptersPerRun`: the stricter of the two counts. A limit never takes the place of a reason the book has to stop (a plan that waits for the author, a question, a complete book), and the pause the author asked for is the one reported when both are due at once. `run-ended` carries `code: \"stepLimit\"` or `code: \"chapterLimit\"`.\n\n**Publishing.** `options.publishEvery` is `\"step\"` (after every saved step, and again when a chapter is closed, so that the chapter is published whole), `\"chapter\"` (when a chapter is closed) or `\"off\"` (never). It is saved with the other options and can change while a run goes on. `effectivePublish(options, defaultPublish)` is the one rule that says where the book is published: `options.publishEvery`, else the host's `defaultPublish`, else the older option `options.syncAfterChapter` (`\"chapter\"` when it is on, `\"off\"` when it is off), so the files of an older engine behave as they did. A host that passes no `defaultPublish` gets `hostDefaultPublish(host)`: a `\"server\"` host publishes after every step (its `afterCommit` hook has always been called then), a `\"browser\"` host has none, so `syncAfterChapter` decides.\n\nThe engine applies it, so a host only says *how* to publish: `hooks.afterCommit` is called after every saved step when the way is `\"step\"`; `hooks.afterChapter` when a chapter is closed and the way is `\"chapter\"` or `\"step\"`; neither when it is `\"off\"`. A host that publishes in every way needs both hooks. The way is read at the end of every step and of every chapter, from the state that step saved. A publish that fails is reported as before (a `sync` event with `syncFailed`, or `publishFailed` for a chapter) and never stops the run. `beforeStep` is not a publish: it is called before every step whatever the way.\n\n**Changing settings during a run.** `session.updateOptions(partial)` saves the options and answers the saved state. While a run is active only `publishEvery` (`null` takes the choice back, so that the host decides again) and `pauseAfterEachChapter` can change (`LIVE_AUTOPILOT_OPTIONS`); any other option throws `AutopilotBusyError`, as `configure` does, and nothing of that request is applied. With no run active any option can change. The change is a compare-and-swap commit, like an intervention: the step in flight is neither failed nor repeated, the run takes the author's value into the state the step saves (`reconcileAuthorChanges`) and reads it at the end of that step, or of the chapter. A value nobody knows changes nothing, and a request that changes nothing writes nothing.\n\n**What the summary says.** `AutopilotStateSummary`, which the `state` events carry and `snapshot().summary` holds for a client that connects later, has three fields that are never saved: `pausePending` (`\"after-step\"`, `\"after-chapter\"` or `null`), `limits` (`{ steps?, chapters?, stepsDone, chaptersDone }` of the run in progress, `null` when it has none or is over; `chapters` is the stricter of the run's limit and `chaptersPerRun`) and `publish` (`effectivePublish` of the options and the host's default). A summary made from the state file alone (`summarizeAutopilotState(state)`) has no `pausePending` and no `limits`: nothing in the file says them, so a client takes them from the events and the snapshot. They change without the state being written, so `updatedAt` does not move: every change of the pause that waits, and every saved step, is a `state` event, and a client keeps the newest event it has seen, whatever its `updatedAt`.\n\n**Message codes.** `run-ended` and `insight` events carry a `code` when a client can say it in its own language: `chapterPause`, `stepLimit` and `chapterLimit` join `takenOver` and the others (`AutopilotMessageCode`). The `message` of the event, and of the saved state, is always in the language of the book.\n\n### Long books\n\nThere is no maximum number of chapters. A plan longer than `PLAN_BATCH_CHAPTERS` (20) is made of several `plan` steps, one per batch, so that no reply outgrows the model's output; `plan.revisionBatches` lists the first chapter of every batch a revision of the plan still has to regenerate, and `plan.skippedReaders` the readers whose reaction stayed incomplete after a retry and who are not asked again. The lists of chapters in a prompt are windowed around the chapter at hand (`outline`, at most `OUTLINE_MAX_CHARS`, 24 000 characters), so a prompt never outgrows its budget and the work to build it grows linearly with the book.\n\n### Chapters the author wrote\n\nA chapter written before the autopilot took over has no summary unless the author wrote one, and without it the story so far of the chapter that follows would start blind. Before anything is planned or written after them, the planner adds one `summarize-imported` step for every such chapter without a `summary`: the model of the `simple-tasks` route summarizes it in two to four sentences, and the summary is saved in its `chapter.md` (created when there is only a folder of scenes) and in its resume file when it has none. A chapter that already has a summary, in its file or in the plan, is skipped and its file is not touched; the plan learns the summaries the files have. One chapter is one step and one commit, with a title and a reason in the language of the book, so it respects pause and budget like any other step, is repeated safely and resumed where it stopped, and is never asked again once the summary is saved. A finished book is not summarized.\n\n## The director\n\nThe director is the author's stand-in: it approves plans and chapters during the autopilot and can be asked at any time what it thinks of the book, a chapter or a scene:\n\n```ts\nimport { askDirector } from \"narrarium-engine\";\n\nconst { opinion } = await askDirector({ store, ai, target: { kind: \"chapter\", chapter: \"003-the-storm\" }, question: \"Is the ending strong enough?\", save: true });\n```\n\nIts profile lives in `director.md` at the book root (persona, autonomy, strictness, approval score, review rounds, priorities, taste, red lines, simulated reader weights and a free vision). Saved opinions go to `evaluations/director/`.\n\nValues are read as the author wrote them: an autonomy whatever its case (`Full`, `ask on critical`), numbers with their decimals (`approval_score: 7.5`, a reader weight of `0.4`) and a decimal comma (`'9,5'`).\n\n### Profiles that cannot be read\n\n`parseDirector`, `parseGhostwriter` and `parseReaderPersona` never throw and never hide a problem: they return `warnings: string[]` (empty when the file is as it says), each naming the file. A warning is YAML that cannot be read (with its line), a value that is not understood (an autonomy nobody knows, a score that is not a number, a list that is not a list, a reader type that is none of `standard`, `genre`, `custom`, a switch that is neither yes nor no), and the profile then has the default for that value, as it always had. The defaults themselves carry `warnings: []`.\n\nA profile with warnings is not what the author wrote, so the engine never goes on with it silently and never replaces the file with the defaults:\n\n- before a step, the autopilot checks the director, the files of the simulated readers when readers are used, and the ghostwriter of the chapter for a step that writes. When one has warnings it spends nothing, emits an insight with code `profileProblems` and stops with `status: \"waiting-author\"` and a `message` that lists the problems (in the language of the book); the author corrects the file and resumes.\n- `askDirector` still gives its opinion, and reports the problems of `director.md` in `warnings` next to it.\n- a host that saves a profile should not do it over a file that has warnings.\n\n## Files in the book\n\n| Path | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `director.md` | Director profile (`type: director`). |\n| `autopilot/state.json` | Autopilot state: brief, options, plan, review cycle, rules, interventions, statistics. |\n| `autopilot/activity.json` | Last 200 activities (what, why, next, result, model, tokens, cost). |\n| `autopilot/plan.md` | Human-readable mirror of the plan. |\n| `chapters/<chapter>/chapter.md` | The chapter, with its `summary` (written when it is closed, or by the summary step for a chapter the author wrote). |\n| `resumes/` | `total.md` (one section per chapter) and `chapters/<chapter>.md` (the recap of each one). |\n| `evaluations/director/` | Director opinions (`book.md`, `chapters/<chapter>.md`, `paragraphs/<chapter>/<paragraph>.md`). |\n| `evaluations/readers/` | Simulated reader evaluations, in the same format as the web app. |\n\n## AI tasks\n\nThe engine asks the host for these router tasks (capabilities): `autopilot` (concept, plan, scenes, prose, repairs), `director`, `reader-evaluation`, `reader-evaluation-summary`, `review` (specialists) and `simple-tasks` (summaries of scenes and of the chapters the author wrote). Missing routes fall back to models tagged `default`.\n\n### What the models read of a scene\n\nProse that is judged or summarized (the specialists, the director's review of a chapter, the consolidation, the director's opinion on a scene or a chapter, the summary of a chapter the author wrote) is cut, when it is too long, in the middle: `clipMiddle(text, max, { head: 0.6 })` keeps the start and the end, joined by `[…]` (on a line of its own when the text has several lines), at a word and never inside a character. The limit comes from the book: `sceneCharacterLimit(wordsPerScene)` is ten characters a word (as much as the longest words of any script written with spaces) and half as much again, so a scene of the length the brief asks for is never clipped; the fixed limits these prompts always had stay as the least a prompt keeps, so no scene is cut that was not cut before. A scene within the limit reaches the model exactly as it is written. The ending the next scene is continued from is the real end of the previous one, however long it is.\n\n### Secrets in a prompt\n\n`secretRules(store, snapshot, chapterNumber?)` lists the secrets with the rule that holds for the chapter (`HIDDEN until chapter N`, `The reveal is planned in THIS chapter`, `Already revealable…`, or `No reveal threshold`) and, for each, its truth. A book with few secrets gets all of them, exactly as ever. A list over `SECRET_RULES_MAX_CHARS` (24 000 characters) is windowed like a list of chapters: secrets revealed more than `SECRET_WINDOW_CHAPTERS` (30) chapters before drop out first, then the truths and the long wording; what the chapter must not reveal (a reveal still to come, or none planned) and the reveal of the chapter itself stay as long as anything can, the nearest chapters first, and the last line says how many are not listed. Only the files of the secrets that are listed with their truth are read, so a book of thousands of secrets costs the same as a small one.\n\n### Languages\n\nLimits on the reply of a step are sized for Latin script (2.4 tokens a word) and grown for the language of the book: `tokensPerWord(language)` gives the factor of the language (by code, name or region: `ru`, `русский`, `zh-CN`), `UNKNOWN_TOKENS_PER_WORD` (6) for one the engine does not know, and `scaleOutputTokens` applies it up to `OUTPUT_TOKENS_CEILING`. Words are counted by `countWords` for every script, with `Intl.Segmenter` where a language has no spaces.\n\n## The AI client\n\n`createFetchAi(config, options)` is the one AI client of every host: the browser and the personal server give it the same `AiRuntimeConfig` (`pickAiRuntimeConfig(settings)`) and get the same requests and the same outcomes. A host adds only what is its own, through `options`:\n\n| Option | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `fetch` | The `fetch` to use (default the global one). |\n| `githubModelsBaseUrl` | Origin of GitHub Models, or of a proxy that forwards `/inference`. |\n| `defaultTimeoutMs` | Timeout for an integration that has none (default `DEFAULT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS`). |\n| `onAttempt`, `onAttemptEnd` | Hooks that see every attempt that is sent and how it ended (below). |\n| `acknowledgeFallback` | Asked before content goes to an integration of another account than the one it went to so far (below). |\n\nWhat every host gets, whatever its options:\n\n| What | How |\n|---|---|\n| Request | `buildChatRequest(candidate, request, { githubModelsBaseUrl })` is the only place a request is built: URL, headers, body, streaming, tools or JSON-in-prompt, and the output limit parameter of the model family. Pure and exported, so hosts can test their configuration against it. |\n| Output limit | A structured request gets at least `STRUCTURED_OUTPUT_FLOOR` (4096) tokens. A model known to reason by name (o1/o3/o4, gpt-5, codex-mini; for Azure the declared underlying model, else the deployment name) gets `REASONING_OUTPUT_MARGIN` (12 000) more, because its reasoning is counted in the limit. Never above the model's own `maxOutputTokens`. An Azure deployment of unknown identity gets `max_completion_tokens` from API version 2024-09-01, `max_tokens` before. |\n| Prompt size | A candidate whose `maxInputTokens` (context window) cannot hold the prompt (estimated with `likelyMessageTokens`, the tool schema included, against `resolveModelTokenBudgets`: window minus the output reserve minus a 5% margin) is skipped. If no candidate can, `AiPromptTooLargeError` (not retryable) gives the estimate and the largest budget. A prompt is never cut. |\n| Privacy of fallbacks | `fallbackDisclosure.sameBoundaryOnly` excludes fallbacks outside the primary's integration (the boundary). `requireAcknowledgement` asks `options.acknowledgeFallback(from, to, { capability, label })` before content goes to an integration it has not gone to: only `true` allows it, a throw cancels the call, and a host without the hook (the server) never crosses (fail closed). The engine asks at every call that needs the crossing (once per pair of accounts within a call), so a host that remembers acknowledgements answers from its memory. When nothing is left to call, the error names the setting and how to change it. |\n| Routing limits | `routingExecution`: `maxCandidates`, `maxTotalDurationMs`, `maxTokenAttempts`, `maxEstimatedCost`. The total time never interrupts the first candidate before its own timeout; later candidates start only while time remains and run for what remains. Tokens and cost are estimated per attempt from the real prompt and the output limit (reasoning margin included): candidates are kept while the sum fits, and a first attempt that alone exceeds a limit gives `AiRoutingBudgetError`. A configuration without these fields (saved by an older host) has no such limits and no privacy policy. |\n| Timeout | An integration's `requestTimeoutMs`, else `options.defaultTimeoutMs`, else `DEFAULT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS` (600 s), in every host. |\n| Hooks | `onAttempt(attempt)`: `attemptId`, the effective `messages` (with the JSON instruction a model without tools receives), the output limit, `stream`, `tools`, `endpoint` and `timeoutMs`. `onAttemptEnd(end)`: the same `attemptId`, `outcome` (`ok`, `failed`, `cancelled`), `usage`, `cost`, `usageKnown`, `costKnown`, the reply `text` or the `error` (without credentials), `failureKind` (`timeout`, `cancelled`, `provider`), `status`, `retryAfterMs` and `durationMs`, for every attempt that was sent, failed ones included. A hook that throws never breaks a call. |\n| Approving endpoints | `effectiveAiBase(integration, githubModelsBaseUrl?)` is the origin and path every request of an integration starts with, defaults included (`https://api.openai.com/v1`, `https://models.github.ai/inference`). A host that approves where API keys may be sent should compare these. |\n| Wire format | Server-sent events with several `data:` lines are joined with a newline; a JSON reply to a streaming request (a server that ignores `stream`) is read as the reply; an `error` in a 200 reply is a provider error. |\n| Errors | `AiProviderError` carries `status`, `retryable`, `transient` (it depends on the moment: 408, 429, 502, 503, 504, network), `retryAfterMs` (the `Retry-After` of a 429 or 503, at most five minutes), `timedOut`, `truncated` and `reasoningOnly`. Keys, `Bearer …`, `api-key: …`, `?key=…` and `sk-…` are removed from every message (`redactSecrets`), also before the runner saves it. |\n\n### Costs and budgets\n\n`estimateCost` bills cached tokens like normal input unless the model has a `cachedPerMTok`. A reply carries `usageKnown` and `costKnown`: when the provider reports no tokens, `usage` is an estimate (`usageKnown: false`, `costKnown: false`); a model without a price has no `cost`. The autopilot counts those calls in `stats.usageUnknownCalls` and `stats.costUnknownCalls`, says once per run that the money budget (`options.budgetLimit`) cannot apply to a model without a price (`budgetNotApplicable`), and `options.budgetTokens` stops the run at a number of tokens instead (0 = off).\n\n### Retries\n\nA step that failed with a transient provider error is retried up to three times, after about 4 s, 12 s and 36 s (with jitter, never sooner than the provider's `Retry-After`); the `failureRetry` insight carries `attempt`, `retries` and `waitMs`. A rejected reply, or a failure that is not the provider's, is retried once, the first with the reason. The same failure twice in a row that does not depend on the moment (an empty reply, a 500 with the same message) stops the run at once. A reply cut while the model had only reasoned is retried once with an output limit three times larger (`AiRequest.outputBoost`), and the next steps of that kind start with it. `retryDelayMs` is the base delay: every delay scales with it, `0` makes them instant. A pause ends the wait.\n\n### What the model's replies must be\n\n- A forced tool call must carry at least one of the schema's required fields (else one of its declared properties); otherwise the JSON in the text is used, and a reply with neither is rejected and retried once with the reason.\n- `<think>` blocks are dropped, and so is everything up to the last `</think>` of a model that never opened it.\n- What the engine escapes in prompts (`&amp;`, `&lt;`, `&gt;`) is un-escaped in prose and in every text field of a structured reply (`unescapePromptData`).\n- The director decides with a score: the reply must carry `verdict` and `score` (0-10; above 10 and up to 100 is read as a percentage). An `approve` below the `approvalScore` of `director.md` counts as `revise`, with the reason in the record, for the plan, every chapter, the final review and `askDirector`.\n- A simulated reader must answer with a score and its reaction. A reader whose reply is still incomplete after the retry is skipped (and said so): the review goes on with those who answered.\n\n## Sync\n\n`syncBookStores({ local, remote, base })` is a three-way sync of two `BookStore`s (the browser's working copy and the personal server's): it copies what changed on one side, reports what changed on both (`conflicts`, settled by `resolveConflict`) and returns the `base` to remember for the next one. It reads from a store that has `readMany` a batch of paths at a time, and commits in chunks of at most 400 changes and 24 MiB, so a book of any size passes the limits of one request.\n\nWhat it does not move is reported, never silently ignored and never a reason for the sync to fail. `result.skipped` lists, in path order, each file with its `reason`:\n\n| Reason | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `too-large` | The file is over `maxFileBytes` (`SYNC_MAX_FILE_BYTES`, 8 MiB, what the personal server accepts), with its `size` in bytes; or a store refused to send it because it is too much (HTTP 413), without a `size`. The size is checked before the file goes into a commit: a store may list `size` in its `BookFileEntry`, and then the file is not even read. |\n| `unsafe-path` | A path that is not a safe repository path (`../x.md`, `.git/…`, absolute): the same paths as `rejected`. |\n| `not-text` | A file that is not text (binary assets are left to the hosts) and is not the same on both sides. |\n\nA skipped file is not recorded as synced (a file that was never synced stays out of `base`, one that was keeps the version the other side has), so the next sync says it again until it can be moved; the deletion of a file is never skipped.\n\n## The personal server client\n\n`NarrariumServerClient` is the typed client of the personal server API, shared by the web app and the CLI (see the [server README](../server/README.md)).\n\n- **Version compatibility.** `serverCompatibility(info)` tells whether the server speaks an API this engine supports (`MIN_SUPPORTED_SERVER_API` to `MAX_SUPPORTED_SERVER_API`, both 1 today): `{ status: \"ok\" | \"server-older\" | \"server-newer\", message }`. A server that does not say which API it speaks is an older one. It never throws; `verifyServerIdentity` returns it as `compatibility`, and so does `client.verifiedInfo()` in `identity`, so the host decides how to show it (update the server, or update the app).\n- **Pairing.** `pairWithServer(options)` still answers the token. `pairWithServerDetailed(options)` answers `{ token, verification?, requestVerification?, compatibility? }`, and `onWaitingApproval({ verification?, compatibility? })` is called while the owner approves. The verification is four digits the server derives (see the server README): while the request waits, the admin panel shows the same four digits next to it (`requestVerification`, which `onWaitingApproval` receives as `verification`), so the owner can tell this device's request from another one; once approved, the panel shows the digits of the token next to the client (`verification` of the result). `onPaired(pairing)` hands over the same details to a host that keeps calling `pairWithServer`. An older server that sends none is fine; a value that is not four digits is ignored.\n- **Signing out.** `client.signOut()` revokes the token the client holds (`DELETE /api/v1/session`) and no other: `{ revoked: true }`, or `{ revoked: false }` when the token was already revoked. A server that cannot be reached is an error, because the token may still be valid there (`forgetSession()` is the older call and reports a revoked token as an error).\n- **Quick controls.** Pause at the end of the chapter, the limits of one run and the settings that can change during a run (see *Quick controls* above) go through three calls. A server that implements them announces `SERVER_FEATURE_AUTOPILOT_CONTROLS` (`\"autopilot-controls\"`) in the `features` of `GET /api/v1/info`, and `serverSupportsAutopilotControls(info)` reads it (never throws; an older server, or an odd answer, says no). Ask before sending what an older server would read as something else: it ignores `limits` (the run would not stop after the step or the chapter that was asked for, and goes on spending) and takes every pause mode but `now` for `after-step` (a `cancel` would pause). The calls need the `run` permission, like the other commands that steer a run, answer the snapshot (`AutopilotSnapshot`, with the server session's `summary`: `pausePending`, `limits`, `publish`), and fail as `{ \"code\", \"message\" }` with the status below, which `ServerRequestError` carries as `status` and `code`. Every one also answers 401 `unauthorized`, 403 `scope` (no `run` permission) and 404 `book_not_found`.\n\n  | Client method | Request | Answer |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `pauseAutopilot(bookId, mode)` | `POST /api/v1/books/:id/autopilot/pause` with `{ \"mode\": \"now\" \\| \"after-step\" \\| \"after-chapter\" \\| \"cancel\" }`. A body without `mode` is `after-step`, as it always was. | `200` the snapshot. `cancel` with nothing waiting, and any mode with nothing running, are `200` too: the summary's `pausePending` says what is waiting now. `400 invalid_request` for a `mode` that is none of the four, or a body that is not a JSON object. |\n  | `startAutopilot(bookId, input)` | `POST /api/v1/books/:id/autopilot/start` with the body it always had (`brief`, `options`, `force`) and `limits?: { \"steps\"?: number, \"chapters\"?: number }`. | `200` the snapshot. `400 invalid_request` when `limits` is present and is not a JSON object. The server hands the numbers to the engine as they are (a value that is not a whole number above zero is no limit) and does not save them. The other answers are as before: `409 busy`, `412 ai_not_configured`, `429 too_many_runs`, `503 maintenance`. |\n  | `updateAutopilotOptions(bookId, input)`, also `updateOptions` | `PUT /api/v1/books/:id/autopilot/options` with `{ \"publishEvery\"?: \"off\" \\| \"chapter\" \\| \"step\" \\| null, \"pauseAfterEachChapter\"?: boolean }`: at least one of the two, and nothing else. `null` takes the choice back, so that the server's default decides again. | `200` the snapshot, of the state that was saved (as `configure` answers), also while the autopilot runs: the step in flight is not repeated, and the run reads the new value at the end of the step, or of the chapter. `400 invalid_request` for a body that is not a JSON object, has none of the two keys or has another key, or a value of the wrong type (`publishEvery` that is not one of the four, `pauseAfterEachChapter` that is not a boolean). `409 conflict` when the state file kept changing under the write (try again) and `409 state_corrupt` when it cannot be read; `503 maintenance` while the server is about to restart. |\n\n  The server answers with the engine's `session.pause`, `session.cancelPause`, `session.start({ …, limits })` and `session.updateOptions`, and takes `defaultPublish: \"step\"` as its default (which is what it does when it passes none). It can validate the bodies against the lists the engine exports for that: `PAUSE_AUTOPILOT_MODES`, `AUTOPILOT_PUBLISH_MODES` and `LIVE_AUTOPILOT_OPTIONS`. For a GitHub book its `afterCommit` hook pushes (what the engine calls after every step in `step` mode) and its `afterChapter` hook pushes too (what it calls when a chapter is closed in `chapter` and `step` mode): without that one, `chapter` would publish nothing.\n\n## Tests\n\n```bash\nnpm test -w narrarium-engine\n```\n";
const markdown27 = "# narrarium-mcp-server\n\nLocal MCP server for Narrarium book repositories.\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install narrarium-mcp-server\n```\n\n## Run\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-mcp-server\n```\n\nIf the package is already installed in the project or globally, the binary is:\n\n```bash\nnarrarium-mcp\n```\n\n## Bootstrap help\n\nThe server exposes `setup_framework`, which returns the exact `npx` commands to start a new Narrarium project from scratch.\n\nTypical starter command:\n\n```bash\nnpx create-narrarium-book my-book --title \"My Book\" --language en --with-reader\n```\n\n## Public HTTP mode\n\nYou can also run the public HTTP version locally:\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev:http\n```\n\nThat serves:\n\n- `/mcp`\n- `/health`\n\n## Vercel deployment\n\nThe repository root includes a Vercel-ready API route at `api/mcp.ts`.\n\nThe package also includes `packages/mcp-server/api/` and `packages/mcp-server/vercel.json`, so a Vercel project rooted at `packages/mcp-server` can deploy the same public MCP surface without requiring a separate static build output setup.\n\nUse the Vercel deployment for:\n\n- `setup_framework`\n- `repository_spec`\n- `wikipedia_search`\n- `wikipedia_page`\n\nUse the local stdio server for actual filesystem writing and repo mutations.\n\nWhen a matching `research/wikipedia/` snapshot already exists, Narrarium now reuses it before fetching Wikipedia again. Use `forceWikipediaRefresh` to bypass reuse, or `maxWikipediaSnapshotAgeDays` to refresh old snapshots automatically.\n\n## GitHub Actions and Vercel\n\nThe repository includes `.github/workflows/deploy-vercel-mcp.yml`.\n\nTo enable it, add this GitHub repository secret:\n\n- `VERCEL_TOKEN`\n\nThe workflow already targets:\n\n- `VERCEL_ORG_ID=team_Jut3umUxSp1D1Z1sqmv4iUjc`\n- `VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=prj_b9bxn0P4ilMmG40DiZoZ057qA1Zj`\n\nProduction endpoint target:\n\n- `https://narrarium.space/mcp`\n\nIf you want local image generation through `generate_asset_image`, also set:\n\n- `OPENAI_API_KEY`\n\n## Typical usage\n\nPoint OpenCode or another MCP client at the built server and use tools such as:\n\n- `init_book_repo`\n- `create_character`\n- `create_location`\n- `create_chapter`\n- `create_paragraph`\n- `create_asset_prompt`\n- `register_asset`\n- `generate_asset_image`\n- `update_chapter`\n- `update_paragraph`\n- `query_canon`\n- `revise_chapter`\n- `revise_paragraph`\n- `rename_entity`\n- `rename_chapter`\n- `rename_paragraph`\n- `start_wizard` / `wizard_answer` / `wizard_finalize`\n- `sync_all_resumes`\n- `evaluate_chapter`\n- `evaluate_paragraph`\n- `sync_story_state`\n- `evaluate_book`\n\n## Continuity workflow\n\nNarrarium now keeps continuity in two layers:\n\n- `resumes/` for human-readable chapter and book summaries\n- `state/` for structured continuity snapshots used by context builders and diagnostics\n\nWhen you create, rename, promote, or rewrite final chapter or paragraph files through MCP:\n\n- `plot.md`, chapter resumes, and `resumes/total.md` are refreshed automatically\n- `state/status.md` is marked dirty\n- the tool response reminds you to run `sync_story_state` manually\n\nThat manual sync regenerates:\n\n- `state/current.md`\n- `state/chapters/*.md`\n- `state/status.md`\n\nThe structured input for that sync lives in chapter resume frontmatter under `state_changes`.\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n1. Use `update_paragraph`, `update_chapter`, `create_*_from_draft`, or rename tools.\n2. Review the refreshed resume files.\n3. Add or refine `state_changes` in the affected `resumes/chapters/<slug>.md` file.\n4. Run `sync_story_state` when the rewrite is stable.\n\nRecommended `state_changes` keys:\n\n- `locations`\n- `knowledge_gain` and `knowledge_loss`\n- `inventory_add` and `inventory_remove`\n- `relationship_updates`\n- `conditions`\n- `wounds`\n- `open_loops_add` and `open_loops_resolved`\n\n`query_canon` sits on top of this stack and answers natural-language questions such as:\n\n- `Where is Lyra right now?`\n- `What does Lyra know after chapter 4?`\n- `What is Lyra's relationship with Taren?`\n- `What condition is Lyra in?`\n- `What open loops are still active?`\n- `How does Lyra's relationship with Taren change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `How does Lyra's condition change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `What open loops change between chapter 3 and chapter 8?`\n- `Who knows this secret?`\n- `When does the brass key first appear?`\n\nYou can also pass `fromChapter`, `toChapter`, or `throughChapter` directly when you need exact scope control instead of relying on question parsing.\n\nSee `docs/query-canon.md` for a fuller guide with use cases, scope rules, output fields, and limitations.\n\n`revise_paragraph` is the editorial counterpart to `update_paragraph`: it proposes a revision for a final scene, does not write files, and can suggest `state_changes` review if the scene touches continuity-sensitive beats. The intended flow is proposal first, user confirmation second, apply with `update_paragraph` third.\n\nSee `docs/revise-paragraph.md` for modes, examples, and the manual apply workflow.\n\n`review_dialogue_action_beats` is the beat-by-beat counterpart for dialogue scenes: it reviews action beats around spoken lines, judges whether they are purposeful, can suggest stronger beats or a simple `said` fallback, and returns a preview without writing files. Apply only confirmed beat choices with `apply_dialogue_action_beats`.\n\n`revise_chapter` is the broader version: it proposes a diagnosis, a revision plan, and scene-by-scene suggestions for a final chapter without mutating repository files.\n\nSee `docs/revise-chapter.md` for the chapter-level workflow and output structure.\n\nChapter and paragraph writing context loads the selected `ghostwriters/<slug>.md` profile, including its frontmatter style and punctuation fields and its body. Selection resolves from paragraph, chapter, then `book.md`, while story context remains scoped to that point.\n\n`resume_book_context` also accepts optional `chapter` and `paragraph` parameters when you want to restart from a specific writing point instead of the latest overall state.\n\nUse `save_book_item` and `save_chapter_item` for structured active ideas and notes, `promote_book_item` and `promote_chapter_item` to archive promoted items out of the active queues, and `update_book_notes` / `update_chapter_notes` when you want to edit the supporting documents themselves.\n\nEvaluation tools now combine objective text signals with an editorial reading against each scope's selected ghostwriter profile, plus canon coherence checks against characters, locations, factions, items, and timeline references when they are present on the page. The saved evaluation files expose both objective and editorial scores, a weighted verdict, recommended focus, and a dedicated `Why the weighted verdict landed here` section.\n\nSee `docs/style-profiles.md` for the chapter style workflow.\n\n## Practical asset examples\n\n| Use case | Subject | Asset path |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Book cover | `book` | `assets/book/cover.*` |\n| Character portrait | `character:lyra-vale` | `assets/characters/lyra-vale/primary.*` |\n| Chapter art | `chapter:001-the-arrival` | `assets/chapters/001-the-arrival/primary.*` |\n| Scene art | `paragraph:001-the-arrival:001-at-the-gate` | `assets/chapters/001-the-arrival/paragraphs/001-at-the-gate/primary.*` |\n\nCreate a portrait prompt for a character:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"create_asset_prompt\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"character:lyra-vale\",\n    \"body\": \"# Prompt\\n\\nPortrait of Lyra Vale, guarded expression, harbor fog, muted cinematic palette, portrait composition, 2:3 ratio.\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGenerate the image with OpenAI into the canonical asset path:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"generate_asset_image\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"character:lyra-vale\",\n    \"provider\": \"openai\",\n    \"model\": \"gpt-image-1\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nImport an existing render as the book cover:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"register_asset\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"book\",\n    \"assetKind\": \"cover\",\n    \"sourceFilePath\": \"C:/renders/my-book-cover.png\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nGenerate chapter art directly:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tool\": \"generate_asset_image\",\n  \"arguments\": {\n    \"rootPath\": \"C:/books/my-book\",\n    \"subject\": \"chapter:001-the-arrival\",\n    \"prompt\": \"Lyra approaching Gray Harbor through cold fog, chapter-opening illustration, portrait orientation, dramatic negative space, consistent with the book's visual language.\",\n    \"provider\": \"openai\",\n    \"model\": \"gpt-image-1\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf you later rename canon with `rename_entity`, `rename_chapter`, or `rename_paragraph`, the MCP moves matching asset folders too.\n\n## OpenCode example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"default_agent\": \"build\",\n  \"agent\": {\n    \"build\": {\n      \"temperature\": 0.45,\n      \"top_p\": 1,\n      \"options\": {\n        \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\",\n        \"reasoningSummary\": \"detailed\",\n        \"textVerbosity\": \"high\",\n        \"include\": [\"reasoning.encrypted_content\", \"usage\"],\n        \"store\": true\n      }\n    },\n    \"plan\": {\n      \"temperature\": 0.2,\n      \"top_p\": 1,\n      \"options\": {\n        \"reasoningEffort\": \"high\",\n        \"reasoningSummary\": \"detailed\",\n        \"textVerbosity\": \"high\",\n        \"include\": [\"reasoning.encrypted_content\", \"usage\"],\n        \"store\": true\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"mcp\": {\n    \"narrarium\": {\n      \"type\": \"local\",\n      \"command\": [\"npx\", \"narrarium-mcp-server\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n";
const markdown28 = "# narrarium-sdk\n\nDedicated TypeScript SDK package for loading, editing, and pushing Narrarium repositories over GitHub or Azure DevOps.\n\n`narrarium` remains the lower-level base package. `narrarium-sdk` re-exports the remote SDK surface on top of it so npm consumers can install a focused package.\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install narrarium-sdk\n```\n\n## Includes\n\n- `BookManager`\n- `GitHubRemoteProvider`\n- `AzureDevOpsRemoteProvider`\n- `NarrariumApiClient` for calling the server endpoints exposed by `Narrarium.Sdk.AspNetCore`\n- profile stores for memory and localStorage\n- in-memory book snapshots and workspaces\n- high-level workspace helpers for chapters, paragraphs, and characters\n- markdown snapshot builders and serializers for Narrarium documents\n\nHTTP note helpers exposed by `NarrariumApiClient` include:\n\n- `updateBookNotes(...)`\n- `updateStoryDesign(...)`\n- `updateChapterNotes(...)`\n\nStructured work-item helpers include:\n\n- `saveBookItem(...)`\n- `saveChapterItem(...)`\n- `promoteBookItem(...)`\n- `promoteChapterItem(...)`\n\n## Quick example\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  BookManager,\n  GitHubRemoteProvider,\n  LocalStorageBookProfileStore,\n} from \"narrarium-sdk\";\n\nconst manager = new BookManager({\n  profileStore: new LocalStorageBookProfileStore(),\n  providers: [new GitHubRemoteProvider()],\n});\n\nconst profile = await manager.createGitHubProfile({\n  name: \"Primary Book\",\n  owner: \"your-org\",\n  repository: \"your-book\",\n  branch: \"main\",\n  token: \"github-token\",\n});\n\nconst snapshot = await manager.loadBook(profile);\nconst workspace = manager.beginWorkspace(snapshot);\nworkspace.upsertCharacter({\n  slug: \"lyra-vale\",\n  frontmatter: {\n    type: \"character\",\n    id: \"character:lyra-vale\",\n    name: \"Lyra Vale\",\n    canon: \"draft\",\n    tags: [],\n    refs: [],\n    sources: [],\n    historical: false,\n    secret_refs: [],\n    aliases: [],\n    former_names: [],\n    identity_shifts: [],\n    role_tier: \"supporting\",\n    story_role: \"other\",\n    traits: [],\n    mannerisms: [],\n    desires: [],\n    fears: [],\n    relationships: [],\n    factions: [],\n    timeline_ages: {},\n  },\n  body: \"# Overview\\n\\nA careful broker.\",\n});\n\nawait manager.commitAndPush(profile, snapshot, workspace, {\n  message: \"Add Lyra character profile\",\n});\n```\n\n## Relationship with `narrarium`\n\n- `narrarium`: base package with schemas, repo helpers, and shared SDK internals\n- `narrarium-sdk`: focused remote SDK package for npm consumers\n";
const markdown29 = "# narrarium-server\n\nPersonal Narrarium server. It runs the **autopilot** and the **director** of your books on your own computer, even with the browser closed, and keeps them in sync with the [Narrarium web app](https://narrarium.net/app). It bundles [`narrarium-engine`](../engine/README.md), the same engine the web app runs in the browser, and the same AI client (`createFetchAi`): the same configuration gives the same requests and the same outcomes in both. What is the owner's alone stays the owner's: where the API keys may go ([AI endpoints](#ai-endpoints-and-spending-limits)) and how much the server may spend.\n\n## Start\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-server\n```\n\nRequirements: Node.js 22.14 or newer with npm, on Linux, macOS or Windows. Install `git` to host books stored on GitHub; without it the server hosts local books only.\n\nThe first start prepares a **verified copy** of the server in its data folder: npm must report a registry signature for it, and every release after the first one must also carry the provenance attestation of this repository's publishing workflow, at the tag of that release (see [Updates](#updates)). Then it starts two listeners, both on this computer only:\n\n| | Address | Who uses it |\n|---|---|---|\n| API | `http://127.0.0.1:24242` | Narrarium, with a client token |\n| Admin panel | `http://127.0.0.1:24243` | You, with the admin code |\n\nShow the admin code with `npx narrarium-server admin-code` and open the admin panel in a browser on the same computer.\n\n## Connect Narrarium\n\n1. In the admin panel choose **Generate a pairing code** (valid five minutes, usable by one device once).\n2. In Narrarium open **Settings → Personal server**, keep the address `http://127.0.0.1:24242` (or type the server's public address), type the nine digits and choose **Pair**.\n3. Back in the admin panel, read the request: the name the device gave itself, **where it comes from** (its address as the rate limits count it, or \"this computer\"), its user agent, the time, and a four-digit **code**. Approve it only if it is your device. Tick **allow updates** if this device may also update the server.\n\nWhoever types the nine digits first gets the request, so the panel shows who that was; if your own device is told that the code is \"wrong, expired or already used\", somebody else used it: **Reject** the request. Every client in the **Clients** table shows the same facts (source, user agent, creation time, last use) and a **Revoke** button that works at once, without a confirmation. The four-digit code of a client is derived from its token (`sha256` of the token, in hexadecimal, hashed again with a fixed prefix: see \"API\"), so a device can show what the panel shows and you can check that the device in front of you is the one that holds the token.\n\nNarrarium checks the server's identity before sending anything: the server signs a fresh random value with its Ed25519 key, and Narrarium remembers the key's fingerprint. A server answering with another key is refused until you pair again. The fingerprint is shown in the admin panel and in Narrarium's settings.\n\nThen, with your consent, send your AI router configuration from the same settings page, approve the AI endpoints it needs in the admin panel (**AI endpoints**: see [below](#ai-endpoints-and-spending-limits)), optionally set a spending limit (**AI spending**), and open a book's **Autopilot** page to choose **Move to my server**.\n\nYou can also create a token without pairing: in the admin panel (**Clients**) or on the server's computer with `npx narrarium-server client-token`, then paste it in Narrarium.\n\n### Permissions\n\n| Permission | Allows |\n|---|---|\n| `read` | Listing books, reading files, the live stream, the update check |\n| `write` | Registering books, writing files, sending the AI configuration |\n| `run` | Starting, pausing and steering the autopilot (the limits of a run, a pause at the end of the chapter, the settings that can change during a run), asking the director |\n| `update` | Replacing the server with a newer verified version (granted only when you tick it) |\n\nRevoking or rotating a token in the admin panel closes its live connection at once. A device that disconnects itself (`DELETE /api/v1/session`, with a token of any permission) revokes the token it presented and no other client's; a token that was replaced a moment ago cannot revoke its replacement.\n\n## AI endpoints and spending limits\n\nWhoever holds a token with the `run` permission can start runs that spend **your** AI credits, and whoever holds one with `write` chooses where the configuration sends your API keys. Both are decided by you here, whatever a client asks for.\n\n### Approving AI endpoints\n\nThe server sends an API key only to an endpoint you approved. An *endpoint* is the **base** every request of an integration starts with: its `endpoint`, or the provider's default when it has none (`https://api.openai.com/v1` for OpenAI, `https://models.github.ai/inference` for GitHub Models). Every integration of a configuration sent by a client, the providers' defaults included, waits in the admin panel (**AI endpoints**) and in `GET /api/v1/ai` (`pendingApproval`) until you approve it. After an update from an earlier version, what the saved configuration needs is listed at the first start.\n\n- An approval is an origin (`https://api.openai.com`) or an origin and a path (`https://my-resource.openai.azure.com/openai/v1`), compared as `new URL` writes them: host in lower case, no trailing slash, no query, default ports dropped. **An origin covers every base on it** (this is what every version up to 0.1.3 saved: nobody who approved before has to approve again); an entry with a path covers that base and what is under it, by whole segments (`/v1` covers `/v1/x`, not `/v10`). The **Approve** button approves exactly the base it shows.\n- Before **every** call the server checks every endpoint the call can reach: the primary of its route and the fallbacks it may try (a fallback in another account that the privacy settings keep out, `sameBoundaryOnly`, or ask about, `requireAcknowledgement`, is never called by this server, which cannot answer that question: it needs no approval either). If one is not approved **nothing is sent**: the autopilot stops (status `error`, and Resume works once you approved) with a message that names the base, and a director question is answered `412 ai_not_approved`. It is not retried, and it is not a network error.\n- The guard around outgoing requests (see [Security](#security)) is unchanged and decides by origin; the check above is what makes the owner's decision exact.\n- GitHub Models is called at `https://models.github.ai` directly: unlike a browser, the server has no CORS restriction, so the web app's optional proxy setting (`githubModelsBaseUrl`) does not apply and is not passed to the AI client.\n\n### Spending limits and the switch\n\nThe server counts everything it spends on AI and refuses a call that your limits do not allow. The settings live in `config.json` (field `spend`, absent when nothing is set) and are changed **only** by you, in the admin panel (**AI spending**) or on the server's computer with `npx narrarium-server spend`; no client token, whatever its permissions, has a call for it (the API only shows them).\n\n```json\n\"spend\": { \"paused\": false, \"maxTokensPerDay\": 0, \"maxCostPerDay\": 0, \"maxTokensPerRun\": 0, \"maxCostPerRun\": 0 }\n```\n\n| Setting | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `paused` | The switch. While it is set **no AI call is made** (the default is not set: spending is allowed). |\n| `maxTokensPerDay`, `maxTokensPerRun` | Whole numbers of tokens (input and output); `0` is no limit. At most 10¹². |\n| `maxCostPerDay`, `maxCostPerRun` | Amounts in the currency of the AI configuration; `0` is no limit. At most 1 000 000. They count only the costs that are **known** (a model with a price, whose usage the provider reported; a cost worked out from estimated tokens counts too, and is said so): a model without a price has no cost to cap, the token limits cover every model. |\n\n- **Counting.** `private/spend.json` (written atomically, 0600, a few kilobytes) holds the totals of each UTC day (the last 31 are kept) and of the latest run of each book. It is fed by the AI client's `onAttemptEnd`, so **every attempt counts**, the failed ones that used tokens included, and tokens the provider did not report count as the engine's estimate; an attempt that used nothing is only a call. It holds numbers and ids only: never a key, an address, a prompt, a reply or an error. It is read again at start, so a restart does not give the day's allowance back.\n- **A damaged record never stops the server.** If `spend.json` cannot be read it starts again from zero, keeps the unreadable file as `spend.json.damaged`, logs a line starting with `Spend`, and shows a warning in the panel.\n- **Enforcement.** Before every AI call (and before starting a run, so that a run that would stop at its first call leaves no error in the book) the server checks the switch, then the day's limits, then the run's. A limit is reached when the total has got to it: the call that crosses it is the last one, and nothing starts after it (calls already in flight finish). A run is one start of the autopilot of a book, from Start or Resume until it ends: its totals begin from zero at every start, and count the AI calls of that book while it runs (a director question asked on that book meanwhile included); a director question asked otherwise counts in the day only.\n- **What the author sees.** The run stops with status `error` and a message that says which limit, what was used and who can change it (`Server spending cap reached: 450 tokens were used today (UTC) and the owner's cap is 400 a day…`). It is never retried. It resumes with **Resume** after you raise the limit or, for a day's limit, after midnight UTC. Starting or asking while the switch is off or a day's limit is reached is answered `429 spend_limit` with the same message.\n- **The switch is a kill switch.** Turning it off in the panel (or `spend --pause`) pauses every autopilot at once and abandons the call in flight (they are paused, not failed), and a director question in flight is abandoned too and answered `429 spend_limit`.\n- **What a client reads.** `GET /api/v1/ai` (and the answer of `PUT /api/v1/ai`) carries, next to the redacted configuration, `spend` (always present, for every token with `read`):\n\n```json\n\"spend\": {\n  \"paused\": false,\n  \"today\": { \"day\": \"2026-11-22\", \"tokens\": 12345, \"calls\": 31, \"cost\": 0.42, \"currency\": \"USD\", \"costUnknownCalls\": 2 },\n  \"limits\": { \"maxTokensPerDay\": 0, \"maxCostPerDay\": 0, \"maxTokensPerRun\": 0, \"maxCostPerRun\": 0 }\n}\n```\n\n`today.cost` and `today.currency` appear when a cost of the day is known; `costUnknownCalls` when some calls used tokens at an unknown or estimated cost. The panel (`GET`/`POST /admin/api/spend`, behind the admin session, the CSRF token and the panel's origin like every other action there) shows the same, plus the warning above.\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-server spend                                   # today's use, the limits, the switch\nnpx narrarium-server spend --pause                           # no AI call until --resume\nnpx narrarium-server spend --max-tokens-per-day 200000 --max-cost-per-run 2.5\n```\n\nGoing back to a version older than 0.1.4 (see [Going back](#going-back)) forgets these settings: those versions know nothing about them and drop the field from `config.json` the next time they write it, so they spend without limits. Set them again after returning to 0.1.4 or later.\n\n## Updates\n\nWhen the server runs under the supervisor (`npx narrarium-server start`, the default), a newer release can be installed without touching the server's computer:\n\n- from Narrarium, **Settings → Personal server → Version and updates**, with a token that has the `update` permission;\n- from the admin panel, **Updates**.\n\nThe server accepts an update only when no autopilot is running and from then on refuses new work. The supervisor stops it cleanly, installs the exact version with `npm install --ignore-scripts`, runs `npm audit signatures`, compares the files with any copy of the same version it already has, and restarts the server on the new version. If anything fails, the previous version starts again and the reason is shown in Narrarium and in the admin panel. At most three updates per hour are performed.\n\nLocally, `npx narrarium-server update [--to VERSION]` installs a version that the next start will use. It never installs a version older than the highest one this data folder has run (see [Going back](#going-back)).\n\n### How an update is verified\n\nA release is trusted only when this repository's publishing workflow built it, and that is read from the **attestation itself**, not from what the package's metadata says: whoever controls the npm package controls its metadata, and could register any GitHub repository as its trusted publisher and obtain perfectly valid provenance for it. The same checks decide what the server **offers** (without downloading anything) and what the supervisor or `update` **installs**, in this order:\n\n1. **The metadata** (`npm view`): the package is `narrarium-server`, the version is the one asked for, the repository is `KeyserDSoze/GhostWriterFramework` (directory `packages/server`), the registry signatures are there, `dist.integrity` is one sha512 value, and the metadata points at an SLSA provenance attestation of that package and version. This is only a first filter.\n2. **Not a rollback**: the version is not older than the highest one this data folder has run. For an older version nothing else happens: no download, and nothing is asked of npm beyond what `latest` is.\n3. **Who signed** (read from `https://registry.npmjs.org/-/npm/v1/attestations/narrarium-server@<version>`, a small JSON document): exactly one SLSA provenance v1, whose signing certificate\n   - has as its only subject alternative name `https://github.com/KeyserDSoze/GhostWriterFramework/.github/workflows/publish-server-npm.yml@refs/tags/narrarium-server-v<version>` (this workflow, at the tag of this very version),\n   - was issued to GitHub Actions (OIDC issuer `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`),\n   - is for GitHub repository id `1176286350` and account id `40713438` (numbers nobody can take over by registering the same names);\n\n   and whose signed statement is about `pkg:npm/narrarium-server@<version>` and the sha512 of this tarball (the integrity npm announces; at install, the one npm recorded), and names that same repository, workflow file and tag. A release that fails is **not offered** (the update panel says that it is not trusted, the API's `message` says why, and the server log has a line starting `Update not offered`) and is never installed.\n4. **Installing** (not when offering): `npm install --ignore-scripts` in a staging folder, with npm's own isolated configuration; the integrity npm recorded in the staging `package-lock.json` must be the one announced in step 1, so nothing can be swapped between two reads of the registry; `npm audit signatures` verifies the registry signature, and that a validly signed provenance bundle is about the installed tarball; then step 3 is repeated against what was really installed.\n5. **Activating**: the installed files are compared with any copy of the same version already there, and the version is activated atomically.\n\nThe hand-published `0.1.0` has no attestation: it needs the registry signature only, and is accepted only as the copy prepared by `setup` or the first start.\n\n### Going back\n\nThe data folder remembers the highest version it ever activated (`private/npm-highest.json`; for a folder from before that file, the version it runs). Nothing older is ever **offered, installed or activated** by an update: not what npm calls `latest`, not `update --to`, not an update request file, not the version of the CLI at start. Without this, whoever takes over the npm account could point `latest` at an older, vulnerable release that still carries perfectly valid provenance. A version equal to the highest, or newer, is fine.\n\nTo go back on purpose after a bad release, use a copy that is already installed on this computer:\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-server rollback --to 0.1.1      # nothing is downloaded; a running server keeps its version until restarted\nnpx narrarium-server status                    # active runtime and highest version\n```\n\nThe record is not lowered by a rollback, so updates stay as strict as before. A newer CLI installs its own version when it starts, so until a fixed release exists start the server with the version you went back to: `npx narrarium-server@0.1.1 start`. If `private/npm-highest.json` is damaged, installs stop with a message; delete the file and it is rebuilt from the active runtime.\n\n### What this does not protect against\n\n- **The very first `npx narrarium-server`** executes whatever npm serves under that name, before any of the checks above can run. Pin the version you mean (`npx narrarium-server@0.1.2`), and check it first: the package page on [npmjs.com](https://www.npmjs.com/package/narrarium-server) shows its provenance (repository `KeyserDSoze/GhostWriterFramework`, workflow `publish-server-npm.yml`, the tag and commit), and in a scratch folder `npm install --ignore-scripts narrarium-server@0.1.2 && npm audit signatures` verifies the signatures. From then on the server only moves to releases that pass the checks above.\n- **Sigstore is verified by npm, not by this server.** `npm audit signatures` checks the signature, the certificate chain and the transparency log, and that the provenance is about the installed tarball; it does not tell who signed. The server reads the bundle to see who signed (the certificate's name, issuer and ids, and the signed statement's claims) but does not re-verify the signature itself, so it relies on the registry serving the same bundle to npm and to it.\n- **GitHub and npm are the roots of trust.** Whoever can push a tag `narrarium-server-v*` to the repository, or change the workflow on such a tag, publishes a release that passes. Protecting that is the maintainer's job: see [Maintainers](#maintainers-releasing).\n- **A proxy that only npm is configured for.** The registry is read by npm through `HTTPS_PROXY`; the attestation is read by the server with a direct HTTPS request, so behind a proxy that is the only way out, updates are not offered and `update` fails with a message that says the attestation could not be read.\n\n## Reach the server from other devices (Cloudflare)\n\nBoth listeners stay on `127.0.0.1`. To use the server from another computer or from your phone, publish it over HTTPS:\n\n- **Existing tunnel**: route a hostname of your tunnel to `http://127.0.0.1:24242`, then in the admin panel (**Approved network**) set it as the public API address.\n- **New tunnel**: in the Cloudflare dashboard create a remotely managed tunnel and route a hostname to `http://127.0.0.1:24242`, install `cloudflared` 2025.4.0 or newer, then paste the connector token in the admin panel (**Cloudflare → Save and start**). The server runs its own connector (`cloudflared tunnel --no-autoupdate run`), never touches other connectors and does not restart a connector that stopped.\n\nThe connector token is kept **encrypted by the vault** (`private/cloudflare-tunnel-token.json`), never in clear text, never on a command line and never in a log: `cloudflared` receives it in its environment. A clear-text token file written by an earlier version is moved into the vault the first time the server starts, and deleted. The connector ends with the server: a clean stop (Ctrl+C, SIGTERM, a closed terminal) stops it, so does any exit of the server process, and a connector that outlived a server that was killed outright is recognised at the next start by its program and its start time (not by its process number alone, which may belong to something else by then) and stopped before a new one starts.\n\nTo manage the server remotely as well, route a second hostname (or the same one) to `http://127.0.0.1:24243`, set it as the public admin address **and turn on \"Serve this panel on its public address\"**: a new installation serves the panel on this computer only until you do (an installation that was updated from an earlier version keeps what it had). On that address the panel is served and `/api/v1/*` is passed to the API, which still checks the client token. Protect remote administration with Cloudflare Access if you can.\n\nNarrarium on `https://narrarium.net` can always call the server; add other web origins under **Approved network**.\n\nSet the public API address (**Approved network**) even when you only use the tunnel: besides the `Host` check, the server uses it to believe Cloudflare's `cf-connecting-ip` header, so that every limit below is counted for the real caller. Without a public address (and without the server's own connector) all tunnel traffic would count as one caller.\n\n## Start automatically\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-server setup --autostart\n```\n\nOn Linux this installs a systemd user service (`~/.config/systemd/user/narrarium-server.service`; run `sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER` to start it before you log in). On macOS it installs a launch agent (`~/Library/LaunchAgents/net.narrarium.server.plist`, logging to `server.log` in the data folder). On Windows, add `npx narrarium-server start` to Task Scheduler at logon. The service runs the supervisor of the version installed by `setup`; the server it starts is always the active version, so run `setup --autostart` again after updates if you want the supervisor itself to move to the newer version.\n\nThe service starts with the `PATH` of the shell that ran `setup` (absolute entries only; so npm, git and cloudflared installed through nvm or Homebrew are found) and is restarted after a crash at most ten times in five minutes: after that systemd leaves it stopped, and `systemctl --user reset-failed narrarium-server` lets it start again. Run `setup --autostart` again to refresh an older unit.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpx narrarium-server [start]            # run the server under the update supervisor (default)\nnpx narrarium-server setup [--autostart]\nnpx narrarium-server status             # data folder, active version, whether it is running\nnpx narrarium-server doctor             # Node.js, npm, git, lock tools, cloudflared, data folder\nnpx narrarium-server admin-code         # show the admin code\nnpx narrarium-server rotate-admin-code  # replace it with a random one (server running)\nnpx narrarium-server set-admin-code [--code-file FILE]   # choose it (typed, or from a 0600 file)\nnpx narrarium-server client-token [--label NAME] [--scope read,write,run[,update]]\nnpx narrarium-server spend [--pause | --resume] [--max-tokens-per-day N] [--max-cost-per-day N] [--max-tokens-per-run N] [--max-cost-per-run N]\nnpx narrarium-server update [--to VERSION]   # never a version older than the highest ever installed\nnpx narrarium-server rollback --to VERSION   # reactivate an installed older copy (nothing is downloaded)\nnpx narrarium-server --version\n```\n\n| Option | Commands | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--data-root DIR` | all | Data folder (see below). |\n| `--port N` | `start`, `setup` | API port (default `24242`, saved in `config.json`). |\n| `--admin-port N` | `start`, `setup` | Admin panel port (default `24243`, saved). |\n| `--label NAME` | `start`, `setup`, `client-token` | Server name shown in Narrarium, or the new client's name. |\n| `--to VERSION` | `update`, `rollback` | The exact version to install, or to go back to (`rollback` needs it). |\n| `--origin URL` | `start` | Allow another web origin (saved; repeatable). |\n| `--direct` | `start` | Run without the supervisor: everything works except remote updates. |\n| `--verbose` | `start` | Log every request. |\n| `--pause`, `--resume`, `--max-tokens-per-day N`, `--max-cost-per-day N`, `--max-tokens-per-run N`, `--max-cost-per-run N` | `spend` | The switch and the limits (`0` is no limit); without any of them the command only shows them. |\n\nThe admin code is never accepted on the command line. Rotating it ends every admin session; client tokens keep working. Editing `private/admin-code.txt` by hand and restarting the server also replaces it.\n\n`client-token`, `rotate-admin-code`, `set-admin-code` and `spend` talk to the running server on its admin port, and **never send the admin code to a program that cannot prove it is that server**: before anything secret is sent they ask the listener to sign a fresh random value (`GET /admin/api/identity?nonce=HEX64`) and check the signature with the public key in the data folder (`private/identity.json`). A program that took the port while the server was down, another server with another data folder, and an answer recorded earlier are all refused with an explanation (\"The admin code was NOT sent\"). A server older than this version has no identity check: the commands refuse it too, so restart it first (or use the admin panel in a browser). The commands sign out of the admin panel when they are done.\n\n## Data folder\n\nDefault location, unless `--data-root` or `NARRARIUM_SERVER_HOME` says otherwise:\n\n| System | Folder |\n|---|---|\n| Linux | `$XDG_DATA_HOME/narrarium-server`, or `~/.local/share/narrarium-server` |\n| macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/Narrarium Server` |\n| Windows | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Narrarium\\server` (a data folder that earlier versions made under `%APPDATA%\\Narrarium\\server`, the roaming profile, keeps being used; nothing is moved) |\n\n```text\nconfig.json              ports, label, origins, approved network, spending limits (no secrets)\nbooks.json               hosted books\nbooks/<id>/repo/         working copy of each book: every autopilot step is a git commit\nbooks/.trash/           removed or replaced working copies, deleted for good after 14 days\nprivate/                 0700\n  admin-code.txt         admin code (0600)\n  authorization.json     client tokens as SHA-256 hashes, pending pairing\n  master.key             vault key (0600; protected by DPAPI on Windows)\n  master.key.bak         a copy of it, made once and never overwritten (see below)\n  identity.json          the server's Ed25519 key, its private half encrypted\n  ai.json                AI router configuration, encrypted\n  spend.json             what the server spent on AI per UTC day and per run (numbers only)\n  book-tokens.json       per-book GitHub tokens, encrypted\n  cloudflare-tunnel-token.json   the Cloudflare connector token, encrypted\n  cloudflare-tunnel.json, cloudflared-process.json, cloudflared-home/   connector settings, and the connector that runs (its process number and start time)\n  npm-runtime/           verified versions and the active one\n  npm-install.json       the installer's state: the active version\n  npm-highest.json       the highest version ever activated: nothing older is installed from npm\n  git-home/              the empty home git runs in (never your own ~/.gitconfig)\n  npm-home/              the empty home, configuration and cache npm runs with (never your ~/.npmrc)\n  update-request.json, update-result.json\n```\n\nOne server per data folder: a kernel lock on Linux (`flock`), a named mutex on Windows, a lock file (`server.lock`) on macOS. The lock file names the process that holds it and when that process started, so a number that was given to another program after a reboot or a crash is not taken for a server; it is created whole, taken over from a dead holder by one server at a time, and its holder checks every few seconds that it is still its own. **A server that loses its lock** (the lock helper was killed, the lock file was deleted or replaced) stops serving at once, pauses its autopilots, closes, and exits with an error, so that a supervisor or service manager starts it again instead of two servers writing in one folder.\n\n**The vault key** is never created again by accident: if `private/master.key` is missing while encrypted files (`identity.json`, `ai.json`, `book-tokens.json`, `cloudflare-tunnel-token.json`) exist, the server refuses to start and says so, because a new key would make them unreadable for good. Copy `private/master.key.bak` over `master.key` (or restore it from your own backup) and start again; to start with no secrets instead, move the encrypted files away first (devices must then pair again, and the AI configuration and GitHub tokens must be sent again).\n\n## Security\n\n- Both listeners bind to `127.0.0.1`; requests with an unexpected `Host` header are refused (DNS-rebinding protection). Private Network Access preflights are answered for allowed origins.\n- Browser requests are accepted only from allowed origins. Every API call needs a client token with the right permission.\n- The admin panel needs the admin code, an `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Strict` cookie and a CSRF token; it is served under a strict Content-Security-Policy.\n- AI keys, GitHub tokens and the identity key are encrypted with AES-256-GCM; client tokens are stored only as hashes and never returned.\n- The server calls only the AI endpoints you approved (by base: see [AI endpoints and spending limits](#ai-endpoints-and-spending-limits)), GitHub and the npm registry: DNS is resolved once per connection, private addresses are refused (except an approved local model on `127.0.0.1`) and redirects are never followed.\n- Updates are verified end to end, from the attestation and not from the package's metadata, and never go back to an older version: see [How an update is verified](#how-an-update-is-verified) and [Going back](#going-back), and what they do not cover.\n- npm runs without install scripts and without your npm credentials: every run gets empty user and global configuration files, a private cache and home and an empty working folder, so your `~/.npmrc` (tokens, registry overrides) is never read. A proxy you configured only there stops applying: use `HTTPS_PROXY`, which is still passed, and `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`.\n- A book's working copy is not trusted (a cloned repository can contain anything): no operation follows a symbolic link, git runs with `core.symlinks=false`, a private home, no hooks, no credential helpers and no URL rewrites from your own configuration, the names of git's own folder are refused in every spelling (`.GIT`, `GIT~1`, trailing dots, look-alike Unicode) together with Windows device names, and only text files (`.md`, `.markdown`, `.txt`, `.json`, `.yaml`, `.yml`) can be written. A commit is validated before anything is written, and a failed one puts every file back.\n- Admin sessions are bounded without ever ending everybody's: at most ten per address and fifty in all, the least recently used one goes first, and an expired one goes before any live one (a new admin code, on purpose, ends all of them).\n- The panel is reachable on its public address only when you turn that on (new installations); the server's name in `GET /info` is neutral (an earlier default `narrarium-server@computer` is shown as `narrarium-server` to everybody but you).\n- Book files are flushed to disk before they are put in place, and the folders that gained or lost files after that, so a power cut cannot leave a half-done commit; one thing at a time happens in a book's working copy (commits, pulls and pushes of the autopilot and of `POST /sync` take turns); the books' revisions are not asked of git on every listing; and registering a book is all or nothing (a failed first clone, or a failure to store the token or the entry, leaves no registration, token or folder behind).\n- Book ids that a Windows computer cannot keep (`con`, `nul`, `aux`, `com1`…, with or without an extension; names that end or start with a dot) are refused for new books; books that already have such an id keep working.\n- The guard around outgoing requests honours what the caller asked for (a `Request` object's method, headers and body; `redirect: \"manual\"` returns the 3xx answer, nothing is ever followed), lets no caller set `Host` or the framing headers, and offers every verified address of a host to the connection (a local model on `localhost` may listen on `127.0.0.1` or `::1`).\n- Behind a proxy: when `HTTPS_PROXY` is set in the server's environment (the variable git and npm already receive; an `http://` address, or a bare `host:port`, with optional `user:password@`; `NO_PROXY` applies), the server's own requests to AI endpoints and the npm registry go through it with `CONNECT`. TLS is spoken with the destination itself inside the tunnel, so its certificate is verified exactly as without a proxy (a proxy that inspects TLS needs its authority in `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`) and the proxy sees only encrypted bytes; the destination must still be approved and a private address is still refused, but a name that this computer cannot resolve within three seconds is left to the proxy, which resolves it and makes the connection, so the address it uses cannot be checked here. An approved local model is never sent through a proxy, and a proxy variable that is not an `http://` address (`socks5://`, `https://`) is ignored by these requests, as before. A proxy that refuses or is not there is one clear error (`Could not connect to <origin> through the proxy named by HTTPS_PROXY (the proxy answered 403)`) that never repeats the proxy's address or credentials.\n- Every limit is counted per caller, never for everybody at once, so an anonymous caller cannot lock you out: 10 wrong admin codes per five minutes per address (a correct code clears the count), 10 pairing requests per five minutes per address (and 5 wrong codes per address, 50 in all, inside one pairing), 120 `info` requests a minute per address, 60 failed authentications a minute per address, 16 anonymous requests in flight per address, 6000 authenticated requests a minute per client token, and 10 live connections per client token (50 in all). A request body that goes quiet for 20 seconds is dropped, a whole request must arrive within five minutes, and at most two large uploads are read at once. Refused requests answer 429 with `Retry-After` (or 408, 503).\n- A server error that is not understood answers `500 internal` with a short `requestId` (also in the `x-request-id` header) and the details only in the log.\n- Removing a book, or moving it to another storage, never deletes its working copy: it goes to `books/.trash` for 14 days, and a failed move leaves the old copy untouched.\n\n## How books travel\n\n- **GitHub books**: the server clones the same branch, commits every autopilot step and pushes it (by default: the author can choose to publish only when a chapter is closed, or never: see [Quick controls and publishing](#quick-controls-and-publishing)); before each step it pulls the author's work. Narrarium pulls the server's commits through its usual repository sync. The book's GitHub token is sent to the server only with the author's explicit consent.\n- **Local books**: files are exchanged with a three-way sync (`syncBookStores` in the engine): changes on one side are copied to the other, and files changed on both sides are reported instead of overwritten.\n\n## Quick controls and publishing\n\nThe controls behind the web app's quick-command bar: pausing at the end of the chapter (and taking a pause back), the limits of one run, and the settings that can change while a run goes on, one of which is where a GitHub book is published. The engine does the work (see *Quick controls* in the [engine's README](../engine/README.md)); the server adds the routes and the push.\n\nA server that has them announces `\"autopilot-controls\"` in the `features` of `GET /api/v1/info`. An older server does not, and would ignore `limits` and read every pause mode but `now` as `after-step` (a `cancel` would pause), so a client checks the feature before it sends them (`serverSupportsAutopilotControls(info)` in the engine).\n\nAll three calls need the `run` permission, answer the snapshot of the autopilot (what `GET /books/:id/autopilot` answers: its `summary` has `pausePending`, `limits` and `publish`) and, when they refuse, answer `{ \"code\", \"message\" }`. Every one also answers 401, 403 `scope` (no `run` permission) and 404 `book_not_found`.\n\n| Call | Body | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| `POST /books/:id/autopilot/pause` | `{ \"mode\": \"now\" \\| \"after-step\" \\| \"after-chapter\" \\| \"cancel\" }`. No `mode` is `after-step`, as it always was. | `200`. `after-chapter` finishes the chapter in progress (its closing and its publishing included) and pauses; the stricter of two requests counts. `cancel` takes back a pause that waits and stops nothing; with nothing waiting, and any mode with nothing running, the answer is `200` too and nothing changes. A pause is not refused while an update is about to start. `400 invalid_request`: a `mode` that is none of the four, or a body that is not a JSON object. |\n| `POST /books/:id/autopilot/start` | The body it always had (`brief`, `options`, `force`) and `limits?: { \"steps\"?: n, \"chapters\"?: n }`: the run pauses after that many steps saved, or chapters completed. | `200`. `400 invalid_request` when `limits` is present and is not an object: it is checked before anything else, and nothing starts. The numbers go to the engine as they are (a value that is not a whole number above zero is no limit). Limits are for that run only: they are not saved, and the next start has none. The other answers are as before: `409 busy`, `412 ai_not_configured`, `429 too_many_runs`, `429 spend_limit`, `503 maintenance`. |\n| `PUT /books/:id/autopilot/options` | `{ \"publishEvery\"?: \"off\" \\| \"chapter\" \\| \"step\" \\| null, \"pauseAfterEachChapter\"?: boolean }`: at least one, and nothing else. `null` takes the choice back, so that the server's default (`step`) decides again. | `200`, also while the autopilot runs: the choice is saved in the book, the step in flight is not repeated, and the run reads the new value at the end of the step or of the chapter. A book with no autopilot state gets one (as `configure` does). `400 invalid_request` for any other key (nothing of the request is applied, not even the part that was right), a value of the wrong type, or a body that is not an object; `409 conflict` when the state file kept changing under the write (try again); `409 state_corrupt` when it cannot be read; `503 maintenance`. |\n\n`summary.pausePending` (`\"after-step\"`, `\"after-chapter\"` or `null`) and `summary.limits` (`{ steps?, chapters?, stepsDone, chaptersDone }` for the run in progress, `null` when it has none or is over) are never saved. They are in the snapshot, in the `state` events of the live stream, and in the `autopilot` summary that `GET /books` and the `book` events carry for every book; `summary.publish` is where the book is published now.\n\n### Publishing to GitHub\n\n`publishEvery` says when the server pushes a GitHub book. The author chooses it (and can change it while the run goes on); a book with no choice takes the server's default, `step`, which is what every server did before. The old option `syncAfterChapter` is the browser's and does not decide here.\n\n| `publishEvery` | The server pushes |\n|---|---|\n| `step` (default) | After every step, and once more when a chapter is closed (that second push has nothing new when the step that closed the chapter was just pushed). |\n| `chapter` | When a chapter is closed, and only then. |\n| `off` | Never: every commit stays in the book's working copy. |\n\n- The engine decides and the server only pushes. The choice is read at the end of every step and of every chapter, from the state that step saved, so a change takes hold at the end of the step that is running. A book that is not on GitHub has nowhere to push, whatever it says (its `summary.publish` is then only what it would be).\n- **`lastSync`** says how the last push went, whether it worked or not: `{ \"ok\": true, \"message\": \"Pushed to GitHub.\" }`, or `{ \"ok\": false, \"message\" }` with what git said (cut to 500 characters, with the token taken out). A push that fails is a warning (a `sync` event with `syncFailed`, or `publishFailed` for a chapter) and never stops the run: its commits wait for the next push that works, or for `POST /books/:id/sync`. With `off` no push is tried, and `lastSync` stays as it was.\n- **What waits on the server.** With `step`, only the commit that records how the run ended; with `chapter`, everything after the last chapter that was closed (the director's judgement of the whole book, how the run ended); with `off`, everything. `POST /books/:id/sync` publishes it all by hand. With `off`, GitHub does not move at all, so the web app's usual repository sync does not see what the autopilot wrote until a publish.\n- **The risk of `chapter` and `off`.** Before every step the server still pulls what the author published (a fetch, then a fast-forward, otherwise a rebase of its own commits under the author's), and with these two ways it has more unpublished commits for longer.\n  - A change made on GitHub to a file that the server also changed cannot be rebased under them (every step rewrites `autopilot/state.json` and `autopilot/activity.json`; chapters, `plot.md` and the resumes change as the book grows). The next step then fails and the run stops with status `error` and the message *The book changed on GitHub in a way that conflicts with the server's work. Resolve it from the web app, then resume.* `POST /books/:id/sync` answers `409 sync_failed` with the same words. A change to a file the server did not touch is rebased under its commits, and the run goes on.\n  - Nothing is lost: the rebase is always undone, the server's commits are all still in the working copy, and the working copy is clean.\n  - To go on, settle it on GitHub (undo or redo the change that conflicts, so that the server's commits rebase cleanly) and resume; or merge on the server, where the working copy is an ordinary git repository (`books/<id>/repo` in the data folder: `git pull --no-rebase`, settle the file, commit, `git push`); or give up the server's unpublished work by removing the book from the server (its working copy stays in `books/.trash` for 14 days) and registering it again from GitHub.\n  - To avoid it, keep `step` for a book that is also edited on GitHub or by another device, or publish with `POST /books/:id/sync` before anybody else edits it. With `step` the same conflict is possible too, but only in the moment between one push and the next.\n\n## API\n\nAll endpoints live under `/api/v1` and return JSON; errors are `{ \"code\", \"message\" }`.\n\n| Method | Path | Permission | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/info?nonce=HEX64` | none | Identity, version and `features` (among them `autopilot-controls`: see [Quick controls and publishing](#quick-controls-and-publishing)), signed over the nonce. |\n| `POST` | `/pairings`, `/pairings/:id/result` | none | Pair with a code (answers `{ id, verification }`); collect the token once approved (answers `{ token, verification }`). `verification` is optional: four digits that the owner sees in the panel too (below). |\n| `GET`/`DELETE` | `/session` | any | The calling client and its permissions / revoke the token it presented, and no other (disconnect; answers `{ revoked }`). |\n| `GET`/`PUT` | `/ai` | read / write | Redacted AI status (with the endpoints awaiting approval and the spending status) / replace the configuration. |\n| `GET` | `/books` | read | Hosted books with their autopilot summary. |\n| `PUT`/`DELETE` | `/books/:id` | write | Register (local or GitHub) / remove a book. |\n| `GET` | `/books/:id/manifest` | read | Paths and hashes of the book's text files. |\n| `POST` | `/books/:id/files/read`, `/books/:id/files/commit` | read / write | Read up to 100 files (16 MiB in all) / commit up to 5000 changes with compare-and-swap. |\n| `POST` | `/books/:id/sync` | write | Pull and push the GitHub branch. |\n| `GET` | `/books/:id/autopilot`, `/books/:id/autopilot/events?since=N` | read | Snapshot / recent live events. |\n| `POST` | `/books/:id/autopilot/{start,pause,intervene,answer}` | run | Control the autopilot (`answer` takes `{ questionId, answer, accept?, asRule? }`: with `asRule` the answer becomes a permanent rule; `start` takes `limits`, `pause` takes `after-chapter` and `cancel`: see [Quick controls and publishing](#quick-controls-and-publishing)). |\n| `PUT` | `/books/:id/autopilot/options` | run | Change `publishEvery` (where a GitHub book is pushed) and `pauseAfterEachChapter`, also while the autopilot runs. |\n| `POST` | `/books/:id/autopilot/{configure,reset}` | write | Change or reset its settings. |\n| `POST` | `/books/:id/director/ask` | run | Ask the director about the book, a chapter or a scene. |\n| `GET` | `/events` | read | Server-sent events: autopilot events and book summaries. |\n| `GET`/`POST` | `/updates` | read / update | Release check / request the verified update. |\n\nThe typed client is `NarrariumServerClient` in `narrarium-engine` (with `pairWithServer` and `verifyServerIdentity`).\n\n**Errors the engine and the owner's settings give.** Every refusal below keeps its message (never a `500 internal`):\n\n| Status | `code` | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| 400 | `rule_empty`, `rule_too_long`, `rule_duplicate` | A permanent rule (`from_now` or `rewrite` intervention, or an answer with `asRule`) that is empty, over the engine's 800 characters (never cut: the message gives the length that was sent), or already there. A note (`next_step`) may be longer; an empty one is `400 invalid_request`. |\n| 404, 409 | `rule_not_found`, `rule_changed` | A rule that is not there, or changed since the author saw it (the engine's `updateRule`/`removeRule`; this server's API has no call for them yet). |\n| 409 | `question_closed` | The director's question was answered already. |\n| 412 | `ai_not_approved` | The call would send an API key to an endpoint the owner has not approved. |\n| 422 | `frontmatter_invalid` | The engine refused to rewrite a file whose frontmatter cannot be read; the message names the file and the line. |\n| 429 | `spend_limit` | The owner's switch is off, or a day's limit is reached (starting a run, asking the director). |\n| 502 | `ai_provider` | The AI provider failed, or the engine refused to call it (a prompt over every model's context window, a routing budget, a fallback the privacy settings keep out): the engine's message, as it is. |\n\n**Verification codes.** `verification` is `String(sha256(\"narrarium-pairing-verification\\n\" + kind + \"\\n\" + hashHex).readUInt32BE(0) % 10000).padStart(4, \"0\")`, where `hashHex` is the lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 of a secret: for the pairing request (`kind` is `request`, shown in the panel while the request waits) the client's nonce, for the issued token (`kind` is `client`, shown next to the client in the panel) the token. A device can compute the same four digits from what it holds, or just display what the server answered.\n\nThe admin port also answers `GET /admin/api/identity?nonce=HEX64` (no session): `{ name, serverId, publicKey, fingerprint, nonce, signature }`, the same signature as `GET /info?nonce=` gives, for the local commands.\n\n## Maintainers: releasing\n\nReleases are published by [`.github/workflows/publish-server-npm.yml`](../../.github/workflows/publish-server-npm.yml) through npm Trusted Publishing, with a provenance attestation and without any npm token:\n\n1. Bump `version` in `packages/server/package.json` and `SERVER_VERSION` in `src/version.ts` (a test keeps them equal); `packages/engine` has the same pair (`ENGINE_VERSION`), commit and push.\n2. Push the tag: `git tag narrarium-server-v0.1.2 && git push origin narrarium-server-v0.1.2`.\n\nThe workflow checks that the tag matches the package, builds and tests the engine and the server, checks the package contents and publishes. The generic `Publish npm packages` workflow never publishes this package.\n\n### Protect the release path\n\nEvery installed server accepts exactly one identity (`NPM_RELEASE_IDENTITY` in `src/npm-release.ts`): the account and repository above, the workflow file `publish-server-npm.yml`, and a tag `narrarium-server-v<version>`. Keep that true, and protect who can reach it:\n\n- **Do not change the identity by accident.** Renaming the account, the repository or the workflow file, giving the workflow another trigger (`workflow_dispatch`, a branch), or recreating the repository, makes releases that every installed server refuses. Such a change needs a server release that carries the new identity, installed by hand (`npx narrarium-server@<version>`). Bump the publishing npm (`npm@12.0.2` in the workflow) only to versions that still write SLSA provenance v1.\n- **GitHub**: a ruleset on the tags `narrarium-server-v*` that restricts who can create them and blocks updating and deleting them (a published tag must never move); required reviewers on the `npm-publish` environment, with that environment limited to the tags `narrarium-server-v*`; no other workflow may publish this package.\n- **npm**: two-factor authentication on the maintainer account (also for publishing), the package's *Trusted publishing* set to this repository, workflow and the `npm-publish` environment and nothing else, and every old npm token revoked (publishing uses OIDC, no token is needed). After any change to the account, look at the package's trusted publishers again.\n- **After publishing**, open the package page and check that the provenance names this repository, the workflow and the tag. It is the check every installed server makes before it updates (`verifyNpmReleaseProvenance` in `src/npm-release.ts`), tested against the real registry data of `0.1.2` (`test/fixtures`): a release that does not pass it is never offered.\n\nFirst publication (once): npm can bind a Trusted Publisher only to an existing package, so `0.1.0` is published by hand from a clean, verified checkout:\n\n```bash\nnpm ci\nnpm run build -w narrarium && npm run build -w narrarium-engine\nnpm run test -w narrarium-server\nnpm login                      # with two-factor authentication\ncd packages/server\nnpm publish --access public --provenance=false\n```\n\nThen, on npmjs.com, open the package **Settings → Trusted publishing** and add GitHub Actions with owner `KeyserDSoze`, repository `GhostWriterFramework`, workflow `publish-server-npm.yml`, environment `npm-publish`. Optionally require two-factor authentication and disallow tokens for publishing.\n";

